        <head>Christ Church, Latchford. Bishop of Chester Preaches at</head>
        <p>Christ Church, Latchford. Bishop of Chester Preaches at</p>
        <p>Jubilee Celebrations.</p>
        <p>Sunday was a day a rea:ring at Christ Church. Latchfcret, spend art jar, hen g held in celebration f the jubilee if the (hutch which ens consecrated t y the late Bishop (Ira' am. if Chester, on July IGth, 18C1. The creation was full f interest for the parishioners, who take a great Fide in their beautiful huech,and the services wcre in (very resptet worthy of tl e importance of bett nniversary, lie altar sena decked with choice lilies a: ii in Ole s.anetual y sod chancel yalmsand oda r plants were tastefully arranged There were celeheot ior nit the Italy Communion at 7.30 and Sl5 am. Matins and Liar y were eurg at 11.45 when the ; rercher was the Lord Bishop of Chester (Illght Bev. Cr. Jsvnel. lb. chutch was well filled and the eery ice,whiCh was conducted by the Vicar (Rev. G. C. Priygel, was of an ap; repriately bright aid joyous chainctit. It began with the enaional hymn We love the Ikea of God." flit choir gave a finished air! ex; restive rlcdeling of the anthem " Blessed are they that dwell in Thy House" (Tours). The Te Itetun was sung to a setting by Winchts'er. Mr. T. L. Fry playod the organ with trove ard akill.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Cletter hatted a striking sermon on the words "One generation obeli plaint, Thy works onto another and dealer., Tby primer" (Fe•lnt 113, ivol llv directed the attention of his bearer, to the labours of bygone years, into which they hed entered, and pessed in review some of tie outstanding featurea of the het half century. First he alludtd to !balite of cm. country which was, of coorse,being kronen vi•lcly before our minds and hearte in this particular year of the Coronation. What did we find to prod.* God fore, we looked beck orlon fifty year. ? At the time of rhe late Gueen's Diamond Jubilee a epeciel than kagi•ing was composed and offered throughout our Church In which 'reference 008,1 made to tbr se points of progresa which bad, on the whole, been as clearly and bsppily marked I during the raced:rig half century end more. First of all I we had during that period been privileged to lenro so I much more of God., tA orb and particularly of Ills work in nature. We meat remember that (led was, as St. Paul nod other icspired writers taught, Oct only the Head of the moral sod spiritual creation, but also of the natural creation. We need never he afraid of anything that tame to its in the shape of new dircoveries, as long as they were patiently and carefully subjected to the test of truth. We should welcome them as more light upon our Father's Kingdom, more light upon those myateries which God intended to be revealed for the veell-heing and pros.. oil lie children. Doting the last fifty years and more we had been learning Immensely shout the secrets of God's Kingdom of nature, and !hose secrets —nal, for exempte what we had learnt bout electricity, steam and no forth—bad largely 1111110ilered 10 the comfort, sell-Leing and brightness of human life. Some of those diSCO•Ptiell Itt first sight</p>
        <p>•;:/P .N-1 Ell-TO BAFFLE AND I'DNAT 01"1</p>
        <p>with regerd to received traditional opitmoirs, lett the more we linil come to understand them and the more we had mme to tee, fir example, of the doctrinee of evolution and development, tie more we bad found that instead if uptetting our Christian faith, they bed enabled no to undenraud, think and feel more Lrosdly tod neme firmly w ith regard to n bet we had lesent shout God. We lad to pram. God as toe looked back upon the growth of education r nd of the I ...ledge of Goo'. uork And ill. Furthermore, Gd hod bright...l, equipped and comforted human life by great discoveriee, by bringing together the nations f man- kind during this period in tuch a way as to minister from the abundance of one clime and one nation tit the %titre of other cations. lhen there had surely been, on the sr hole, a</p>
        <p>111(WT11-01 EL9TFTt ROTATIONS</p>
        <p>between different clseser, between rich and poor. It might be very truly said that there was still a great</p>
        <p>I'l.oo</p>
        <p>[Bittles.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 11. A. Lester, MA., v'ice.p6nsipal of Warrington Training College and culate I f Sr. Thomas s Stockton Heath, has been ell:rioted by the Biahop of London as director of Sunday schools in the dime.° of London. This new and impottant pest is part of a forward movement on the part of the Church to bring the Sunday schools into line with modern educational methods end it is a great boat or to the Warrington Training College that its vice. president sheuld have been selected from candidates from all parts ,f the kingdom to take up this pioneer work. While congratulating Mr. Lester upon his sppointurent, the people of Warrington and Stockton Heath will regret his departure from their midst, for be has done a great deal for the advance- ment it local Church and cducational life. He has worked in Warriegtoo for over thirteen years and has by diligent and conscientious service earned the esteem and goodwill rot only of Churclapeople but of members of all denominations 1 As bon secretary of the Warrington Church Society he did much to increase the influence end usefulness of that body. It was doting WI term deities that the Yridayl.ent lectures for men and women were started f . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lester was educated at Bea. rd Modern Sehoclj ' anchnt the late Rev, Cam&amp; roele. 1' ga'ned the [trier Trurt hilitta to Lorough-rcad College where he was trained as teacher. After working for several years in 'doublet various types, including two years as science master at Maidstone Irammar school, he went to Ortord University, taking the B.A. degree with second clam honours in modern history in 1895 and MA in 1809. Ile then was appointed resident tutor of St. John's Training College, Batterees, and lecturer is school method, and ordained by the Bishop of Rochester in 1801 i. He came to War rlngton in 1808 ard has helped in various perishes ever since. From 1808 to 1900 he was Sunday momma rwacher at St. Nee. wwiagton ; from 191,2 to 1905 he was curate of Dareabury : and since 1905 he has been curate of Sr. Thomas's, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>'deal of attire—Ml.lkm locknu,s and misunderstandings. The path of progress was apt to be • very rugged one, and Iberia were slippings back as well as movements forward. But if they looked it the position oalmly ' and fairly, from all points, they would he constrained to acknowledge with thankfulness that there had been , a very aolid and grateful growth of better feelings between different classea and o/ more harmorriona relations within the body politic, they had also to rejoice in a very great increase in yam for the o:Lentiloo of the Kingdom of God. Viewing the wesithese an well as the strength of missionary effort—acknowledg- ing as we were bound to do, with deep humiliation that it was far from what might have been repented fm m chrittin wbesti WC d made ell deduotions, it Min quite plain that the faith or Christ had been Co Ili iog in loyal and heroic hearts that there had been a marvellous! exponsion. The more there was of unselfish teal for missionary work abroad, the more there would be of faith, love, hope and seal for the work which wee being done at home. There could not he. greater mistake than to suppose that those who were seal°ns for missionary work abroad were not also the most zealous and laborious foe the extension of the Kingdom of Christ at, that it might rule ore, the ! bodies, minds end spirits of all classes here at home. Another cause for thankfulness to God was that leg the Fast fifty years there had been among the Christian cations of the world a grret growth of the desire for peace and good g ill. There were still</p>
        <p>"ADS Ott lii 3101 :NS OF WAit end overpowering armaments had to be maintained, though they, of course, had their tatter side in the discipline and many other high quilities which they developed among our soldiers and sailors, and those who felt their responsibility as citizens. There were very dangerous end anxious eymptom a, but we had cense to thank OA and take courage wh,o we new England, America, and Japan making plans for settling differ- ences on terms of rquity. righteoushese, peace, and mutual consideratii,n. In that respect it might almost he said that Christian end other States had gone in front of the Christian Church Itself, and had set an exarople which he Loped they in the Church might have grace to follow. His lordship then alluded to C e progres. which had : been mnde in the parish of Christ Church since the work Breit began in the small infant school in 1814.1 The provision of tile present church in 1861 sena inn very real sense n pious work,the building being erected by the sin children of the late Thome. Greetall in grateful, dutiful and lovC g memory of their father. Having paid a trit.ute to two of the sons with whom he had had the privilege of n personal acquaintancB— late Revs. Thomas and Richard Greenell, former rectors of Grappenhall • the Bishop pointed out that during the hell century the population of the parish had increased from 401) to warty 4,000 and the church had been doubled in its acolmmodation. Having known the puish for twenty-two years he could beer witneee to the Irue pester.] spirit and fsithful labours of those who had been vim, I.f Christ Church. Alluding to the future, the Bishop said he had spoken e ord. of encouragement and good cheer but he did not forget that there mere :dark and einister signs and obstacle, to be overcome for which they would need the beet strength of Goon Holy Spirit. lie particularly urged the reed for, knitting together in chiller fellowship end the rolling away of the atones of aeperetion from between those who named the Name of Chriet.</p>
