        <head>Re/. E. C. E. Carleton and the Constitutional Struggle.</head>
        <p>Re/. E. C. E. Carleton and the Constitutional Struggle.</p>
        <p>In the course of a pamphlet entitled " Fair Piny for the Sovereign in the Coming Strum/le," the Nev. E. C. V.Carleton armies that Monarchy in the form of govern- ment under which the people's liberties can most emily be safeguard.' ant thows that the peonees of limiting the energies of the Sovereign'. power has been the gradual work of centimes. An historical Constitution hen followed, under which the people can claire their rights and li berths. en an inheritance secured to there by law, and ettleemly pledge.' by the Coronation Oath. It is ob•ictim, I think," continues the rev. gentleman, that what the present Government is angling ' for is &amp;Motet. power, and to that end ere quite ready to destroy all tie mows safeguards of the Constitution. One of the safeguard., it in true, is not yet openly attacked, although attempts urn being made to helitGe its power and authority. I refer to the Monarchy. A Monarchy atom of all nothority and prerogati•e, I soppose, they will put up with—in other sortie, a Sovereign without a veto, although apparently he in still to be morn to the old obligations as if he had a veto. Bat why adrninister an oath to a man who in to have no choice or my ea to how the oath ha takes shall be kept ? The truth is that the oath is to he kept up ae • make. believe to disgiiise from the people the fact that the prerogative of the Crown hen been taken over by Mr, Asquith and his colleagues. Moreover, in Is. Mr. A..tuith and bin friend, get into a aerate, tlme mantsbeliere of the oath may prone uneful in helping them to nbirk the maponsiltilities. Meanwhile, Mr. Atquith does not mind having • King provided brie a sham King, nor does he mind lumina a sham Coronation and a sham Coronation Outb. A sham Coronation Osth, however, Is blasphemy, and en Oath, certainly, thet the Archbishop of Canterbury could not administer to his Sovereign in the people's name, and an Oath that the king himself dare not take out of peril to hi, aoul,hearing in mind the solemn admonition t bet the men shall not he held guiltles3 who takes God's name in vain, awl an Oath alto that could not be taken without trestle's to his subject., inas- much as it implies a secret reservation tvith ' respect to the security of their liberties hitherto , tlolrOmn to the Cots tuition. Here, evidently, there are ei..:mtions rsined that are of. kind to compel the PbeLber be IIOr BO to resort 10th, Courts for legal guidance its justice Cr himself. lhe Oat of Mr. Asquith, ' the King'e veto In dead,' pronounced without any anthorim for It, !eget or otherwise, being alleged, though, no doubt, an Warming pronounce- ment, is not • legal decinion, her is it one that the King mould be jaatilled In relying upon rather than upon his Oath which the law has premiribed. It is to he hoped that in this struggle the House of Louie will fully rialige the nature of be struggle In which the Government baa involved the country. It is not their interests no much as the intereste of the conntry that are at stake in the person ' of the Sovereign. A Conetitatioall Sovereign means a: free people—a people effectually safeguarded from the tyranny of arbitrary government. And it will be a leraentshie thing if the King. called upon to meet the . strain of &amp;monde the most momentous that could fall It" any man', 1011, left without the full support of the Peers. on whom, above all others, he bass right to rely.l</p>
