        <p>Mr. Limiter, on. make •pplicstion for fencing the Isnd oppoeite my house. Mr. Wow'ward naid that when he desired to enclose a piece of lend adjoining hie land he bed to approach the , Council and consult them as to the ingotl fencing. lie thought every landowner, with reference to laud loch had been t.,ed by the public for any length of time, should be made to go through the same course. Ile did got see why they about,' not. Mr. Lineler, jun. The Council do not own it. Mr. Woodward moved that the District Council do not recognise the right of snynody to eacloerr land which bed been dedioated to the public for any con- siderable period. Mr. Da•ies seconded and this wee carried. IMIK.IIIII' hiIIVIMAOK 1 ONINIITTEr. Th's committee, in their minutes, recommended that the sewer in the main road, Deresbury, be extended to a point opposite the elementary rchool ; that the cost In borne two-thirds by the County Council and one- third by the District Council ace apecielchaJge against the parish of Daresbury ; that the work Im carried on by the Diatrict Council and the County Council repay to them their proportion of the cont. bt was resolved that tenders f. r the work be obtained from Mame. J. E. Davies and Co. end Mr. Thome Stringer, and that such tenders be rubmitted to the District Council together with the recommendations. The Clerk repotted that the Co linty Education Com- mit tee had agreed. It was also resolved MO Mr. Beckett of afanchester for a fee 01 20 guineati,he engaged to report on a sewer- age scheme for Moore and that in the event of his echerce being adopted he le paid 7 per cent., on the contract, inclusive of the cunt of the report. The preliminery report, it was stated he the clerk, had been received and the Moore Sewerage C ininiittee proposed meeting Mr. Beckett in the village with the object of seeing if Nl.ime Dail, Moore 'louse and the Dross could be pro•ided for in the scheme.</p>
