        <p>The Warrington Progremive Labe. Leagne held another tinge and splendidly enthusiastio open-air meeting at Bridge Foot on Monday evening. There VII no attendance of about 1,500 and the opeembeis in condemnation of the House of I,rds met with maeifeet approval. Mr. B. Wyo. Jr.nes preerided sod the principal peter wra Mr. T.H. (amide, of klanchester. The CHAIRMAN said they wete PIGHTINO THR LAST ROUND in the Veto I stile. /Appian. ) Tory had gained on pointn in all the p *viva rounds and they were seine to sin rh. (hat it nag', (Applaore) Why obeuld the House it 1.. rds in its present condition be allowed to exist ? It mm an rot of harmony ssith time spirit of the time.. There ran no other et Witty In the world that had to, h an ins, ution. It was not in accordance oilh ri pr.. entative government that the horde should bare the point to block the popular will No one coold claim that they were a revising Chamber They did not se kto feriae remise,. that ei re /mooed 13, he HOWO of Commonc for they lad frequently panned Bills whir h they hollered to be hsd.i'ley co.ido rid the Old Age Penoirma Act to bets had mem., al d yet them did oil n•virre it.Tbe Hon. of Lone I did not ertrot to prevent hasty leglolotion ; ! they Would reiret Or prom Inn inn minutes ruettellrea olt which the iii . of Commons had spent weeks and evna moot.. lint now there on going to in an end to all that in future and, after tire penning of the Veto Bill, Liberal logialotion would hare a. good a ch.oe or, sill,. F. hi Smith amid, as had a cheer. of woolnr into law as Tory mentorco had had in the p tot. (Aryl. e.) Mr. °AR IDS, in I lie C..11,8e of a Inert, and forceful addreso, raid we had been living months in • mire of terror. t Laughter ) tin Lords had told In they were not going to lake it lung down. (Laughter.) They Ito re not going to neon the Parham at Bill. 1 hey w•re not going to do thir,that and the otl or : hot they had not told on shot they were going to do. (Lanahlef.) We had that to barn ye. no we moot be p.tient and wait cod see.' But asere were one or two things about the m, sr nt mood of the Houser of Lord. that were worth r or.oid. ration. " I do not think you would be notch • 0.6 fl " continued the opeeker " if they were to emigrate inc body. ( lecteht...) BcPl'oce we had no Lords oh a'l I think, we should be Brune. what hetAer 01l than snare to-day. If our Conserva- tives Blends are tams, t. if •</p>
        <p>STRONG:AR !STOCR ACT</p>
        <p>is reeded, if it is requisite thst no should have • great estate of nob'ea and all tint kind of thine, it or he much better if ne bad Wile Id them. (Laughter). So I/ Woo we say wear, going to make 500. 000, SOO or even 1,010 ol them, surely we are doing exactly what our Tory Irked, want us to do. (Laughter). If these gentlemen are such shining lighte to our poor, benighted common centre, let us have more light. (Laughter). I ere glad they ere going to bane 500 more pews. I wish they would make it 5,000 or, better still, 10,000, because the more peers they male the more COMMA. Will peemses b.... If you have Lady aomething taking in your caching, ci they do in Prince, and it you have Lord aomebody else bringing loom gror.evies,ss they do in Italy, it would not hurt the peerage would it? But it would certainly do one thing —it would tends the great mass of the coml.nc of this country the common dignity that belongs to manhood and womanhood. (Applauth). You me told that the Constitution Into danger —thnt es are preaching blood red revolution, and that presently property will .t be safe, and the lives of your a is.. and famPitor and your he...throes will be in danger. (Langhter.) This land of yours—this grand and Cl ri011• lund (or which yen ere told you ought to tight, stiffer and die—ii going tel. tack and ruin. (I...e,hter ) Ire are anted to behave that this land is 70Ure—tb.t it belongs to yon. You won't he offended if I say that you •te not in it at all. You don't count. Yoe are not ody. You are only somebody no long 114 you can work Its profit for some. body else. (Ile., hear.) Take away ell the frills and the trimmings in m what met, eenerally say, and you know It ia an tact that the business of the workingmen I. to be sol or, to feed and clr the the children that God I. nil to have given him, to educate then. an that they can nork, •13.1 %%ben th. y are tumble to cork—there is alway • the ritilkhOlirie door Opel% it may Inputting it rudely and brutally, lot it in . elatoluto, bare, bald fact." Continuing. Mr. ()made pointed out that the Constitutional difficulty bed not come about in litte minutes ; it was no newfangled notion. The Liberals %ere</p>
        <p>AILL,ED Or RED REVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>The answer to that was very simple—there never bed been a revolotion in any nation upon earth that bad not been brought shout by the aritdocrecy. They said to the lon nehmen over lost jeer. ego " Are you hungry? Eat gtese." What did they say to the poor of ? They had not got so far es to say Est gr,ss " but they had got no far as to use it WM God's will that they should be honer,. Ile (the speelter) said that vvrs a lie. (Applause.) We were not the only nation in which there war unrest at the present time. lie Ittrew (MIMI king of Bosnia and of the condition. there. The Cony was raid to be nest by Clod to rule over the Russia., just as the Duke of Norfolk was Mid to be sent by God to rule over his estates. (Laughter.) There wan • passage in the old Book which said" The cattle upon a thoutrand hills are mine, with the Lord." That might be no, but somebody had got a tremendous mortgage on them. (Laughter.) Germany was more uneettled thew we were, but the trouble was beneath the autface and was even more dangerous. "It has not come jet" raid the speaker " that you are whipped end flogged if you gather at street corner., 'I bat will come after a while if you allow it. All you have to do into be patient, sing hymns, he good, eat dry bread when coo can't get bread and butter, and thy a hipplug will come in due time. They tell us all is quiet in America, but it is not. Proportionate to the population of America, as com- pared with England, there are three to o. of</p>
        <p>WORKING:4LN 0000P1.01160 IN A VRICA, se °Diapered with England. You my there in no House of Lords there. No, bat there is the same system which has created the loam of Lords in this country and while we hare nothing whatever against the individual members of the House of Lords as such, we have everything against the system which made them." (Applause.) Mr. Derside referred to the greet struggle when English workingmen had to obtain the vote, but now that they had got it, they were of no , more value, no far as the legielative power of a vote was concerned, thao a drop of water in the river. Why? Became, there was another power, higher than they, which could wipe them our entirely. They could undo all that the people lied done and say "We know better then you what is good for you, and we will give you what we think you ought to have." Did the old men remember the Act by which it wsa attempted to make it legal for Nonconformista to be buried in consecrated ground ? The Lords slid it would be sacrilege end the Ilishope said God would nut hive them if they allowed it to be done. Ile (the speaker) was not poking fun at the Establiabisient but at the</p>
        <p>!,!'1.11,.!111:0[11.1".</p>
        <p>of the men a ho bad the ruling of things at that titne. (Applause). Was there any workingmen present ohose non had pasted throneh the Board school and the higher grade school sad bad, hy winning • scholarship, weed on to the Unive.ityr. let Lint remember that the lloise of Lords atood for twenty- nine yeare and taid that no child of a Nonconformiat should ever enter a University as a student. ("Shame.") Discusaing the C 0101,06411,0 of the House of bottle, Mr. Ga.ide said"! have heard of Men who say their ancestors came over to England with William the Conqueror and they are vary proud of it. Mine did not. I believe about the time of the Norman Conquest my anceators tvere keeping pigs in Seddleaorth. I ern very proud of the fact. I would rather they were Seeping pigs than that they had come over with lurch a gang of unmitigated ecoundrels sa came over at the Conquest. To-day the descendants of those gentlemen have improved the</p>
        <p>occasion. Take an example or two. Take his I;rico of Norfolk, on estimable gentleman, a gentleman from the end of the longest hair of his heed to the end of his his toe nail. (Laughter.) Ills eooestrnl home is Arundel Cantle. You could Omani pet Warrington in the hack rad and you would not mine it. (laughter ) The Duke of Norfolk loots you, and of course, he !mos ' the Lenente on his estate. But do yin know there not • poring roan On the Duke of Norfolk'. estate %OM 10-day can give in the banns for his marriage in the Pao lab Chu oh in order to merry and re ttln down in lie village in which he was born. Why ? Becaute in Greceof Norfolk ref...o to allow any cottage po party LO be erected on hi• estate, only to ...lief', the ri quire- moots of kin own reteiners. And 3rt he lot. 3on. (Laughter). Take his Grace of Northumberland. Ile I. President of the Sanitary Ana°, tattoo of Ii suit firitain—that in to enty, he locate after the tewea end drain., and sera that your houses are well eared for •• d that yen hare enough cubic fent of air to hren he— in somebody eines bowies, not bin own. Ms Grace ow Ns A OIiKAT COURTLY</p>
        <p>ID Durham, from which he !PCP,. a Irene rent mullet.. log eon.., £lO,OOO aye .r, besid.b r.iye ilia, (A Voice : " Lloyd George aid take some of iloit from him.") Yen should not my that. You will get arrested if you do. (Laughter). The colliers work night Ileum ahilia, sad when a collier bee di an eight holm, OA knows be is tired. The colliery proprietors have offered to rent or to buy land ftom the fluke; hey have not offered to mesl it—they woutil if they had my , opinion. (Laughter.) His Gracie m3s "1 will neither Ii eon nor sell, nor will I give you a pod of my lend to build 31 tinges for colliers." Al.ll they hoes tot to walk manly for Intim, morning st.d Light, back to their kennels. I bay</p>
