        <head>Examiner Saturday, July 22, 1911</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, July 22, 1911</p>
        <p>THE VETO PEERS. Mr. Harold Smith's "Offensive Remark."</p>
        <p>In the Howe of Commons on Tuesday, Mr. Wedgwood asked whether the prospective addition to the members of the Upper House would connist chiefly of landlords, nod whether the Prime Minister would give the Hoene an opportuni y of discussing the mole: t ion on the subject of the Parlia Lent Bill of which he (Mr. Wedgwood) bad given notice. Mr. 11. Smith (U.. Warrington): Having regard to the hon, member's obvious ensiety not to be excluded front the list, could not the right hon. gentlemso recommend him for • peeraga st Once? (Opposition cheera and laughter.) Mr. P. W. WWI (h. Leigh) : Mr. Speaker, to it in order to make an offeesive remark of that kind about • member ?—(Opposition laughter.) Mr. Lloyd George : The first part of the question . the per appears to deal with ahr j, dhetical altos- lion. "'be answer to the second part is lathe net/Wive. Mr. G. Lxonbury (Lab.—Bow end Bromley) Will the Government have coy guarantees that the gentlemen who will he made peers will vote straight. ? (I aughter). Mr. W. Crooks (Usk—Woolwich) : Would it not lie better to abolish the lot! 110, f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SISTER VERONICA'S SECRET.</head>
        <p>SISTER VERONICA'S SECRET.</p>
        <p>Br DOROTHY BAIRD,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Impertinence Of Kr. kleroggine," "The Story of a Look," "The Girl with the Sweet nee," "A Seexible Girl," dm</p>
        <p>It wait such a PI WOO. face so very sweet and gentle, and there was a pathetic look of inner suffering and repression about it which made it all the more. ottraetive. It was no pale, too— almost as white as the flapping nun's coif that surrounded it—and the eyes were so large and blue and patient, and the lips no hard set, yet so mobile, that no one could page it without turning to look at it again. Dora Morgan studied it long and closely an she ivalked up and down the dingy platform of Baker-street Station, and she took even more turns than she need have done in order to do flO. The face interested her strangely. Besides, it was so fresh and pleasant to look upon in the midet of the murky, under- ground atmosphere. "I wonder what they do to the poor thing to make her look like that ?" she thought She looks an if she were going to the dentiat, and knew he wan going to hurt her terribly, but knew also that ha would kill her outright if she screamed or made a single moan. Is that like a nun's life, I wonder, to suffer a great deal, and never murmur or complain or even tell a friend? I should go mad if I couldn't talk about all I felt and did."</p>
        <p>- She turned, and again she could see the nun, sitting very still and impassive, only looking up now and then when a train name in. . .</p>
        <p>....... _ "1 wonder if she knows how becoming that grey gown is to her, or whether she never thinks of anything no earthly?" continued Dora to herself. "She isn't supposed to notice such things, I know; but I don't think even a nun could help doing things she isn't supposed to sometimes especially when she is young and petty like this one.</p>
        <p>Thin' signal at the end of the platform went down with a click.</p>
        <p>"How late Hugh 1...• ..'ne thought. "I wonder if that nun ever waited for a young man, in the jolly days before she became a nun ? No " looking shrewdly at the pensive face—"l don't think no. If she had, she would have found out how nice it ix, and she would never have taken the veil. I should like to know what she thinks about as she sits there no quietly. She hasn't cot one of those little books which nuns generally read while they wait. She looks as if she had prayed no much that her thoughts were holy enough without the aid of books to keep them so."</p>
        <p>Another train came Apo 'gig through the tunnel, gurgling and scrooping as trains will when they come into Baker-street Station. "Will the right train never come P" said Dora to herself as the train drew up along the platform, and the carriage doom flew open. Then she looked to see if the nun were still id her post. But she was gone. The right train had come for her. . _</p>
        <p>e- Down at the far end of the atation Dora espied a familiar figure. Ilia back wan towardn her, however, and he was searching for her in the opposite direction. In another moment she was speeding down the platform toward. him. . . . . . • .•</p>
        <p>"thigh !""she cried:touching him lightly on the elbow. "How late you are. The words were not encouraging, but her eyes expressed welcome enough to satisfy the most ardent of men. "I am sorry; I was detained," he replied, shaking her hand. They were very formal in public, these two. The train slid by them towards the tunnel; for an instant the steam parted and • gleam of light fell upon a little grey figure seated demurely in a third-class carriage, and a sweet face smiled at them from under a flapping white coif. Then it wan lost to eight in the gloom. "Wasn't she pretty ?" asked Dora after • moment's pauve. "I have been watching her for a long time, and I have wondered all sorts of things about her." "It was a sweet face," he said, absent- mindedly. , They had climbed up into the open air, and stood watching the traffic crossing and re-</p>
        <p>"Now for a green 'bits," cried Dora. "I am dying to see that dear little house." He drew her baek into the relict of the booking-office. "We cannot take that little house," he said, gravely. "Can't take it ?" she repeated, in sonic con- sternation. The idea presented a whole host of unpleasant probabilities. He looked down at her smilingly, as if 6 enjoyed the sight of her unnecessary perturba- tion. Indeed, she looked pretty enough to warrant his delay in supplying an explanation. "What would you say to a large house in the country and double the income you have promised to manage on ?" he asked at last. Her eyes danced. "What do you mean ?" she asked, incredulously. "I mean," be answered, "that I have a new and excellent appointment. Only it it buried in some dead-alive village, miles from anywhere or anybody. And you were looking forward to living in London," he added, regretfully. "Only because I was going to live there with you," she said, softly. "The country will be just as Mee." He smiled, suddenly and sweetly. "Sure?' he aaked, just for the sake of asking, "Sure," she repeated, steadily. And she knew it to be true, because she loved him.</p>
        <p>..... . . Sister Veronica knelt alone in the cloister chapel. The dim, red, sanctuary lamps shone softly down on her grey-robed figure, and on the placid face of the Rimmed Virgin carved above the altar. It was very sweet and tender, that Virgin dice, but Sister Veronica loved better to gaze at the figure of the Crucified which hung above the oyes beneath the crown of thorns looked down so lovingly, so pityingly from under the pain-knit brows. Up in the organ-loft a nun was playing dreamy cadences, soft, low melodies, as if she had wandered into some calm haven of her own, far removed from the fret and fume of the world.</p>
        <p>Sister Veronica WWI very happy. The peace of the cloister had entered into her heart. As she knelt she was pouring forth her soul in praise and thankfulness for the calm joy of her life. For her there were no hankerings after the world she had left; it had used her badly in the days gone by, and she longed to forget it. She WWI even glad that when she died ahe need not go beyond the convent walls, but would be put to rest among the flowers, within sound of the yokes of the nuns as they chanted in the chapel. The door at the far end swung softly on its hinges, and a breath of warm air, laden with the scent of flowers, was wafted up the cool, dim aisle. A nun stood beneath the arch and beckoned to Sister Veronica, who rose and followed her at once. .</p>
        <p>"The Holy Mother wants you," whispered the nun, as they crossed the velvet grave of the court. "They want more nurses at hleamarsh. The fever has broken out in the big school, and you and I are to go at once." She looked about her furtively as she spoke, The nuns were not supposed to speak to one another save at certain hours. But as Dora Morgan had surmised, they sometimes did what they were not supposed to do. Sister Veronica looked round at the grey, weather-beaten cloister walls, and stretched out her arms absentmindedly to the convent pigeons which fluttered cooing around her. Something very near to rebellion rose in her placid breast She must go out into the fretful world once more, and only just now she had been thanking Heaven that she could forget it. Yet she might not even question the command. She would not if she might. It was her duty to go, and duty to her had grown almost a joy. Almost, not quite, for her heart was very heavy when she left the convent on that sunny afterno)n.</p>
        <p>4 • . • "Will he ive, Sister Veronica Do you think that we rya save him?" Dora Martin's face wse very wistful as she bent over the cot, and her voice was full of the agony of a young mother in deadly fear for her firstborn.... . . . .</p>
        <p>Sister Veronica looked long and gravely at the little man who tossed moaning to and fro; then rho raised her head and met the other's eyes. Dora was still very young and very pretty, and hhe wax very much distreimed, but her mouth was firmly not, and ahe looked brave. It was safe to tell her the truth.. ..1 am afraid no will die," said the nun, sadly hut firmly. "But we will de what we eau," she Wed, more cheerfully,</p>
        <p>Dera'e lip treinbled, "He is only four years old," she said, folt.eringly. "And " with a wan little smile—" there never was a baby like him, and never will 11C. 1--"</p>
        <p>..- Sho stopped. She was no very near to tears. Sister Veronica was silent The years she had spent in conventual seclusion seemed to have deprived her of the power of expressing her sympathy. But they had not robbed her of the power of feeling sympathetic. She stroked Dora's bright hair timidly. "I am so glad you came," said Dora, looking up and trying bravely to smile. "You have such a strong, good face. You will help me to bear—it. I am sure I have seen you before." And she puckered her brow, searching back through all her memories to find a former vision of the sweet face before her, Sister Veronica shook her head. e I have been • nun for many years," she said, quietly. Dora watched her as she bent over the little fellow in the cot, soothing him with gentle words, and a strange little smile played round her lips. "You ought to have been a mother," she said. She was sorry directly the words were spoken, for the nun's face flushed painfully, and for a moment the repression which always seemed th bind her features was broken, and the women's "soul showed through, quivering as it a raw wound had been touched. . .</p>
        <p>"I do not know," she said, calmly. "I am oontent." And in her heart of besets she added the simple word "now." Downntairn a door was opened and cloned softly, Ond Dora rose. "There is my husband," she said. "I must go and tell him about Dicky. It is better he should know before he sees him." Iler face was brave, almost cheerful. It was necessary she should be RO for her husband's sake. Sister Veronica sighed. She knew well enough that in times of trouble women bear their sorrows best in comforting those they love. They can almost forget their own suffering in trying to alleviate another's pain. Only some women have no loved ones to help, or, worse still, have not the right to comfort them. Dora left the door ajar. "Hugh," oho called, softly. "Come here. I want to speak to you." "Ilow is be ? " Caine in eager tones up the staircase. . . .</p>
        <p>"Bush! Come into the boudoir; I will tell you all about him." That war all she heard before the boudoir door was closed, yet Sister Veronica stood by the cot like one stormy.. Only three words, hot it was quite enough. The voice seemed to speak out of the past. For the moment she wan a girl again, and she was playing in the orchard at the old farm. Overhead the apple-blossom blushed against the crisp blue sky, and underfoot the leaf xhadows danced upon the lush, green grays All the world was full of spring and sunshine and the song of birds, and it was good to be alive. Only the convent bell tolled slowly from over the valley, and its harsh note jangled strangely among the merry voices. Slowly the colour came back to her cheeks. What had elm been dreaming? She pulled her- self together. After all, it was but a mere likeness in a voice. There might be a hundred such voices in the world. Yet the name was the name—thigh. She did not know the surname. She bad been told to go to the schoolhouse, and she had come. She had not asked the name of the stricken family and no one had told her. She most not lie so silly. Hut when husband and wife came into the reoin together she knew there was no mistake. It was the same dark fare, the name clear-rot features, the same midden. sweet smile, which came and went like sunshine on an April day. For a moment her blood ran chill, hoping yet dreading he should recognise her. But no. She had aged more than he in the years since they had met, and, beaidea, the dress she wore was an ample disguise. Ile greeted her as an absolute' stranger. Yet she was glad to withdraw and leave them alone with their child. She told herself that she could not bear it, that ahe must either speak or go away at once. cre under hie roof, to tend his child, to help Itlio to bear his voice, daily, hourly to be tantalised by the sight of their happiness—it was too horrible. And she might not even hate the woman who stood in her place. She was a nun, and once more she must struggle against all the dearest emotions of her heart and beat them down. She had done it before, and she knew how hard and how bitter it was. .</p>
        <p>71.1.u.t.'We"s•ta."y.e-dv.o;..—lt ;a:X.-a-joy, after all, to be near him and to see him. She felt it to be a sin that she stayed for that reason, but never- theless ehe stayed the sin was so sweet. Some- times she looked at his hands, strong and brown and well-knit, and wondered how it was she could keep herself from seizing them so that once more she might feel their warm, firm grip. But she kept her self-control. One night, no they both bent over the cot in the gloaming, she stroked the sleeve of his coat. He did not feel it, the action was so soft and gentle, and nobody saw her do it, yet her cheeks burnt guiltily for hours after. As a rule she was just herself, a quiet, sweet-fared nun, who went about her duties patiently and unobtrusively, and who effaced herself most tactfully whenever she was not wanted. It seemed to her that she was living two lives —one the daily round of sick nursing, the other a dream-life, a life which always claimed her at the first sound of Hugh's voice. For hours together she would be playing once more in the orchard, sometimes among the daffodils of</p>
        <p>-0 spring, or lazily idling away long numiner after- noons on the abed), grass, watching the clouds roil *cream the blue beyond the leafy boughs. Sometimes it was autumn, and they were pilfering from the Imsketa piled with rosy fruit, while the holt grey clouds hurried over the valley, and the wind blew buoyantly, as it could only blow down there among the dear hills of the South. And all the while the convent bell was calling, calling. It I had called her across the meadows all the days of _ . . . . . „ .</p>
        <p>her childhood, till she had yielded in her first real trouble. And now it coiled in her memories, bidding her repined what and who she was. And it helped her to endure, no that she made no sign, only the look of inner suffering grew on her pale face, and her lips twined tighter drawn than ever, no that Dora looked at her anxiously now and again. "You most take more care of yourself, Sister," she would inv. "I look to you to save the child —if we can." And Sisfer Veronica would smile brightly, and protest that she was well and strong. She (lid all that was possible, humanly speaking, for the child. He was Hugh's eon, and he ems dear to her for his father's sake. But she had known from her first glance at the poor little fever-worn face that there was no hope, and that in spite of all their care the little life must go from them. When the child died her time of bitterest suffering came. It had been bad enough to see Hugh daily, share him anxiety, talk to him, watch him, and not ileclare herself. But when the blow conic and she now his grief, the sharpest sting lay in the knowledge that another woman must comfort him and she must stand aside. She could not go back to the convent; she was wanted to help in the school infirmary. She must see him many Dines a day, and it seemed as if the knowledge of his sorrow were always with her, 80 that sometimes she felt as if she could scream aloud in the agony of her mind. But no one guessed, she was so quiet and no patient. At last the time grew near when she might go, and she was glad. 'Fhe pence of the convent would be very sweet ntter these weeks of struggle. She e-as tired, too, with the long strain, and felt oddly weak and feeble, and she was haunted with the dread—almost the certainty —that she would betray herself before she went. Every day also prayed more earnestly against thin dread, and yet every day the conviction grew stronger. She could not leave his roof without one word of farewell—not to "Sister Veronica," but to that other girl he had known so well. It would be so 'meet, and she would never see him again. It was not fair to Dora, perhaps, but then Dora had had him for all these years, nod would have hiin always till he died. Surely she might have a moment, one little word of fare- well ? Besides, she had loved him first. It would be so easy too. She had only to loosen the fastening of her coif and allow a strand of hair to fall across her forehead, and elle was her old self. She had lost very little • of beauty, and, oh how sweet it would be to hear him call her by the old name. She dwelt upon the 'scene of recognition till she knew it by heart, and it lweame living before her. She must do it. Almost before she was aware of it she made up her mind. Accordingly.aiu the dusk fell on the last evening Of her stay at Me achoolnotiew, 550 loofiened ner coif and let Nome of the short curls fall about her forehead. It seemed as if she heard the convent bell call to her warningly as she did 10, Filn Mier it wig a eiu. but t ttuit mom.</p>
        <p>I she did not heed. She was but human, after all, no she smiled to herself in the looking-glasa —how nice it was to have • mirror again I—and then stole softly downstairs. She merely meant to any good-bye, to see that he recognised her, and then to rosy good-bye again. As she crossed the landing she beard the sound of voices through the boudoir door, which atood ajar, and she alipped into the deserted nursery and, begun to put away the toys Which still lay strewn about, for the mother had been too broken-hearted to do it herself. "Hugh, I have remembered," Dora wan saying. "I remember distinctly when I saw Sister Veronica before. It was on Baker-street Station that afternoon that I waited for you. We were going to see that little house in Bayswater. It wan the day you told me about your appointment here. Do you remember p I pointed her out to you as the train went off." "I do not remember that," came Hugh's voice in reply. "But she reminds me of someone all the same—someone I knew long ago, when I wan</p>
        <p>, a boy." His voice sounded dreamy as it came across the passage, and Sister Veronica stood still. She could hear her own heart heating loudly. There was a little creak of a wicker chair, as if Dora had drawn up closer to her husband. "Tell me about her, she said, coaxingly. Sister Veronica held her breath. What could he tell her, what could he tell this sweet young wife of km? Whet could he make of the crooked tale which was the truth? .</p>
        <p>"There Mal much to tell," she heard him nay. "She and 1 were playmates as children. I only saw her once or twice after we left the old home."</p>
        <p>"Tell me, what waa her mine ? Was she beautiful? and what colour was her hair? I have so often wondered what colour Sister Veronica's hair was when she 'hewed it to the world." .</p>
        <p>Ho laughed, a little uneasily the listener thought `lfer name was Elsa, Elsa Brand, Madam Curiosity," he replied. "She wax very beautiful, and her hair was just a tinge between gold and auburn," and a faint sigh came through the boudoir door.</p>
        <p>"How sentimental you sound!" said Dora, lightly. "I hope Sister Veronica's hair is that shade, it would suit her. And I wish I could see her with her cap off ; I think she would be beautiful. What became of El., Hugh ? Do you think that she could by any chance be Sister Veronica? "</p>
        <p>• "I do not know what happened to Ehat. I heard a rumour that ;the was married, that is all, he answered, slowly. But lam quite sure that i Sister Veronica s not abe."</p>
        <p>Another woman might have been satisfied, but the sharp cars of a wife could plainly detect n difference in his voice—s little note of diffidence which sought to slink by unobserved. "Why?" she risked, sharply. "Because "—it was plain to hear the reluctance In the voice now—"beeause I knew Elea very well, and I could not help recognising her if we were to meet."</p>
        <p>Sister Veronica smiled grimly to herself in the little pause before Dora spoke again. "Was—was El., anything to too?" The words were startling, unexpected. How quick is the instinct of intuition in some women! Sister Veronica gripped the bark of a chair for support. She could not move away even had she wished to do so. Was he going to tell his wife of that one slimmer he had spent in the South after he was grown up? "Vera time• yes. " "You loved her ?" "An • boy of twenty loves his old playmate, nothing more." "You thought it was more at the time?" "Yes."</p>
        <p>she ? " "She was very fond of me in the same way." "Did you oak her? " There was silence for a few minutes. Dora</p>
        <p>Was a crucible WOlOlll/, and she knew that there are very few men who have not noise such events ias this n their past, only she had stumbled upon : it rather suddenly. "How did it end?" she asked, slowly. Again Sister Veronica drew her breath with a gasp. Could he, dared he tell the story of how he had left her with loving words and many promises? Would he explain why he had never written, why he had sent no address, why he had left her to go through all the weary stages of hope deferred, broken faith, and final despair? Was it possible that he had had some good reason, and that she Woo to hear it now, after all these years ? "Don't ask." The answer rime huskily, imploringly. "I thought differently about sin+ things in those days— before I met you, dear or rather, I did not think at all." , Sister Veronica drew a deep breath. Ile lind no excuse to offer. Her god of clay was still a god of slay. "Yon never told me you had been engaged." Dora's TOWSI was wistful, and a little re- proachful. "1 /earest "—Sister Veronica would have stopped her ears if she could, for there was that in his . . _ . .</p>
        <p>tone whieh.mode her feel at; it she w^re on holy</p>
        <p>ground, but she seemed powerless to move- " dearest, I know I have never told you. There are many things in my past life, my life before I met you, of which I am ashamed, and of none am I more ashamed than of this incident upon which we have touched. But they were over</p>
        <p>and done with, and they could not be undone or remedied. Moreover, they had been repented of. Before I dared offer myself to you I tried to make myself more worthy of you, and I came to you, I verily believe, with a soul cleansed from the guilt of toy misdeeds, if not from the remembrance of them. It seemed to me unneces- sary, if not wrong, to worry you with a recital of them. I knew you were too sensible to imagine that my life had been blameless, and I thought I was sparing you pain in sparing you details. It was not se if I had still felt a lingering affection for Elsa, and so held beck the confidence. That would have been very wrong. I told you then, and I tell you now, that you are the only woman the world holds for me, and I have never lied to you, Dora. You believe ' that ?"</p>
        <p>The answer came quick and firm. "I do." "Perhaps I was wrong in not telling you. If SO I will tell you now. It is for you to decide." Dora's answer wan little more than • whisper, yet Sister Veronica heard. "You were right— you always are. And lam</p>
        <p>, not jealous even of your love for her. It must have been a poor sort of love if it ended meanly. Your love for me has pointed up, not down, has It not?"</p>
        <p>And then there was a long silence. Sister Veronica's lip trembled. Yes, she saw the difference, only too plainly, but it was not her fault that her love too had not pointed up. , Perhaps, if she had not been such a child in ' that tar-away summer she might have succeeded. Oh, the bitterness that lurks beneath that word .perhaps"! Almost mechanically she continued her task of putting away the unneeded toys, trying to arrange the room so that nothing that could pain the young mother should obtrude itself too obviously. She seemed quite numbed. Indeed, looking back on those moment., she was sure that even thought itself was stilled. "I am sorry Sister Veronica is going." It was Dora's voice, peaceful again and full of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>content</head>
        <p>content</p>
        <p>"So am I. Wo can never forget all she did for our little one, can we' I must try to see her and say good-bye to-night. I shall be in the schoolroom when she goes to-morrow," he added. "I think I hear her in the nursery now," said I)ora. "You had better catch her before she goes upstairs for the night." As in a dream Sister Veronica heard him posh back his chair, and his firm footstep on the landing. Before she had time to collect her thoughts he was in the room. "Busy, as usual, Sister," he said, cheerily. "But why work in the dark ?" ' ' • • •</p>
        <p>He fumbled for his matches. It was but • moment before he lit the lamp, but that moment was enough. It was enough for her to realise that slw loved him, and because she loved him she could not let the love which had not pointed up re-enter his life, nor could she even remind I him of it. Her handu went wildly to her map, land when the lamplight Hooded the room it was Sinter Veronica who faced him and not E 1... He thought she looked calmer and paler than ever,and he did not bee that hers was the intense calm of terrible agitation. . .</p>
        <p>tie -ilLee—dTorri;d—ihe-room. "Ever thought- ful." he said. "I do not know how to thank von : for all you nave done, ant tor nit ,on mart spared lay poor wife." "Do not mention it, rue repiirk though it lieenied as if the worsk totid ciao her . _</p>
        <p>I must wish you good-bye (o-ht. Ideli 0,4 imr Trou in the morning." he %id. bolding</p>
        <p>hann.</p>
        <p>oat his ...coma me yon will carry away nothing but grateful and even affectionate thoughts from Oda house." Her hand rested for an inatant in his warn, firm grasp, hut it seemed to burn her, sad she drew quickly away. You are very kind," ahe said. "Not at all. I mean it. tood-bya• "Good-hyt.." And that was all. In the dead of night, when 11l the house was .erapped in qiiietness and darkness, Sister Veronica crept softly downstairs to the hall, and laid her face against a Pont that hung upon the pegs. She tinned it many time., but she did not cry. The tears would come later, when she was buck in the monotonous quiet .of the Cloister. It was a stupid thing to do, perhaps, and it oa also a sin for • nun. Butts lola a very - I,rmlesa one, and it gave her a few moments , if something very near happiness. "Sister Veronica looked just the same as she did on Baker-street Station when she went away this morning," eaid Dora to her hitaband the ana night, "I wonder what her sorrow wits • • "I wonder," Repent-2d he. [Tim IND.I</p>
