      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>they had in view could be most surely attained if the procedure was by stages ; and "by a ntrady development of administrative in. chinery " the Bwird 'issued the order now in question embodying further regulations to provide for the notification if more of mt. monary tuberculosis occurring amongst inpatients or ent-patients at hospitals or similar institutions for the tneatment of the sick which are suppnrted wholly '</p>
        <p>or paNially otherwise than from rates and taxes. The notification must be met within IS hours alter the first recognition of the disease. Warringtoniana may be recent:led that the order confers some special powers upon C,ouncile for the prevention of the spread of infection in cues of pulmonary tuberculosis. They are empowered to take the necessary in, retires to moore ths disinfecti.,n of infected premises and of infected articles. and in encore the destrection of infectious discharges. They are also empowered to supply all each reasonable assistance' as will tend to diminish the risk of infection arising from the conditions effecting the use or occupation by a patient of a room as it sleeping apartment. I note that they are further entitled to supply for the use of the patient any appliance, uteneil or article, (each, for instance, an sputum lii.xes or bottles, paper handker- chiefs and ilisenfectantai which may be of Raistonce in preventing the tweed of infection. Mona., at such institutio. as I have described, expectoration on the part of patients is strictly forbidden. Each is pro- vided with a bottle for the purpose, and scene wear a muzzle-like arrangement for purposes or disiefiction. ST. SWITHIN. Saturday last wan St. S., ithin's Day. The go d old mint, eh°re ruetnory lam suffered ridicule on account of the nonsense about his having to do with a wet July, was first a priest and afterwards Bishop of W inchester in the ime of the Saxon Kings Egbert and Ethel wolf (father and son). That he was of exemplary clientele, is dew from the testimony of William of Malmesbury and other monks of those times. Hear what comfortable words the homer mith :—" Though thie good Hahne w.a a rich treasure of all virtue., those in which Le took most delight were those of humility and charity to the poor; and that, in the diecharge of his episcopal functions he omitted nothing belonging to the true pastor. Ile built divers churches, and repaired others, his mouth was always open to invite runners to repentance, and to admonish those who stood to bewails of falling. Be was meet severe to himself, and abstemious in his mode of living. He delighted in spiritual exercises, and in conversetion would bear no discourse that did not tend to edification. He died on the 2nd of July, 861." He wan desirous of being buried in the church- yard, and not in the chancel, of the Minster. On his death the request was complied with, but the monks taking it into their heads that it was wrong for a mint to be in the open churchyard , resolved to remove the b ,dy into the choir, which was to have been done with solemn pomp on the 15th July. But it rained so violently on that day, and for forty days afterwards, that they were obliged to alsodon their design, and instead they erected a chapel over his grave. That, as all readers of the , " Examiner " know full well gave rise to the timing that a wet 15th is invariably mcceeded by 10 of the like kind. Its lila has been tented by reference to the record taken at Greenwich for 20 years, and it is given in Clmnibers' " Book of Days." According to thin, in six of thoee years Swithin's day was wet, and rain fell on many days, varying from 13 its one year, which was the least number, to 26 in another year, which was the high.% number. Then in 11 years the day was dry, but there were rainy days in every one of thoee, rain falling on 12 days in one year, which was the lowest number, and 31 in another year, which was the highest number. Indeed, on the average, there were most rainy days after St. Swithin's Days had been dry. Thus for the present July h.' for the greater pert been one of the most sultry that I I can recollect siece that of 1887, but it is often a moist month ; indeed, we seem periodically to get into a cycle of wet Julys. and of course St. Swithin has no more to do with it than I haye. WEATHER-WISE AND OTIIERWISE. Something over 50 years ago, or just about a thousand years after the decease of the notable saint just meet,, oed, the "Builder" ,informed its readersl that there was before the Academy of Science at Paris 1" a wonderful invention " of Mons Helvetins Otto, of Leipide, by which he promised to "insure fine weather." Here is the wilier'. explanation : Ile erects a platform at a considerable height in the air, on which he places a" propeller," or huge bellows, worked by steam. With these bellows, which are "very powerful," he blows away the donde as they gather; and, as rain comes from the clouds, it most macessarily follow that where clouds are not allowed to gatheri there can be no rain. Ile maietains that if a cattail, number of hie " Rain Propellers," or "Pluviluges," as he has named them, are placed at intervals over the city, be can provide fur the inhabitants a continuance of ;fine weather, and a certain protection from sudden showers and muddy streets, no long the terror of fair pedi striate. The poor inhabitants of adjoining towns would be to he pitied. What would become of them? As for the lhlovifsge proprietore, their motto ought to be," A pres noes le deluge." Victims, however, as we in England have of late been towel weather, atilt we cannot Warne the Frenchman's " Pluvituges," inasmuch as France itself has been in cearly the same predicament. Vi hat think my Warrington readers of this? At all events, it came to nothing like the much more recent con- trivancre for inducing rain in droughty seasons by persistent artillery fire! HIRST HOLLO WELL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"THE 130 W-WOWS."</head>
        <p>"THE 130 W-WOWS."</p>
        <p>The phrase which came so readily to the lips of Mr. Mantalini may nerve as text fur a eupplementary paragraph respecting the show specially referred to Mat week. What some of the sucewevies thereat achieved by certain doggy exhibitors involve in the way of preparation may be illmtrated by mention of a alight incident occurring in toy own experience on Sunday Ida. I was strolling through the fields in the evening of that day when I happened upon a little group. in the midst of which wae a wee doggie with its haw carefully and elaborately plaited, and its hind feet encased in a eon of slipper. A lady admirer of the toy " beadle " eno impiring an to the meaning of all this, and the proud owner \VA° explaining that "he" was a valuable chow dog. "'hat this sort of thing is all very right and proper and mecessary, I have previously recognised and been at some pains to unlike plain tie any who might be disposed to scepticism and cynicism in relation to it; hut, after all, it meat be allowed that there in something in the complaint that dog shoe° are spoiling our terriers, and that the type one men on the bench nowadays ate not suitable for work. To be norm, most of those who will reed thin may not want in dog for work ; but, even so, there are cousiderationa of importance which are unaffected by the point an to Mows " The great Lafayette," who lost his /if* in the dreadful music hall fire in Edinburgh, had a favourite saying "The more I see of mei, ihe more I like dogs," and the newspapers made mud, of this without noting flint it was originary coined by Mark Twain, and that the lamented illusionist had borrowed it and omitted to acknowledge its source. Anyhow, there's a good deal in it, significant and sufficient comment being supplied by the fact that the great majority of British house- holders are dog-keepers. A while ago, Mr. T. F. Dale, writing in "Beachwoorra Magazine " claimed that of all our dogs there is none no versatile in mind or body as the terrier ; and penioually Tam 80 strongly in mnipathy with the proposition that I should like to enlarge upon and add evidence in support of it. As, however, I way not dwell longer upon the theme, it will perhaps be more useful to close these random remarks with the advice of the authority who urges If a dog is no good in any dial% or form, why keep him ? II you want to keep a dbg, and don't intend, or try, to educate him to neefulnees, why get one? It is easier by Inc to suppress the temptation to keep a dog than, having acquired one, to harden your hearts to have it des- troyed. And ahould you desire a continuance of their esteem, it is scarcely wise or kind to afflict your friends with a 08188088 you cannot longer tolerate yourself, baying bought, begged, or been presented with the dog which takes your fancy, your chief aims should be First, to keep it in health; and secondly, to train it in the way it should go, End not in the way it would go. Sucosesful thus, you will for ,none years probably permits a source of joy, even if not a thing of beauty. THE GREAT WHITE PLAGUE." I read with sympathetic interest and profit Mr. C. E. Parker's mesaage of "hope for consumptives" Having within a fortnight revieited one of the " excel- lent Sanatoria " which he so earnestly commends, I can bear out what your valued contributor writes an to these. I have previously taken occasion to mention this particular Sanatorium. Eetablisheel seven or eight years ago in connection with the Bradford Calm it nestles OD a sheltering hillside at Eastby, four mile% beyond Skipton, and as approached by road from the later place presents the appearance of a picturesque Swim village. The petients practically spend the time of their sojourning, sleeping :old waking, to the open air. Their work and exercise and all theydo is systematised and regulated under the active supervision of a resident doctor. Divided into" light "and" heavy "gangs, they are daily set to gardening and other work " according to their emend ability " with intervaln fur rest, refreshment, medicine, and no forth. I should like especially to ompliaatee Mr. Parker's point that it is not necessary , for any afflicted with pthisis to leave this country for treatment abroad. As a matter of fact. I know of at least one instacce in which it wee positively harmful. A young newly-married man of my acquaintance 'sub- mitted himself foe medical examination for insurance purposee, and the policy for which he was anxious was declined on the report of the doctor that he wee pthisical. A member of the firm by whom lie was employed, being a Swiss, TM- monended a sanatorium in his own country, and the noticed, spent some six months here;t Lot the food was loathsome to him, and the conditions generally were such that lie heard with intense longing soy testimony respecting the dietary, A., at Eaattiy. As for dime to whom Mr. Parker alludes as being unable to leave home for such place., it may be remembered that I have previously written of benefit derived by car who had a hut built in the trees near hie house; now a medical student in lUanehester, he is apparently cured. My point is that the local authmities will not in such circumstances be at all likely to put restrictions in the way of open-air structures by individuals. Nothing ago, eta conference in London,kliss Catherine Webb suggested the prevision of In me shelters for suitable cases of coneumptiomand a model of one invented end per fectrel by Stanley Bates was on the same occasion exhibited . . _</p>
        <p>and desenbed. It Is worthy of note in passing that "J. Hirst Hollowell and the Education movement," this siourge of civilisation is not found w ithin the is the title of a book just published by the Northern Arctic or Antarctic circles. Pie"ntion being pro- Counties Education League. Italy William Evans verbialle, aid, indeed, most obviously better than and William Claridge. I know Mr. Claridge, having cure, one may here recall Mr. John Burns' important travelled with him on a memorable occasion; butt new regulations with iegard to tuberculows, which knew the late Rev. Hirai Hollowell better. I reported came into force rither mere than than • couple of him roan, a time and oft. While yet a Congregational months ago. An explanatory circular stated that the minkter -in Rochdale, he need to deliver political p, in,iolo of cemPokorY notification whinit ma. ketures, bat I thought little of the ordinary platform embcdied itt the regulations of 194,{ had been justified i fforts of either Mr. Hollowell or of the Rev. Joseph bverrerienee• It in now generally reeogoineek an Hocking, the novelist, brother of the popular Silaa affording valuable aid in securing effeetiye adminis- yr. Joseph Hookitt, and hit.. Hollowell were both too trative (manures in relation to tubercedoste: But the much given to ant 'dotage - considering that they Local Government Board felt dna the object which poeed al aer ion lecturete, flat the latter WU I</p>
