      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EDUCATION PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>DECLINE IN SCHOOL ATTENDANCE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Exploitatioa of Child Labour.</p>
        <p>The principal discussion at the monthly mem ing of the Worrington Education Comntitt. on Monday centred in the important mutation of the decline in 81 hool attendance. Coin cillor 11. Roberts presided and them were 6180 present :—Mr A J Jolley (depost3- chairman), Alderman Bennett, Councillors Hellion, !tittles, Dickinson, Jackt,n-Holmea, Jenkins, Gocker, Ecrnherton, Steel, and Wilkinnon, r.. James Davies, Nlostas R T Fairclough, J Cbritnen, I, elreening,ll Eingdon, E F It Roberts J Upson nod 0 Walser, Mrs C Jackson Holmes,and MrJ Moore Murray (director).. . . .</p>
        <p>‘-iii:-.l.lileso7t raised the goration nn the minutes of the School attendance Committee. Ile mid be did not wish to attack nineoo or to criticise the action of that Committee in any sense of the word, but he should like to put before them. few feet... Bing for inyeatigatlon. Nine years ago the average attendance in the schools in Warrington wan 11,627. Since that tin e the population had increased by at least seer n thousand. Therefore the children of wheel age moat be 1,100 more tine in 1902. The bust monthly return, however, showed that the average attendeeoe was now only 300 more then it I was nine years ago. That motet that in spite of the 'fact that they bad a moch larger number of tifluero and s more complete organixsteon, with an education rate of I. 7ld compered with I 1.8,1., the relative propor- tion of children attending achool wee leas now than nine years ago. It would he said that nine years ago there were children tinder four years of ace In the schools. (Hear, beer). The number of children under four in 1902 was 388, but at the same time there were only 61 over 13 years of age. Ile ventured to submit that they had se many children over 13 now an they had under four in 1902. Ile was not blaming anyr ne, but the fact was •serious one and called for explsnation. Notwitbetandlng their rat idly Inermaing pepulation and the provision of 2,500 additionel wheel places, they had proportionately fewer children In their wheels than they had nine year. ago. They ought to take the matter into serious coneideration and are how It was that tee attendance at present witl so low. Woe it because the School Attendahce Committee wee innon• aliment in ire polic3? Wee It due to nnholoealegrantieg of partial exemption from school, or to the action of the magistrates In the i cop. tlen of three for bed attendence? Or was It the failure of any part of their organixati a? Those questions might be studied and investigated by the School Attendance Committee, or the full com- mitt° in geom.' purpoere. Ile thought that their r &amp;wetland expenditure was no great that so ferns the actual children receiving ohiontion were concerood,Lhey were not getting value for their money. It might be doe to acme extent to the medical leaped ion of the children. It might be that large numbers of children were exclnded by the school doctor who were not pre- viously excluded. But the fact remained that they had new only 300 more children in the schools than I bre yeara ago, and he tuggfutod inquiries should be made as to why it wee so many children who ought to be in the schools were apparently not there. Councillor l'ittillnutoB maid the Fchool Attendence Committee did a great deal of work and sat long hours. A vest amount of information wee required to got to the bottom of the matter, sod he thought a special sub conimitt. should be appointed. Councillor Betties; supported Councillor Pemba,- ton's suggeetion and said he thought it would be to the advantege of the committee. Variety] Wets hod hew discussed that were vital to the interests of the town end Ito admlnistretion.</p>
        <p>—Mr. A. J. JOl.l.lSt mid he was afraid it was not no much a knowledge of the facia that was required an collage to deal with the facts when they were known. The point Faired by Mr. Upton was important from the educational and sochtl points of view, hut until the members of the Education Conttnittee and the Town Council freed their reeponsibilitiao in the right epirit and were prepared to strengthen, their regnlations and prevent the</p>
