      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>F.% PI 01.TTION ONCIFILD LABOUR</head>
        <p>F.% PI 01.TTION ONCIFILD LABOUR</p>
        <p>es it oas atill carried on tonln-rge-e-ste—a'i; the town, it was not much use appointing a sub-comrnittee or talking about the matter. Anyone who had thought lunch about the question knew what the remedy win. It was aver; simple one, but it wanted mord courage to apply it. As yet, neither the Education Committee nor the Town Council bed traced itself up sufficiently to face that difficulty in the spirit an whiob it ought to be facet The School Attendance Committee spent an enormous amount of I time which might be saved and which, it, the long run, really did more harm than good. It one like a voice crying in the wildernese to ask the maj trity of the members of the Education Committee to face the subject in the manner in which it ought to be faced. No child under fourteen should be allowed to leave school hilt unfortunately many hundreds loft almost as soon as they mere thirteen, That wee where the diffi- culty In;. het them etrengthen their byelaws and stop thlgra.nting.eTexTtptiona.</p>
        <p>• The Bev. JAOSO, DA V I its said timid labour was going on nine years ago, but as far no hia personal knowledge mans, its exploitation in Warrington had decreased of , late yeara.There must be some other leeksge.l'ersonally he was quite willing to face the odiurn,if there was any odium,and deal with the matter. . .</p>
        <p>The CHAIEMAN said It would be interesting if the School Attendance Committee wood venture upon some reply to Mr. Upson's remarks. Among other poesible Causal Mr. Upson bed euggeeted that the magistrates might be too lex in the infliction of Hoes in regard to defaulting paten.. As one who am lergely concerned in any ctiticism in that direction, he (Councillor Roberts) combed to say that of all the unpleasant and unhappy experiences they had on the Bench there was nothing which came up before them that equalled lathe intensity of all that made for what was unhappy and miserable as the dealing with school ettenden. cases. The tales—and many of them were true—to which they had to listen of widows with large fatuities., who were dependent upon their own phyeical strength or the possible anistance of young children to keep their honon together, were scatter, that impressed the metro tratee, and until the State sires prepared to step in and give greater nsaistance in necesatolan nesn of that kind he did not see how any of body men with humanitarian feelings—. the School Attendance Committee undoubtedly was—could resist the appeal for exemption from school. They as magistrates could not shut oat altogether conditions of family life end as there nes no fund to provir'e the financial aasistance which must Ire given if the children were to be kept out of the work• house they had sometimes to take a lenient view. Other cases avere an absolutely obstinate in character that the Bench were pumled whet to do. They had fined and fined and better fined, and in some ca.n they found no feeor then four or five fines still unpaid when they were asked to inflict another. Ile knew Mr. Upson had made the criticiame in the best possible spirit and he was certain they would bear fruit. Ile (the speaker) sympathi.d with the ruggestion to appoint especial committee but he thought the School Attendance Committee had • good deal to say in its own defence which they shottld listen to before venturing upon nay drestic proposition. ( tommillo• nr ETON said his idea wee not to blame the School Attendance Committee hut to help them. Mr. UPSON said he was not attacking the School Attendance Committee. Ile Imes, how laborious and unpleasant their work was and how admirably meny membern did it. Ile particularly wished to associate the Chairmen of tho enhcommittee with that remark Councillor JENKINS said he entirely disagreed with Mr. Jolley int to the necessity for keeping ptor children at school until they were fourteen, whatever their quality of brain or capacity for learning might be. It was to his mind</p>
