        <p>Alderman BEKNIVIT expreeeed disagreement aph one part of C Jeuk h' speech. Ile did not approve of the differentiation which he endeavoilred to establish between the Live sexes. Moat of what he said was full or thst mated settee which they olwayo associated with Councillor Jenkins, bet it was inviditma to any that while we could hr with clever men we di.l not want too many clever women. Ile (Alderman {Sennett) was a believer broadly in the equality of the lieges end to propound such a heter.wlos doctrine as Councillor Jenkins's before a teemed body of that kind was not very creditable to the committee or to the individusl who Cr.. bold enough to proclaim it. (Laughter.) Hewes afraid that on that tuatter Committer Jenkins was hopelessly behind the time.. In the post it had been considered in accordance with civilization to treat worme, merely as dulls, mimes, co.rke, Ac., hot they had begun to show that they were capable of something more—anis work which was just as neccessary to the State. Whilst it eons miotake to try end innoculate a boy or girl with more knowledge thou they were capable of amimilating, or to try and make &amp;II children into geniuses, there should he no differentiation between theorizes. The question raised by Mr. Upson touched the whole social life of the nation. The problem lay in the lamentable poverty of manses of the population and the fact that they were not Milo to live in a decent way and to give their children the advantages they ought to have. Under the</p>
        <p>.... which minted in our .o-called ci•ilised country, it I beanie necessary to take children away from school end send them to work at an age when they were not fit for it. We wore not to defend that elate of thing., but if we were to justify our title to be ci•ilimel nation we moot lay onr see to the rout of the tree, introduce some clement of fair dealing into oar relations with all chums of the population, and one that there was no undue poverty in any home when people were willing to do their fair Mare of work. It nos much too deep a problem even to attempt to discuss that night. Ile was quite cure until they • ndeavoured to anise eta CAf WO level of aubeiratence below which no willing worker could fall, they could not claim to do their doty to thei!soffering fellow creatures. " '</p>
        <p>Mr. Pi.asTi., chairman of the School Attendance Committee, thanked Mr. Upson fur having raised sock an important point. If the members of that anthdrity had studied the monthly report which emplaced before thorn that question would have been raised long ago. The moat important matter he could reply lo and lay the blame in lho proper quarter was in respect to medical inspeation. The system in operation with the : 11. aI th Com re t tee,hy which children had been exempted holecale from school, load altered considerably since the time of which Mr. Upeon sera speaking. The children exempted on account of infections diseases accounted torn fulling off of 530 In the average attend- ance last year, and that took a great deal from the point raised by Mr. Upson. Reference had hoes made auto c hather there was unison in the action of the committee. Ile complained that !hero was a lack of syctematic action in rqgard to granting exemption.. The applications were dealt with by mar:alters at weekly rota Testier., and on come occasion.. expmp• ' "</p>
