      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CANADA'S GREAT WHEAT HARVEST.</head>
        <p>CANADA'S GREAT WHEAT HARVEST.</p>
        <p>Owing to the largely increased areas of land takea up by new settlers and the favourable season it Is believed the h t of wheat in Western Canada will he more than double that of last year. Over 200,000 freight cant will be required to carry to the elevatora the 200.0011,000 hurdlers of wheat expected. The Canadian Government officials at Ottawa state that harvesters who go over from Great Britain will be looked after. Mr. J. Bruce Walker, Government Com- missioner of I rnmigrarion at Winnipeg says that 40,000 extra harvest Minis will be required at wage. from 10, to 12s. bd. per day with board and lodging, to gather in this enormous crop. A large number of °reunions will leave Quebec, Montreal and Toronto to convey harvesters to the West at reelooed eaten in Attalla. The aortal Canadian Paciao barvester'e rates from Quebec (the landing port) to Winnipeg are ten dollars (about 410 Is. 34) single ticket, and eighteen dollars return (about £3 155.) hut to obtain this return rate a imitication'certincste from the farmer hr whom the applicant has worked meet be produced. The steamer faro from Great Britain ranges from Lit upwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Perfection Band at Bradford</head>
        <p>Perfection Band at Bradford</p>
        <p>The annual brass band content for the Lord Masbate Cup took place at Bradford on Saturday. The tent piece ass " Gems of Schubert." Mr. F. W. Frayling, haudataater of the Scots Grey., acted as ! judge. Ilia award waa as follow.: —I, Cleckheaton Victoria, S; points ; 2,Foden's Motor Waggon Works, ; 3, flawthnrpe Victotia, Si ; I, Ferfectton Soap Works, 17; i, Sowerby Bridge, 52.</p>
        <p>PRINCE HENRY'S MOTOR TOUR. VISIT TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Our picture shows Prince Henry in shirt sleeves "tuning up" his car.</p>
        <p>„ . Prince Henry of Prussia and the sixty English and German motorists who have been taking part in the reliability tour through England and Scotland passed through Warrington on Monday on their way from Windermere to Shrewsbury. Although it was only at very short notice that the final arrangements Came to the knowledge of the townspeople, preparations were speedily set on foot to accord the visitors a hearty greeting. Flags were hoisted over the Town Hall and other public buildings, and the main streets were gay with Union Jacks and lines of streamers. Large crowds assembled to await the arrival of the motorists who, before coming to Warrington, had lunched at Knowsley Village Hall. The first car appeared at Market Gate shortly before half-past twelve and until three o'clock there was a more or less continuous procession. Prince Henry, who was driving his own car, was recognized by many of the spectators and he responded to a hearty cheer at Warrington Bridge with a smile and a salute. All along the route the reception of the visitors was most cordial. At Stockton Heath a pleasing little ceremony was witnessed. When the cars reached the swing bridge they were "hold up' owing to the gates being closed. Alderman Bolton, who lives in the vicinity, had arranged a beautiful bouquet of rare orchids for presentation to Prince Henry. In asking permission to present it, Alderman Bolton mentioned that he had had the honour of presenting flowers to the late Queen Victoria, Queen Alexandra, and Queen Mary. Prince Henry giving the asked for permission, Mrs. Bolton stepped forward and handed the bouquet to the Prince, who seemed delighted to receive it. A large crowd witnesssed the little ceremony and raised loud cheers. The Prince remained in conversation with Alderman Bolton for several minutes and also shook hands with a lady who was waving a German flag. The motor tour was brought to an end on Wednesday with the run from Cheltenham to London, where all those who had started save three arrived within two hours. The first in was Prince Henry himself,who drove up to the Wolseley Garage, Westminster, two minutes and a half before half past six. There were about a couple of hundred people present to welcome the motorists in, and almost as much amusement was caused by the dust-begrimed and travel-stained appearance of the tourists as by the mascots, ranging from cats and monkeys to large-sized dolls, which they carried on their cars. Prince Henry, however, append spick and span.</p>
