        <p>The CIMUM. said their chief baroness on the agenda, was to make arrangements for the forthcoming °peewit feativadin pronoun years everything had proved satisfactoty and the work bed been carried teroug`t without a hitch. 'llia year however they were going to hold it on a Sunday nail that might mke tiajontion front atone go mem The concert would be hold in the afternoon and kiroilil nit interfere whatever with any of the aerric e in the churches. A great deal of good had been done in Stockport and other places where similar festivals had been hell and the ininieters of the various denoroin.tione laid taken part in them, lie also announced that Mr. Ilatupson, Mr. Daintith and lie terd approached Messrs. Peter Walker an) be is with veepect to the loan of a stand as in the pnvious yet,' tie mid they were kindly received by Mr. Hutton who consented to put them in the way if getting whatever they anted for. They Mao visited ("swain Ctoefleld who had consented to the limn of chairs, forms, Ao. (Applause.) Progress was 131-. v terra made and he hoped that they would haven substantial balance on the right side. (Applause). Mr. llontiocks in submitting a auggeation to the client that lie thought ahopkeepere, tradesman and those who dill not subscribe to the infirmary as did the roen in the worke, aleittld be approached and incited to contribute to the funda. lie aktim•ted that if they cent! ibuted in the lame ratio as the workingmen an edditinnal income of something like V2HI or zno would he the result._ _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that in 00000 d to the Insurance Bill it had been said that it would interfere with tie I ustituLion. Be thought that if the Government could see its way to make. direct grant to the Infirmary, say I of Ga. a loud of the pop dation it would amount to £l,BOO and a corresponding also, in respect of the aurroundiug diLtriets which took an interest in the Infirmary, would bring a hantlimue total and save a grant deal of trouble. Mr. MAG.,. in supporting Mr. Ilwrocks' suggestion sail be synipathised with the workingmen wise were called upon to do their ehsre while there wore others left untouched. Ile once i 1031,1 of • r theme in which small shopkeepers and small einem nere mode to con. tribute. If they did not want to be troubled by calls on behalf of the Infirmary they were sold a red crows shield for ss. The shield was then bong up and pro. tented them from collectors for subscriptions and the amonnt was handed over to the lostitution. Its would support any scheme that would help forward the work of the Institution. It wrie decided to fully investigate the matter •fter the psstival. .</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. O 1601106 WALKeR seconded I y Mr. DA INTITII and supported by Mr. SHAW a hearty I vote of thanks was passed to Captain Crosfield for his ' generosity in allowing them the use of the ground., Mr. Slew stating that it could not but have • most beneficial effect upon the work of the committee, composed es it was of all class A and ahades of opinion. Captain CRO,rIKI.I, in reply, elated that he could not do too much for such a valuable natiae,and he would be only too pleased, if it stimulated them to come there, to we them again in the near future. (Applsuee.)</p>
