        <p>Social and Presentat,on</p>
        <p>On Friday evening lest a social and smoking conoert we. held at the Albion Hotel, Rattershydane, by the employee. at the Cheshire Lines Railway Engineering Department to recognise the long Bernice of foreman S. Swindells, who has retirel after over 36 years' service. There ware about 80 present and the chair was taken by Mr. George Walker, works manager, who in his opening remarks mild he was sure each gal.-- legs were productive of good fellowship and kindly feelings and he regretted that whilst nearly every works in town had its anneal trip or social gathering. they, with far greater opporttraities and privileges as Railway somanta, never availed ourselves of them collectively. lie regretted the particular circumstance which had ceused thin meeting, of which more would be said later on an there were present many friends who desired to show their appreciation and at the same time contriuute to the evening's enjoyment by song and story. A very interesting concert followml. A male voice onartette : Messrs tiophton, Dolton, I ammo, and Ramos rendered with great baste and feeling "Shemrock," "Soldier's farewell," "little Tommy, went a fishing. Other items given included : Thom, "The Children's flown" and - r 3 robs, song" by Mr I Taylor of Moneta-sten "Th. ltattle Ern" Messrs Sephtun and Dutton ; blowy " file Hannan; " England 1.1 Ireland " Messrs Ilighena ; "Alice where art thou" and" Hood Company " Mr Sephtan ; " Love me and the world is mine," W who also recited inn very telling manner "Christmas Night in a Workhouse." The bunion.,n port of the programme wee in the • .ry ramble bands of Mr. Ono. Bennett whose rendering 414 " The Yorkshirrimana Motto "" By the saa " " filauicat sketches ' D in't take me home "nun intecil a rich tree.. Mr. A. Trow officiated as accompanist and eoluist in his used able manner. . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>During tbn thn chairman on loMalf employees in Lb e Noginecring and Stoma Departmnut, premtnind Mr. S. Swindells twilit a silk noll•rells, il • at mounted and suitably inscribed, tocether with a purse ill gold. In doing so be oomplimentod Mr. Swindells</p>
        <p>on having attained the •ge lierit In good health. At the nod of 36j years service he had gained thormghly the sppr .ciaturo of oil sh had hid dealings with hon. Ile was nu exrimplo to younger mon of the soccess in life IA it ineori %My follow r piti rut industry. Ii WWI alsays rolinble and trustworthy, ...Wyk./ alike the intercity of his employer oat of the Men, in'ereitel In the detoils of hi, work, id whrch be woe grmtly Assisted by on excellent on I unfailing memory. They were all glod to know that he left the mmiee whil.t In goo/ booth and phyomally lit to enjoy many year. of o quiet home life into which he carried with him the seteem, goo Ise and hest wishes of all sho had boon closely asdociarsd with him doting his lengthy Remind with the Cooshire Lints Itailwxy. timers. Briluelow, 11,1wleas, Kilduff, Millintno, XI relives, llngbes, Rushton and Irving spoke briefly, asaociating themselves with the sentiments exproned from the ' chair, sod bearing toatiniody to the very high charnels, of Mr. Swindells, who In reply, thanked the donore for their kind gifts, which had come upon biro non animist.. lIN bad tried to do his duly, and his revised woo in their generous apprecittion of Ms effort.. The usual vote trf thank/ were given to the artisten who hid so larcily contributed to the 0500008 of the meeting and WilelSO efforts were greatly //pm/willed by the compiny, tool to the CI Woman for bin aer • Mei. Tula concluded a very pleasint not memorable gotherirg.</p>
