        <head>CAMPAIGN OF FALSEHOOD.</head>
        <p>CAMPAIGN OF FALSEHOOD.</p>
        <p>Ir. Lloyd-Goorge and Luton Tory Palopikt. Mr. Lloyd-George in a letter to Mr. Cecil Menne- worth the ldheral candidate for the Luton Lao Tory leaflet en the Insurance Bill, which . hes been circulated during the caimesigo. " I cannot remember ever to hare neen AIICII a chaos of impudent mis-statements as adorns that sheet pnper,' VS. Me Chancellor ; and after imposing COMO of the Oft, or sidty oniednatementa the pamphlet cdotains, he •dds : " If this compaign of falsehood contion, I Moll feel lamed to ilepirt from the unwritttm role ehill bee unitally precluded Cibinet Ministers from •isitiog • conatitnency in which a by-election ir Meng fimght,and to come myself and p'ece the truth wiih regard to the Bill Wore the electors."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</head>
        <p>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket Notes. TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>'Ashton-under-Lyne v. War. ington. Tram 'cares Central Station 12 55. Warrington teem B Sikock, W Richaideon, jun., J Callon, S Partridge, J W Peamon, A T L °rear, H Hands, S Baxter, W Bennett, A Duckworth and Peale!.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Whitecrovs Recreation v.Sankey. Latchford Wesleyans v. Sankey Wire Mills. Warrinston Conservatives v. Walker's Brewery. Rylands Bros. v. Orford.</p>
        <p>Warrington had the somewhat rare satisfaction of tasting the sweets of victo:y on Saturday, tind the match with St ckport provided one cf the most exhilaiating displays seen at Arpley for some time. The bowling and fielding on the home side showed a welcome improvement and, thanks to the keenness in these departments, the visitors were all disuoersed icr 9 I, the highest individasl figure (17) being knceked up by R. Lester and S. R. R. Stockdale. The War- rington professional was the chief destructive agent for, in addition to capturing Ire wickets, he brought elf a magnificent one-handed catch in the slips. for which he won loudly cheered. S. Partridge maintsined a fine length, and secured 3 wickets for 29 runs, while W. Bennett accounted for two wickets at the very low cost of two runs.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest centred in the appeerance in the Warrington ranks of G. Woodcockothe young Boteler Grammar School batsman who made the record score of 143 not out against Buxton College last week. irs inclusion in the senior eleven was amply justified for be made 23 by confident and accurate hitting. Great things may be expected of him m the future. The Warrington batsmen in general did themselves credit and the innings had realized 120, with a couple of wickets in hand. H. Hands, another extremely promising young player, distinguished himself with a faultleesly compiled 2ri. A. T. L. Gresr gave a capital forcing display and carried out his bat with 22 to his account.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd made a splendid effort at Stockport, but had to he content with a draw. Batting first the visitore amassed 231, no fewer than 85 runs being put on for the first wick. t. This was the outcome of a fine partnership between S. Baxter and I'. Dixon, their respective totals numbering 63 and 45. R. Lee also batted with conspicuous success, but unfortunately bad to retire hurt when his news reached 10. Stock- port were not to be caught napping, and when time -was called they had put together 194 for 4 wickets. Hayes batted with confidence and freedom, and made a brilliant 85.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League, Latchford Wesleyann made no mistake about their victory over Ityandn Drys., the majority being 49. J. Knight was the most successful batsman, him innings realizing 20.</p>
        <p>There was an exciting struggle between the two local brewery team% Walkers' triumphing by a margin of 4. It was a rare sporting game and the sinners buy deterved their success.</p>
        <p>Warrington Conservatives added another to their list of victories, Orford being vangoished to the extent of 22. The scoring on each side was very low. Ores caldared seven wickets for two runs.</p>
        <p>Sankey retain their leaderi.hip of the League, their encounter with Smkey Wire Mills giving them another vict iry by 14 runs. Fosier was the top scorer for the winners with 27, and W. Bennett beaded the Wire Mills nom list with 13. Millington was Sankey's most eff,ctive bowler, capturing six svickets for 15 rune. Blade° and Craddock each took dour wicket, for the Wire Mills.</p>
        <p>Lymm entertained Brooklands, the outcome being a draw. The home eleven complied 104. B Bell (tI3 and W Harrison (47 not out) eharing the batting honours. Brooklands responded with 115 for 5 wickets, E %Tattoo knocking up 46.</p>
        <p>Oughtrinzton Perk were victorious at the expense of Didsbuty "dad, the best bitting display being given by F Champ (31).</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission had not much difficulty in beatiog Bank-street. Mission, the margin totalling 39. II KeBllllo made 24 and W Winstantley 21 for the winners, and G. Carless was responsible for 24 for Bankstreet.</p>
        <p>Time prevented Winwick Asylum from recording a fine win on 13,turday. Manchester Linotype were the visitors, and they batted first, knocking up a total of 171. With an how and a half to play, Winerick batted merrily, putting up a score of 170 for the loss of four -wickets. Dr. Rogers, the home captain, had bard lines. Ile time in to face the last °an-, with three runs wanted to win, and after scoring a single, was dismissed by a fine catch, just as the church clock was striking wren. The chief scorers were C Cunningham, Winwick, 53, Dr Brunton, Winwick, 46, Kenyon, Unotype, 31. Floyd touk fire wickets for 33 runs.</p>
        <p>The following is the League table up to date : PM. Won Lost Den. Ft.. Sankey ll 8 2 1 ... 17 W'g'Cu Cooser 9 8 . 1 0 .16 Walkers armory Whitecross Rao Latchford War. lO . 4 ... 4 ... 2 ... 10 Ryland' Bros Sankey Wire Kills 11 ... 3 ... 6 ... 2 ... 8 Orford Wilderepl Brewery 10 ... 2 ... 8 ... 0 ... 4</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptiste triumphed over Monks Bell after a most exciting finish, oolr three runs separating the teams. J. Webb i MoLk a Hall) distinguished himself with a particularly fine display for 41 and It. Williams knocked up 31. For Latchford the top scorer was A. Lee (28).</p>
        <p>Latch/ ,rd Baptist 2nd had a ridiculously easy task at Faroworth, where they disruimed the home eleven for 28 and then put together 123. H. Wright carried out his bat with 38 to hid credit. Carman and Ilaybury booted splendidly for Latchford, their respective anall see being five for 12 and five fdr 11.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath tried conclusions with an Orford eleven and won by 16 runs. Strange to say the top scorer on the winning side was' Mr. Extras " with 23. A. Hughes batted well for the losers, making 29. -- G rappenhall 204 had to bow the knee to Altrincham Wealeyans 2nd who secured a majority of 29. For the winners Waterhouse took six wickets for 28 runs -and S. Worrell four wickets for 12 runs.</p>
        <p>analog Notes. WARRINGTON WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION. DRAW FOR THE FOURTH ROUND.</p>
        <p>To-day the fourth round in connection with the Warrington Work. Bowling Association will be played on the green attached to the Horse and Jockey Inc. Armitage avd Rigby's, 30 Lonigford No 2, 70 Whitecross No 1, For v Corporation No 2, 70 Oiford Tannery, 25 v Fennel-street Tannery, 70 _ _</p>
        <p>•.L Castle Rubber, 70 v Guardian Office, 7,./. Referees: J Willett mad G Ball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>LAST SATURDAY'S GAMES.</head>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S GAMES.</p>
        <p>NO SUPPRISES.</p>
        <p>The third rated wan concluded last Satutday in tl.e present* of a large number of interested spectators. They. were no surprise results, but there was 1100111) good bowling, the handicaps as a whole being about the same number.</p>
        <p>GREENINCS NO 1. V. ARMITAGE AND .RIGHT.</p>
        <p>Front the start it was evident that both these teams "Me out to win and though the general opinion was in favour of Armitage and Rigby, Greetting's men fancied their chance. A handicap of thirty on each side placed them on a common level at the commence- ment of the game and corsequently this match was of paramount interest. The players pat in their all and one can easily imagine the excitement that prevailed towards the dose when it is mentioned that Armitage's only pared through by 4 points. Quite the most exciting game, because upon its result no much depended, was that between W. Roston (Armitage.) and R. Edgington. Before this game commenced Armitages were leading by one point, a circutosmuce which inspired confidence into the team. Both men had been found true many times before and all each side could hope for wan for the hest. They started fairly well —a give and take business—and pveled at 4 when Edgington made a break of 3 to his great satisfaction. Then Roston put on a bit of a splint and equalled at It and continuing his rod form ran home victor by 3 pointp. That It Clarke (Grvenioga) should succumb by 11 p ints to S. Riley was provo- cative of seine surmise. crnsidered o.eir</p>
        <p>Photo, L.</p>
        <p>• • or 4 = # tt w 11Pl: 4t, lo r 640 40 4 , .</p>
