      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS AT BOWLS.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS AT BOWLS.</p>
        <p>The National Independent Order of Oddfellows (Loyal Garibaldi Lodge, No. 484), Warrington District, decided their annual bowling main on Saturday, the venue being the sward attached to the Brooklands' Hotel. There was a record entry,no fewer than 80 members answering to their names, and some very interesting games were played. On the whole the handicaps worked out satisfactorily and the committee are to be complimented on their efforts in this direction. The final provided a keen contest between Bros. J. Hands and J. Bramball, the former winning after an exciting finish by 13-12, and thus qualifying to hold the Greenall Challenge Shield for 12 months. There was a large number of other minor prizes. The four prize winners were :—Bros. Hands, J. Bramhall, J Wilkinson and W. Edwards. Our photo shows the winner sat on left of Shield and the runner-up on the right and last year's winner, Bro. Chadwick, sat behind the Shield.</p>
        <p>man quite safe, for he is a bowler of no mean merit as will be gathered when I recall the fact that two or , three years ago he won the Association's Individual Merit Cmpetition. He started well, at ihreedoubling his opponent, but Riley with a worthy effort made a break of i, more than overhauling Clarke by 2. De vteadily increased the distance, Claike hardly ever having a look in, as it were.</p>
        <p>LOCKEII•9 NO, 2 v, OU/RDIAN</p>
        <p>..... I offer my hearty congratulations to my Con- temporary, who In:ruitco i f have m,. h ave exceeded my .it forget t in the second round, but tbey came out successful as a result of skill, and not by mere chance. And on Saturday last they accomplished a similar feat, Locker'. No. 2 with a like handicap of 70 points, being the victims by 21 chalke. Several of the "Guardian men were in fine trim, n. tably W. Caldwell, J. Wood- ward and S. Thorley, who adminis'ered a sound hiding metaphorically speaking, to A. Tinsley, W. 11. Llewellyn and T. Callon respectively. Between them they obtained only 14 points. Encugli !</p>
        <p>PAPULE. RUBBER V. ST. ItEll5ll CABLE.</p>
        <p>The sp:rit of keen competition betwetn rival firms cannot be thrown aside even on a Saturday afternoon, as WU amply evident in the combat between Castle Rubber Co. and St. Helens Co. The former started with a 70 handicap and the latter being 20 points behind. The teams were of about equal strength for the Cable Co. only diminished the difference by seven chalks. One of the most peculiar games was played between G. Parton (Castle Rubber) and W.Bradbury, the opposing captain. The former obtained 6 points before his opponent mold record a single, but undeterred the captain played finely and was able to peel at 7. Later Ina figures stood at 14 with Dal ton at 11, but with a commendable effort the latter made a break of 4, thus defeatiog Bradbury by 1 point.</p>
        <p>WINE NOPERY V. VENIVEL-PTREET TANNF.RY.</p>
        <p>The Tannery Co. had a great *dr-ants-Re at the start thek handicap being 70 and the Ropery's 30. Though they were by no means as good a team as their opponents they played some excellent games, but at the conclusion it was found that thee bad only won by 15 chalks. The Popery team polled the magnetic number on no less than eight cceasions. one of the most delightful drubbings being administered to J. Boyle, sea., by J. Lingard who won by 13 points.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING ASSOCIATION: _</p>
        <p>The Council have made an excellent choice in selecting the King and Queen Green to play their animal Individual Championship. The event will take place on Saturdays, August 5, 12, 19 sod 26. There will be sixteen prize. tut up for competition and the entrance fee is lc 61. Competitors will be supplied with a ticket which may be bad from the secretaries to the clubs affiliated to he 26(0Cial inn which will permit free admission to the green on day of play. Considering what a success the Individual was last year, the Council are anticipating to go one better this anion.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY INDIVIDUAL MERIT COMPETITION. - VETERAN BALL WINS THE FINAL.</p>
        <p>...„.... Last Saturday was an ideal day for the concluding stages of the South-Western section of the Lancashire County Individual Merit Cornpetiti, n. The Cemetery !Green waa in splendid condi.ion, fulfilling the antic,- pations of thoee who chose this sward. I have never seen such excellent bowling on this green before, and that is saying a good deal. The exhibition in the final between George Ball, sear., and Tom Bolton was a fine display of b wling dexterity, and it was worth while walking a mile or two to see the game proceed. The fluctuations in this game created • great amount of excitement, as will be gathered from the appended score card :- 1</p>
        <p>-6:i3;11, 1,3, 5,6, 6,6, 6,6, 6,8, 10, 11, 11, 11, it, 11, 11, 12, 13, 13, 13, 13, 15, 15, 16, 17, 18,19,20,21. T. Bolton, 0,0, 0,0, 1,3, 5,6, 8,8, 8, 8. 9, 10, 12, 13, 15, 15, 15, 16, 17, 18. 18, 19, 19, 19, 19, 10, 19. In a moment or two I vrill give a description of this most interesting game, thinking it better to give the games in their consecutive order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ALP fI,COTT V. JOE (HEAVE.</head>
        <p>ALP fI,COTT V. JOE (HEAVE.</p>
        <p>SECO:IU</p>
        <p>ROUND</p>
        <p>This was a run-away game for Scott op to the oth</p>
        <p>end, the crone card recording 10-2 in Lis favour. Oh are then realised li, preition and wiped cff • little of hie deficiency by obtaining G good chalks. Then Scott got going again and be looked quite an easy witner up to 19-9. but a member of the Weave family Ins never been known in he daunted even i when n such an apparently hopeless position, and Joe, rallying, found a round vg mark and merle • break of it, which gave him an advantage of 1. However, Scott was fortunate to gain the nece•earry couple and won a bard game by the narrow margin of one point.</p>
        <p>DEIII ANI, V. TOM 1tAR21.10,1.</p>
        <p>There two provided come good sport in the third round. It was a capital performance of Sunderland to beat the Cemetery captain by no lees than 7 chalk and he must have felt elated on Saturday to find himself among the envied few who will take part in the final stages of the competition pr per at Manchester where tlte ficalists, semi-finalists and the bowler with the highest luting wore in the fourth round in the district competitors will wrestle for honour. Sunderland was a fancied candidate for the fiord as soon es he had soundly di ibbled Humphries by 12 chalks in the fimith round.</p>
        <p>/VIC 411.ElVE V. TOM 7101.T0N.</p>
        <p>B ith players were in fine fettle as was evidenced by their keen work in the third round. Again Bolton pulled an excellent game out of the fire, just as he did the previous week. 178 were the pleasant figures against him, but playing consistently he made the score 19 all at the 39th end. Then Gleans obtained a further paint but not pitying up to his usual standard allowed Balton to register the neces- sary two chalks which made him winner by 21-20.</p>
        <p>Tog EIAISON Y. ALF. s,,rn</p>
        <p>Tlie fourth ;end game was an in and out affair They were constantly pee'ing and Ellison suffered defrat by the odd chalk. Still, by his reverse of 21- 20 he is allowed to take part in to-day's championship at Manchester, through obtaining the highest defeated !score in the fourth round. flunk forward tx Ellison gaining h• swore is hie pest experience assures rue thst the George Et tot Green. Manchester, will suit him down to the ground, as folks any.</p>
        <p>L SEA , V. 1...801T.:</p>
        <p>Bolton won the toss and 'opened towards the town end,'llall winning the first end and making a fine run of six before Bolton opened his account with • single. Bolton getting on his favourite mark caught his opponent at the oight'i end. Later • break renders(' 7 cha'ks. There wa, great applause wben Bolton defeated Bairn firat wood which lay about 8 inches of. Ball came again land ran the block to his first wood and made twa anaitht much excitement. Bolton p'aying • fine specimen wood, added • loace and passed Ball by one chalk l-11. He accumulated six chalks G. BALL, SES, before Ball got possession of the block, the latter being 3 chalks in the minority. Tom Bolton so eared to be gaining any amount of c3nfidence and playing • wool about 3 inches off, Ball hat a alMe of luck when he gl.ded on Bolton's first wool and put him first. It was really a fine game, only two chalks separating the players at the conclusion.</p>
        <p>V 'V</p>
