      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN CYCLING GLOB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN CYCLING GLOB.</p>
        <p>July 15th, lore our Cheater. 'Ma it another district very little known to Wartington cyclists. The group of little hamlets includieg Elton, llapeford, Ince, Thornton-le-lloors, and Wimbolds Trafford is contained in a corner of Clawhire that forms a col de one, with the Mersey and the Canal on the North the River Gamy on the West, and Frodslum Marshes on the East.</p>
        <p>--91-.e-lett members, through the kindnese of the vicar Hev.F.G.Slater,and at the ovitation of If re.Fark Yates of lace Hall, spent a delightful evening roaming over the park, and through the greenhouses and grounds. When cactuses and heliotrope can be trusted out of doors all the 'warner, you can have some idea of the rest of the garden, with its profane vegetation and blaze of flowers.</p>
        <p>, -7171-te—vl;r—iinet us at the church and gates most interesting history of the building end its monuments. He pointed out the graveelone of the eleven men who were killed in the disaster during the cutting of the Ship Canal, and that of the Id village blacksmith named Bell whose epitaph is : " My sledge and hammer lies declin'd, kly bellows too have out their wind, My tire's estilict, my forge decayed And in the duet my vise is lay'd My Cole is spent, my Iron is gone, The nail is driv'n, my work is done."</p>
        <p>A most beautirol gantlet wee witoeseed o'er the eetuary, which is here three miles across. sad Birkett- . bead ten miles away could be clearly seen.</p>
      </div>
