      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SAIIIRDAY, JULY 22 BIL</p>
        <p>What JOHN BRIORr THE PEERS' wrote to a corres- SURRENDER. pondent in 1869 is recalled in a very timely way in connection with the momentous event which took place on Thursday."lt is possible," wrote the great orator of a former day, " that a good many people may ask what is the special value of a Constitution which gives a majority of 100 in one House for a given policy, and a majority of 100 in another House against it." It may be asked, also, how is it that while the Crown through its Ministers in the House of Commons is found in harmony with the nation the Lords are generally in direct opposition to it. "It would be well," added Mr. BRIGHT "if the Peers could bring them- selves in line with the opinions and neces- sities of our day." "In harmony with the nation," he observed, "they may go on for a long time ; but throwing themselves athwart its course they may meet with accidents not pleasant for (hem to think of." The Peers are no doubt entitled to meet this with the reply that they have gone on with out much serious discomfort for more than forty years since JOHN BRIGHT warned them of the consequences of opposing the will of the people. They may further proceed to surmise that they may contrive to go on for forty or even four hundred years more. Many of them entertain this view of the case, no doubt. But, to use an American form of speech which appears to have been lately adopted into the English language,they are now " up against" a situation which has never before presented itself. Up to now there has not been a Government which has been twice returned with a majority for the express purpose,first of all, of bringing the Lords into line with the opinions and necessities of the day. We have spoken above of an event which will make Thursday memorable in our Parlia- mentary history, but as a matter of fact the really momentous decision was arrived at in private two days since. The "wild Peers" have proved to be amenable to persuasion. Lord HALSBURY threatened to vote against the third reading of the I arliament Bill, despite the fact that he voted (or the second reading. It goes without saying that Lord WILLOUGHBY DE BROKE and those for whom he speaks were in the same frame of mind. These noble lords were, if Lord LANSDOWNE should decline to lead them into a dangerou impasse, determined to beat up as many Peers as would out-vote the Liberal Lords, and throw out with every circumstance of heightened melodrama a Bill which Lord LANSDOWNE and his more tractable sup- porters have been for some time past indus- triously engaged in transforming into a I measure that would make the House of Lords the supreme power in the State. But the projected revolt has not taken place. The backwoodsmen remained in the backwoods; the Bill as amended received its third reading : the more intractable Peers did not pay Lord LANSDOWNE the compliment of obeying him, they showed discretion in refraining from forcing a division on the Parliament Bill. And this, we may add, is not all that has</p>
        <p>been settled. When the third reading was passed the House of Lords had done its worst and its last to the Parliament Bill. There will from to-day be plain sailing. The Bill as amended will, of course, be sent back to the House of Lords for reconsideration. It will then be seen that there is no sufficient reason for standing by the amendments. The Bill will be the subject of many verbal assaults. But it will pass through its ordeal without any injuries that call for surgical treatment. The Lords will protest, wrap their togas about them, and pass a measure concerning which, if we may ve..ture on a prediction, they will soon be asking why it was considered to be "the end of all things." In short, the crisis is over. The people's battle has been won.</p>
        <p>It will be a distinct: SCHOOL surprise to many people' ATTENDANCE. to learn that, notwith- standing the steady increase in Warrington's population, the growth of the Education rate, the extension of school accommodation, etc., the attendance at the schools is proportionately less than it was nine years ago. Mr. John Upson drew attention to the fact at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday, and gave chapter and verse for his strictures on the present situation. According to his calcula- tions, the average attendance nine years ago was 11,627, and to-day it is only about 300 more, though the population has increased by 7,000, and the number of children of school age must have increased by at least 1100 since 1902. During the same period 2,500 additional places have been found in the schools, thus showing that while the authorities have carefully pro- vided for the growth in the numbers of scholars they have not seen that the children themselves were gathered in to the schools. Apart from the educational, social and moral loss to the community caused by this serious leakage in attendance, there is a heavy financial loss involved. Each of those 1400 children who should be attending school may be reckoned as worth 355. per year in grants, which totals up to a loss in educational revenue of £l,4oo—equal to a 14d. rate. There are probably many causes contributing to this result—exemptions for sickness, for child-labour, and possibly some leniency by the magistrates in dealing with refractory parents. Whatever the causes, the facts remain, and Mr. Upson deserves the thanks of the community for drawing attention to a state of things which merits the closest investigation. We are glad to note that a special committee was suggested on Monday, and will probably be appointed at an tarly date. Education is becoming so costly that it is essential we should get value for our troney. At present both the children and ithe ratepayers appear to be losers.</p>
        <p>Substantial progress is A DIFFICULT being made with the</p>
        <p>POSITION. National Insurance Bill, but it is encountering a serious difficulty. The Tories have shrunk from repeating the mistake which they made over the Old Age Pensions Bill. They are not going to put themselves in direct opposi- tion to a great measure of amelioration. But they are repeating the manceuvres which they practised when they found that it was useless to oppose direct resistance to the Old Age Pensions Bill. It is, they think, a very clever move to propose such extensions of the benefits embodied in the Bill as would make the Bill financially impossible. Then there arises a fine opportunity for denouncing on the plat- form the niggardliness of a Government which showed much less sympathy for the masses than the Tory Party. It is not, of course, the whole Tory Party which is chargeable with these mean tactics, and it is not suggested that all those who want to increase the benefits under the Bill beyond the limit of what is actuarially sound are really enemies to the Bill. But it is obvious that the Chancellor of the Exchequer, in having to resist plausible but impracticable proposals involving greater expenditure, is placed in an invidious position that deserves the sympathy of all true friends of social reform.</p>
        <p>On their return from TO LET OR NOT Germany several of the TO LET? "Tariff Trippers" boldly reported that there were no shops, warehouses, or dwell- ings to let in Berlin. Either something serious has happened in Berlin since they were there, or (what is much more likely) the trippers were very blind, for the Berlin correspondent of the "Standard." a Tariff Reform organ, writing on July 16th, says : "Over 65,000 flats are at present standing empty in Berlin and suburbs,according to the Annual Report of the House and Land Owners' Association which has just been issued. The number of these tenant- less dwellings in Berlin itself is 28,987, out of which approximately 21,000 consist of Ismail flats of two rooms and a kitchen. . .</p>
        <p>The thoughtless throwing open of ground for building purposes by the various Berlin com- munities has caused an artificial boom in the building trade for which there was no real ground. In spite of these conditions, how- ever, rents in Berlin are far from cheap, and from the tenant's point of view would hardly ' compare favourably with those of Greater London." Had the Tariffites taken the trouble to enquire they would have found that in Berlin alone there are published two newspapers devoted entirely to advertise- ments of premises to let. "There are none so blind as those who won't see, and none so deaf as those who won't hear."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WORKINCIAEN S INFIRMARY COMMITTEE.</head>
