        <p>If you nigh to eojoy o musical treet attend the festival in Rank Park on Sued3v, the 30th July, 1911, et 3.15 p.m. You will then be able to support the Warrington Infirmary as well. Remember the fact that it follta nearly Elto a week to keep your hospital going. The Warring'on Male Voice Choir and the Apollo Male Voice Choir, with Croklielde Rand, will render • choice selection of musim There will be no charge for mlmission to the P•rk,but there will he a collection at the gate, to chick all will be expected to contribute. Special arrangement• will he made for the accomm, &amp;tient of the audience.</p>
        <p>NEXT WINTER IN TIIE SUNNI' RIVIERA. Striking programmes bare already been published for west winter's smueemen.• Ia tbe differ. nt towns of the beautiful Ilivient. The Paris, Lyons and blediternmean Railway, with their habitual eeterprim, are &amp;Inutile( , for Incremed facilities for tourist, from England to the sunny South. Person. leasing for the Coronation Garber in India may break their stay for short Isere at Marseilles and run through to Canoes, Nice or Rome Carlo, either on the outward journey or returniag, means that the "amain UN South of France and Italy I will be of unusual tallith this year and the hotels are already preparing for a record influx of visitor*. The North of Freon. Railway has started new through servlonsto Launchers, matral Gernmay and Switser. lead. Travellers rave by this route one and • half hours from Lcadoe to Luxemburg, over all other routes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>N (YIL,a2,N.ILTiI?.]</head>
        <p>N (YIL,a2,N.ILTiI?.]</p>
        <p>_ - - -- —, The long-awaited sanction of the Board of Trade to the new Warrington Bridge Scheme has at last been received and the way should now be clear for a start being made with the work. But perhaps it would be rash to prophesy when those piles will be driven.</p>
        <p>Warrington is getting used to lightning visits from Royalty. There were two on Monday. Their Majesties the King and Queen, passed through Bank Quay station about one o'clock on their way from Wales to Edinburgh and, almost at the same time, Prince Henry of Prussia was motoring through the main streets en route for Shrewsbury.</p>
        <p>We have been asked what would have been said by the Tories if Mrs. Crosfield, as President of the Women's Liberal Associa- tion, had applied to the Education Committee for the help of the teachers in organising a political picnic. The question is beyond our answering, but we are sure the comments would have been pungent. It is not con- ceivable, however, that even Radical pre- sumption could have risen to such a height.</p>
        <p>• • There was once another Lord Willoughby de Broke who was a very singular character and had more peculiarities than any noble- man of his day. Coming once out of the I House of Peers, and not seeing his servant among those who were waiting at the door, he called out in a very loud voice: "Where can my fellow be?"—" Not in Europe. my lord," said Anthony Henley, who happened to be near him, "hot in Europe." •</p>
        <p>Messrs. Parker and Townroe have brought new ideas into the discussion of National Service. The burglar illustration has opened the way for quite an unhooked-for dis- quisition on what would be the right or wrong course under the circumstances. Poker persuasion finds no favour with Mr. Parker, and his justification of more peacable tactics is not only logical,but would doubtless command the approval of the burglar.</p>
        <p>At the Wesleyan Methodist Conference at Cardiff on Monday,resolutions on the temper- ance question were passed. The conference welcomed the promise of the Prime Minister to speedily introduce a Bill dealing with temperance reform, and were strongly of opinion that any such Bill should include such provisions as the entire closing of licensed houses on the Lord's day and the regulations and control of clubs where intoxicating drink is sold, such regulation to include the item that no alcoholic drink shall be sold when licensed houses are closed.</p>
        <p>As compared with a year ago, employment in Juneaccording to the "Board of Trade Labour Gazette," in nearly all the principal British industries showed an improvement from a labour point of view. There was, however, a considerable decline of employ- ment at blast furnaces and in the lace and jute trades. In th e 394 trade unions with a net membership of 758,666 making returns, 22,895 (or 3'o per cent.) were returned as unemployed at the end of June, 1911, com- pared with 2'5 per cent, at the end of May, 1911, and 31 per cent, at the end of June, 1910. Returns from firms employing 450,221 work people in the week ended June 17th, 1911, showed a decrease of 23 per cent. in the amount of wages paid compared with a month ago, and an increase of 28 per cent. compared with a year ago. With one excep- tion (South Wales Miners) all the changes in 11 wages reported as taking effect in June, were 1:</p>
        <p>* • • The first idea of" Punch," which this week celebrates its seventieth birthday, occurred to Henry Mayhew and Mark Lemon one night in June 1841, when they sat chatting together at the latter's house in Newcastle- street, Strand. Lemon drafted a prospectus straight away, and the first number of the Ijournal made its appearance on July 17th. llt was owned, at first in equal ,Ishares, by Landells, the engraver I Last, the printer, and Lemon and Mayhew who acted as joint editors. For a while the ' paper met with small success, and was only kept running by the money which Lemon made as a playwright. A change for the better, however, took place when the journal was purchased by Messrs. Bradbury and Evans. Mayhew retired, leaving Lemon sole editor, and from that day to this "Punch's" career has been one triumph. Lemon's salary as editor is said to have begun at 30s. a week. When he died twenty- nine years later, he was receiving £1,500 a year.</p>
        <p>• • Mr. Consul Leay, in his report for the Consular District of Boston, refers to the high cost of living. "The high cost of living" he says, "has continued to he a serious drawback,and there is a general feeling that there must be a complete re-adjustment of prices before permanent improvement can be expected in general business lines. It is true that towards the end of the year (1910) the wholesale prices of necessaries showed a tendency to fall, but as yet the reduction has not yet reached the consumer —largely because middlemen are endeavour- ing to maintain their old scale of profit as long as possible." Tariff Reformers who have declared that there is no Free Trade Ifeeling in America will be annoyed at Mr. ILeay s statement "in connection with Reciprocity it may be added that there is an increasing number who advocate, not merely closer trade relations with Canada, but absolute Free Trade throughout the entire North American Continent."</p>
        <p>One of the late Cady.Meux's mummies is that of Nes-Amsu, who was a priest of the gods Amu and Khonsu, and a personage of some importance. A papyrus was buried with him, and this gives an outline of what will probably happen to anyone interfering with his reverence's mummy. The rapyrus says that "If any person of any foreign country, whether he be black man or Ethiopian, or Syrian, carry away this writing, or if it be stolen by a thief, then whosoever does this no offering shall be presented to their souls, they shall never more breathe the air, no son or daughter shall arise from their seed, their name shall be remembered no longer upon earth, and most assuredly they shall never see the beams of the Disc (the Sun God)." Now, it is 2,200 brief summers since Nes-Atnsu died, and one might suppose that the curse was now wear- ing somewhat thin. But it seems not. This 'particular mummy was found by Mr. Walter Ingram, who was the younger brother of the founder of the " Illustrated London News." Mr. Ingram gave the mummy to Lady Meux, but why he did so is unknown. Perhaps he read the curse and hastened to pass on the responsibility, which was ungallant, especially to a lady. Moreover, it availed him nothing, for he was killed by an African elephant in the following year. Lady Meux died childless, and the baronetcy became extinct.</p>
        <p>The. Smith Brothers have both dis- tinguishedi themselves this week. "F.E." on Thursday once more took upon himself the role of adviser-in-chiet to the Conservative Party, and preached "no surrender" on the Veto Bill while, as the "Morning Leader" puts it, obviously fumbling with the bolts of 'the back-door. Since Mr. Smith's manifest, the door has been opened and the Peers are in full retreat. Mr. Harold, the member for Warrington, intervened in a Commons debate with one of those offensive personal remarks, which seem to be his idea of humour. It is a pity these two gentlemen do not model their behaviour on more modest lines.</p>
        <p>..... I • Steady trade and the grant of old-age pensions are responsible for the remarkable diminution in pauperism which is shown by the latest returns. The total pauperism in May was less than for nine years, the ratio per 1.000 inhabitants being 228 as against 226 the last time a rate as low as 22 percent. was reached in this particular month. During the past ten years the total pauperism has been from 221 per 1,000 inhabitants to 264 in the month of May. It is, however, the indoor pauperism which keeps these figures fairly constant, the ratio having varied as little as four per thousand during the last 28 years. There are actually fewer indoor paupers, but the decrease is com- paratively small, a matter of three thousand odd, as against last year, when the number was the highest recorded for thirty years or more.</p>
        <p>• • • Outdoor pauperism, however, shows a marked declension, the number in May, 1911 being 28,620, against 41,457 in May, 1910. Indeed, the return, which goes back to 1871, contains no record anything like so good as this, both in regard to number and ratio. Soon we shall know with some definLeness how much of this decrease is likely to be permanent. It is emently satisfactory to have got the indoor rate so low—it was 290 per 1,000 of the population in 1871, 101 in 1881, and 80 in 1891—and we believe that in five years' time there will be a similar reduction in indoor pauperism. Present legislation is sometimes derided, but unless we are greatly mistaken it will materially lessen the number of paupers, both indoor and outdoor.</p>
      </div>
