        <p>OH-Licence Holders Trip. The Warrington Off-Licence Holders' Association held their annual picnic on Thursday, when Workeop and the Dukeries were visited. The party numbered about lifty, and included the President (Mr. W. Meyers), the Vice-President (Mr. T.P. Throssell) and the Secretary (Mr. Ft Smith). Train was then to Worksop, where lunch was served, and afterwards the visitors drove through the beautiful rural scenery of the Dokeries, returning to Worksop tor dinner. BABY CARRIAGES, epeolsl value at 39,', Go-carte from S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., War- rington.—Advt. _ . .</p>
        <p>Presentations at the Secondary School. There was a pleasing htile ceremony at the "breaking up" concert in connection with the Warringion Seconda• y Soho(' yesterday aftern ton when Mies B. Dawson, a much esteemed member of the teaching staff, was the recipient of ventral handsome presents in recognition of her approaching marriage. A pair of silver candleeticks was presented by William Swains on behalf of the bursars; a silver marmalade stand by Bessie Jones in behalf of form 2 B ; and a picture by Edwin Hudson oa behalf of the student t-sehers.</p>
        <p>Liberal Mass Meeting. The handsomoly apppintad Liberal van will make iln first appearance at a meeting to be held at Bridge Foot on Saturday evening next under the auspice. of the Warrington Liberal Asswiation. Mr. C. E. Parker will preside and those trim are announced to address the meting ac, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. Fred Maddison (ex-111 P. f,r Burnley) and Mr. Ilarry New. Mr. Fred Maddisou is a favourite with Wartington audience• and is re,gni•ed as a capable and foiceful exponent of Liberal policy, He ia sure of a 'warm welcome on Saturdoy. - - -</p>
        <p>Warrington Lady Motorist Summoned. Mrs. Jimmie Aehten, of Warrington, was summoned at the Runcorn Pe ty Sessions on -Monday, far driving a motor car without being in posseesi,n elf • drivers licence. The lady was rrpreeetted by her Milhaud, who pleaded goilty to a technical ogfence. P.C.Savage stationed at Ila'ton, said that at 470 pin. on the 17th June he saw' a motor cycle with a sidecar attached cm. round Gamon's coiner into Main-street, Ila'ton. The car was bring diiven on badly that he immedi- ately asked dm lade, who was driving, if she had • licence. Her husband, who N. in the &amp;decor, replied that aim had not, and was only learning.—Mr. Ashton said the vehicle was driven so carefully round the corner that it had not sufficient speed to go up the lull on the other We. It stopped jug as the constable came on the scene. DefeLdent was fined the costa, 7s. Cd.</p>
        <p>Masks la the Park. 1 he Wart ington Borough Brass Bend 'viii play the following programme of music in the Park to-morrow: - Hymn, " Redemption," J. A. Greenwood; march, " Mtnerva," J. IL Carter; selecfon, " La Gipsy," Balls; air " Spanish Chant," H. Round ; selec- tion, "Siege of Itrclo lit'," Salle; "Hallelujah Chorus," Handel ; God sane the King. Conduct-r, Mr T Griffin. —On lhursday evening next Mr. T. J.Dasn's military band will play. 4the jollosing selection of music com- mencing at .11/ Conduct Mr, Arthur Dawn :- March, " The Peace-miker," Lotter ; patriotic fantasia, " Reminiscences of England," Godfrey; cornet solo, " Kathleen Mavourneen," Cr. uch, saloife, Mr. J. Appleton ; overture, " Stradella," Flotow ; valse, " The girl in the train," Fall ; selection. "The Gipsy Baron," Strauss; gland march, " Militaire," Thome; "God Save the King."—On Thursday even- ing, July 27th the Blue Coat schoA band will occupy the stand in the Park at 7.10.</p>
