        <p>School Children's Coronation Treat.</p>
        <p>Amid the beautiful surroandings of Beevaey Dark the scholars of St. Barnabas' day school hal their Corona- tion treat on Saturday. The arrangements were admirably carried out by the teachers and the day will long be remembered with Veasure by all who participated in the festivities. Asaernfiling at the school at two o'chnk, the children walked in proces- sion to the park. Here they were dismissed for play until 330 when tea was served ix a Dutch baro, kindly placed at the disposel of the party by Mr. Reel. At six o'clock the sports were held, flat races, sick races, skipping races, egg and spoon races, &amp;c., b ins entered into with great zest and enthusiasm. Coun- cillor Bellian who has taken a great interest in the treat, act rd as' starter." The visitors of the afternnon included Conocillor Robert., chairman of the Education Committee, and Mrs Roberts, the Rev .3 if U tiolliegerorth and Mrs Ilellingworth, Mrs Bellian, Mr and Mrs Westwell, Mr and Miss Evans, as representing Alderman James Evans, who was unavoi lably absent, Mena,o Wand T Proud. After the Sparta the sport. the prizes were distributed by ! Mrs. Hollingworth who was thanked on the proposi- tion of Mr. Westwell, seconded by Councillor Bellian. The thanks of the children to the teachers and managers were voiced by the Vicar. Mr. Turner, the head teacher of the mixed depatment, stiff that he hoped the children would always bear that day in miod, and would live to the honour of St. Itarnabas School, as being a " Church" and v. " Empire ' B.lhool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PEERS TO ACCEPT THE VETO.</head>
        <p>PEERS TO ACCEPT THE VETO.</p>
        <p>CONFERENCE WITH MR. BALFOUR</p>
        <p>Rum3ured Surrender.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning Mr. Balfour held at his house in Carlton House-terrace, a meet- ing of his supporters, which was largely attended. The meeting, which was strictly private, commented at 11.30. The conference lasted for upwards of a couple of hours. Among those present were Lord Lansdowne, Lord Halsbury, Lord Selborne, Lord Derby, the Marquis of Salis- bury, Lord Midleton, Sir Robert Finlay, Lord Balcarres (the Chief Conservative Whip), Mr. Boner Law, Sir Edward Carson, Mr. George Wyndham, Mr. Austen Chamberlain, and Mr. Walter Long.</p>
        <p>No statement has yet been made as to the nature of the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TO YIELD TO FORCE MAJEURE.</head>
        <p>TO YIELD TO FORCE MAJEURE.</p>
        <p>The Central News has the best reasons for stating that it was decided that Mr. Balour, in the speech which he is to deliver in the City of London on Tuesday, should announce that the Peers would yield to "force majeure "—that is to say, their lord- ships would pass the Parliament Bill rather than compel the Government to make use of the Royal prerogative for the creation of , Peers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>REVOLTING PEERS MEET AT LANSDOWNE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>REVOLTING PEERS MEET AT LANSDOWNE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A largely-attended meeting of Con- servative peers was held at Lansdowne House in the afternoon to consider the political situation. The attendance was confined to about thirty members of the Upper House, who had been specially invites to attend by Lord Lansdowne.</p>
        <p>Most of them were peers who had publicly or privately proclaimed their intention of rejecting the Parliament Bill rather than pass it without the Unionist amendments, and it is reported that these few insist on "fighting to the last ditch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>_ 1 – IRISH AFFAIR -s: NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. – –</head>
        <p>_ 1 - IRISH AFFAIR -s: NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. - -</p>
        <p>IRELAND AND STATE INSURANCE:</p>
        <p>In the waiting time for the triumph of the Government on the question of the Veto Bill, Irish minds have been considerably exercised on the Insurance scheme as laid down in Mr. Lloyd-George's Bill. After the chorus if praise and blessings which greeted Mr. Lloyd-George's introduction of the measure, it will have been noticed how the clouds have been since gathering over the head of this plucky statesman. The Labour Party would seem to be split into two strongly convicted groups on the Bill and Mr. Philip Snowden it will have been observed has recently done some very straight speaking to Mr. Ramsay MacDonald. What is the position of so far-reaching a measure in Ireland where all chminitic reforms meet with a highly developed political interest. As soon as the Bill was introduced into the House of Commons, Mr. John Redmond had a committee appointed to examine closely the provisions of the measure in so far as Ireland was concerned in its working. This Special Committee has held several meetings in Dublin and no pains have been spared to ascertain what effects the Bill would pro- duce under the special conditions of Irish life. They have been much helped in their deliberations by the criticisms which the Bill has encountered on the other side of the Irish Sea. And so long as that criticism is relevant and genuine it must be welcomed by all who desire the Irish members to have the soundest possible basis in pressing for the modification of the Bill so as to render it of any service in Ireland. A better illustration of the hopelessness of trying to legislate fro:n Westminster for an agri- cultural country like Ireland with her wholly peculiar and special needs, simultaneously and similarly with the many nsillioned industrial poople of Great Britain could not be found, and on this account we desire to Invite English and Irish attention to the report of th 3 special Committee of the Irish party. The recommendations of the Com- mittee are of a sweeping character. In the first place they stipulate for a separate administration of the Act in Ireland, distinct from top to bottom from that provided for England. The most cur- sory glance at the Bill will chew the obvious need for such a separate Irish administration and the committee is well advised we think in putting in the front rank of its recommendations a plea for a real Irish authority, not a mere Dubin Cattle Board, controlling a distinct and separate Irish Insurance Fund. They next recommend that, for the purposes of Ireland, the medical benefit should be eliminated from the Bill. To the English mind this will appear at least a curious recommendation. The explanation is that there is already in Ireland a system of medical relief for the poor which is efficient and, in the main, paid for out of the rates. Ireland being so conspicuously a healthy country, the greater part of the workers in Ireland will therefore be classed under Class lof Hazard, and on that ground a smaller contribution both by employers and employes because of the very much smaller risk, ought to suffice for the benefits provided by the Bill. To avoid any burden being placed upon the cottage and home industries it is asked that home workers be excluded from the MI, a principle already partly recognised in the operations of the Bill. Similarly exclusions from compuluory insurance are asked for in the case of sons and daughters working for their parents or other persons who are liable to maintain them, and also to such migratory labour as the small farmers and farm labourers who leave the West of Ireland each year to work in the harvest fields of England and Scotland. Thus it will be seen that considerable saving may possibly be effected if these recommendations are accepted and since the committee ask for a separate fund they rightly insist that these economies should be credited to the Irish Insurance Fund. Under a special clause Mr. Lloyd-George has wisely left himself a free hand to model the Irish application in accordance with Irish needs and it is earnestly to be hoped that he may be able to accept these and other recom- mendations which the Irish Party con- templated. We think the Party deserve great praise for the way in which a difficult matter has been dealt with. The criticism sent out from several sources, notably the meeting of the Catholic Bishops of Ireland, was opposed to the principle of the Bill. But the Party, notwithstanding that, having first examined the proposals, approve the basic principle of the measure and proceed to recommend changes which, if accepted, will mean such a reduction in the contribution both of employer and employed as will make the scheme not a burden on, but a blessing to the community of workers in Ireland. MR. REDMOND AND HOME RULE. Mr. Redmond's letter acknowledging the loyalty and public spirit of Mr. Muldoon in</p>
        <p>resigning his seat in Wicklow in favour of Captain Donelan contains a commentary m the political situation which must attract widespread attention. Naturally the tactics of the Factionists who drove Captain Donelan out of Cork on legal technicalities after a huge majority of the voters had returned him, come in for Mr. Redmond's review. According to some of the latter day prophets the Government are going to offer, and the Irish Party are about to accept a financial basis for Home Rule, wholly inadequate and involving the breakdown of self-government within a few years. Mr. Redmond makes short and sharp work of this foolish sugges- tion. But, unlike his critics, the Irish leader descends to no personal taunts and the public will contrast with the criticisms of the malcontents Mr. Redmor.d's directness and frankness. "We can and will" he writes "carry Home Rule during the lifetime of this Parliament if we are let alone" and the tone of his letter makes clear his confidence in the completion of the work to which he has consecrated the best years of his life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>STRIKE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>STRIKE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>L. and IL-W. Waggonmakers Come Out.</p>
        <p>Three hundred and fifty traggnnmakers struck yesterday morning at the L-ndon and North-Western railway works, Earlestown. The men ask for several concessions, including a higher rate for piecework. A deputation has interviewed the employers, and • mass meeting was held on Institute Green, but no sett!ement has yet been arrived at.</p>
        <p>SAD DEATH IN 'TIIE QUICKSAND. While bathing on Wednesday evening off Machsl- banish golf links, nets Campheltown, Robert Strschan, aged twenty-five, was caught in the quicksands in the presence of his fiancee, to whom he wan shortly to be married. The young lady, geeing his distress, frantioally called to some people near, but before •!WIWI. arrived Strachan was swallowed op by the breakers. A painful scene took place,tbe young lady refusing to leave the water's-edge for • time.</p>
        <p>FIT AT LATJHFORD LOCKS.</p>
        <p>inder the disguise of charity and large- 'leartedness has been very properly nipped in the bud. Lady Greensll and the Women's fariff Reform League may,without criticism, spend a few hundred superfluous pounds in giv:ng the children a treat, but they must not expect that their bona-fides will fail to be closely scrutinised when they attempt. however ingenuously, to take advantage of a rate-supported organisation. What party capital is derivable from such promiscuous charity they are entitled to. But they must have a care lest they damage the sacred cause of charity by tinging it too strongly with political colour.</p>
        <p>Photo, Tong,</p>
        <p>Quite a sensation was caused on the Ship Canal route yesterday by the passage to Manchester of the Glasgow steamship Argyllshire. the largest vessel which has yet navigate d the wat3rway. She is 525 feet long, 61 feet broad, and her registered tonnage is. 6,610 tons. Coming under the cantilever bridge at Latchford just before 9 a.m. yesterday, she appeared to almost fill the canal, and her huge cream-coloured funnel was only a few feet below the underside of the bridge. The Argyllshire was drawing 25 feet of water, and at the lo?.ks an even better idea of her bulk could be obtained. The big lock is 600 feet long and 65 feet wide, so that there was little to spare either way to get the new-corner through. Only one tug could enter the lock with her, and this at the bow. She took some time to work through, and as a matter of fact had taken six hours to reach Latchford from Eastham. Her cargo is general, and she carried a few passengers. No doubt a great deal of interest will be shown in her return voyage to Liverpool.. . .</p>
        <p>• Considerable difficulty was experienced in getting the vessel round the awkward bend at Runcorn Bridge. This is the worst turn in the length of the canal, and thousands of pounds have recently been spent on improving it. Three tugs attended the steamer, and men were stationed on the bank at either side to receive her ropes and "stem " her way if necessary. The steamer slowly and safely negotiated the turn. It took her an hour to get from Runcorn laybye round the bend—the distance being a mile.</p>
