      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM TRIPS AND TRICKS.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM TRIPS AND TRICKS.</p>
        <p>LADY GREENALL AND THE</p>
        <p>TEACHERS,</p>
        <p>The Scheme That Failed.</p>
        <p>I Quite a hubbub has been caused in certain circles lozally by developments in connection 1 with the proposed treat to six thousand Warrington school children by the Warring- ton Women's Tariff Reform League, of which Lady Greenall is the head, front and body— almost. It is no secret that correspondence has passed between her Ladyship on the one hand, and Councillor Henry Roberts (chairman of the Education Corn- mittee) on the other, relative to certain very essential details in connection with the trip ; and it is also pretty generally known that Mr. Roberts' reply, while couched in terms of extreme courtesy, expressed his inability to comply with Lady Greenall's :equests. Unfortunately for " Examiner " readers, the correspondence is deemed I confidential, though we believe that, in most respects, it is quite " fit for publication." t, There was a passing reference to the I matter at the meeting of the Education Com- Mittee on Monday, when the following intimation appeared on the minutes: At a meeting of the School Management Cora- mittee that day. tbe Chailman of the Education Committee having read carreepondence and reported his action in resa.d to request for assistance t i issue tickets in the schools fore proposed picnic, it was resolved that the setion of the Chairman of the Educatii,n</p>
        <p>C =litter) in this matter be approved. Whatever the Chairman has done has received the full approval of this Committee, but all the same it is a pity that some member of the General Committee did not ask for information on the matter, and for the correspondence to be read. Discretion is not always the better part of valour, and an inquisitive educationist might have done good public service by putting a few perti- nent questions, which would have been quite in order. _ _ _</p>
        <p>To supply the deficiency in information, we have made inquiries in authoritative quarters, and the facts are these, so far as we have been able to ascertain them without a peep at the aforesaid correspondence.</p>
        <p>- In the first place Lady Greenall wrote to Gouncillor Roberts in his personal capacity asking his assistance in getting an announcement of the forthcoming children's treat made by the teachers in the schools, and in providing facilities there for the distribution of tickets to scholars between the ages of 8 and 12. Councillor Roberts, in view of his position as Chairman of the Education Committee, consulted the Town Clerk as Law Clerk to the Education Authority and the Director of Education. I hey informed him that by a precedent created some years ago he had no option but to refuse such facilities. Lady Greenall then wrote to enquire what the precedent was, and Councillor Roberts replied that it was in the case of the late Mr. John Maginn, who organised a treat for the children of St. John's Ward. He also assured her Ladyship that he was anxious the children should not be deprived of a treat, but they could not use an organisation and a staff of officials, towards the maintenance of which all classes and shades of political and religious opinion in the town had to contribute, for promoting the interests of any political party Lady Greenall then wrote asking Coun- cillor Roberts to bring the matter before the full Committee. At the same time she approached the Teachers' Association through the secretary, Mr. Longshaw. The teachers were also anxious that the children should not be deprived of a treat, from what- ever source it emanated, and made a sug- gestion that they were prepared, with the approval of the chairman of the Education Committee, to distribute tickets. It the meantime Councillor Roberts had sent a personal and unofficial request to the head teachers to supply him with the names and addresses of children attending the various schools between the ages of 8 and 12. This information was supplied to Councillor Roberts, and by him forwarded to Lady Greenall.</p>
        <p>The suggestion of the Teachers' Associa- tion was, of course, vetoed in deference to the precedent of some years ago. An alterna- tive suggestion by them was identical with that already adopted and carried out by Councillor Roberts.</p>
        <p>..... These are, we believe, substantially the facts. They leave with Lady Greenell and her organisation the duty of acquainting personally those children whom they wish to treat to the delights of a Tariff ' Reform picnic. The idea of making use of between three and four hundred public Iservants to facilitate party political projects</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH LIBERALS</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH LIBERALS</p>
        <p>OPEN-AIR MEETING. The Veto Crisis.</p>
        <p>SCOTCHING THE LORDS POWER FOR EVIL</p>
        <p>The Knatsford lii•isi3a Li oral A•soziation held a saer,raful...ope.o-alr...wet!ag I. Vic:aria Saaa!e</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath, on Thursday inning. There was rain In the early part of the evening but it cleared oft jolt before the meeting began, with the result that the pro- c:edings attrac.ed a large gathering. The speech*. were 'ironed to with great attention and not a single dissentient voice was raised. Mr. W. 11. Sherburn pre- sided and the principal speaker. were Mr. J. R. Tow limo°. one of (be hon. ...or the Association, and Mr. T. 11. Gareide, of Manoheeter. .</p>
        <p>The Chain... contended that elthough it bad only been in ollioe a few months, the present Government had fully ,pistifled the oonddence placed in it by the people at the last election. Although the Koutaford Division was not found on the right aide, the electors of the country as • whole endoned by • good majority the Parliament Bill, and said it most be aimed into law. (Apple...). The Peers professed that they were not blocking the measure but that they only wanted the people to say whether It should become law or not. Their action since that time, however, had not proved :hat they were in earnest in their profemiuns. The</p>
        <p>M CTIL ATIM HILL</p>
        <p>which now lay on the table of the Horne of Lord. showed what the Lords would really like to do, if they dare. (Hear, hear) In addition to re-affirming the Parlia• merit Hill the Government had done a great dee) of reel good progressive work. (Applause.) Touching upon the recent censor', Mr. Sberburn said it disclosed the fact that the population of Scotland we' not incressing lu its proper ratio Some people said the steady ll Isv of emigration Pram thst country was caused by the country becoming poorer and there was cot eufticient industry to keep the people there. lhe opposite was really the case. Some privileged individuals were getting eo ich from the induetry of the people that they hal power to withhold from the people great tracts of lend in Scotland for sport int purposes and drive the Inhabi- tants to emigrate when they would rather remain at home.</p>
        <p>THE LAND WERE NOT IRKED FOR THE PLOPLE the evil would become still more flagrant. Liberals did not plead for privilege, but for junk. ell round. (Applaus.) They asked that their tegislatlon should , have an equal opportunity with Tory legislation of peeling into law. (Applause.) Mr. Tom usson sold they were met at a crisis in the , great struggle between the force* of progress and Liberalism and the forces of Conservatism and privilege, as exemplified in the House of Lord. The Parliament Bill, whieb was at present before the !loose of Lords, would secure the Bonnet Commons, the elected repre- sentatives of the people, In their susrem icy in legisla- tion, end it relegated the House of Lords to Its proper position—which wee that of revision end delay. ft did not matter very much whether the Lords passed the Bill at once or not. They would</p>
        <p>AVENTOALLY ICAVII TO SWALLOW IT in its entirety. (Applause). The ameminaents which , they bad sought to add to the Bill took all the good and virility out of it. Mr. Aegeltb, however, had got the Lords in the hollow of his hand, and if necessary, be would exercise his power to pan the Bill through. (Applause). Al • strong Liberal and one who was absolutely opposed to the hereditary principle-In the Beyond Clamber, he (the speviker , was delighted that they bad at last, once sad for all, got to g, ire with the berlditary legisistore which stood against their Liberal programme and the cause of progress. If the Lords persisted in their amendments, he presumed they would beer on Monday that Mr. Aar-pith intended to</p>
        <p>A DOI, THE ONLY ONSTIEUTIONAL HEAN, at bin proposal by creating a sufficient number of Peen to swamp the obstintcy of the present members of the House of Lords. If. (the &amp;praiser) confessed that he did not like the heredi- tary principle or a House of Peen at all, but to his mind the creation of more Peers would be making the best of. bed job—in fact it was the wily Constitutional method and it would be adopted if necessary, and the will of the Meuse of Commons would preveil. (Applause.) The Litrarals were twitted with not placing the reform of the House of Lords in the forefront of their programme. They were, however, determined first of all to</p>
        <p>SCOTCH THEIR POWER OF DOING THE. WORST OE EVILS and then they would talk about potting them on a better basis. (Applause). They did not tonere in the sincerity of the attempts of the Hausa of Lord• at reforming themselves. They did not believe in • death bed repentance, or that the Lords really hateaded to subordinate themselves to the elected Parliament. The only reform" that the Lords would willingly agree to would leave them much •tronger for mischief and for the upsetting of Liheret legislation. There was no peed to he afraid that the Liberals were analoos to introdsoe Meals Chamber government. We did not more any too quickly in this country end there wean very stroll( and sane feeling spinet any 111-cormidwed attempt., at. change. 're. safeguards that were intro- duced ID the Pan I:ameet Bill were of the most ample character. It was only when the elected represent.- tire, of the people had plased three time• • Bill intro- duced within two years of their coming hack to Parliament with a fresh MAIIiDID. that the House of Lords would not be able to throw it out. Would they tell him, as reasonable people, whetter there was anythieg In the nature of hasty kgislation or single Chamber government in that ? (" No.") If anything they were •LLOWING THE HOCSE or LORDS TOO )41:,H TI,PE</p>
        <p>to ieterfere 'rate legmlati the Loomis sought leas fair treatment. ,Applause.) When they were beaten st Lbe polls and • Conservative Government wes returned, Umy expected to be governed according I o Coneervative Idees.A II that they claimed was whoa • Liberal Ministry wO5 returned, they should be allowed to govern she</p>
        <p>country on Liberal lines 11.11 not be Interfered with by an irreeponsiLls House of Lords. (anYietms.) Owe of the first things that would come shout when the posse of the House of Lords was curtailed, was tb• _</p>
        <p>AB3LITION OF PLURAL VOTING. They coeld not hope to get a measure of that Lisa through no long as the House of Lords retained Its present petition. Although he did not say ties abolition of plural voting would turn the Knots- ford Division Into a Literal conotituency, it eruld rare a dilTeres^e of sereral buad,egl VOW. becAser Ours were torn in that constituency oho exercised the power of voting In (ram five to tvreoty.flre d.flerent places at one election. There were three great terrors In the workingman's life which, he was praud to say, were in • fair way to being abated by means of social legislation. The drat was the con- stant fear of old age overtaking non, ; the wood the fear of losing hi. job; and the third the fear of sickness preventing him from working. Had ever so many efforts been made t 3 cope with rho.c difficulties in the seat as they found In the Old Age Pensions Act, the Labour Bureaux and the State fmnirsoce hut? (Applause.) He maintained that never in the history of Parliament had so mach time been devoted to the consideration of the Kelel conditions of the workers a. dit ri g the last three Libels! Government.. (Applause.) As to the geuend 000dition of the Country, TRADE HAD INCREASED ENORMOUSLY, paoperiest bad decreased aid seeing' bank deposits No shown an advance. The Government had also succeeded in tapping sources of revenue that wore never open,d before, and they were making the westbly b ar their fair share of the national burden. (Applause). Hr. Garaide said he held that the svistoorscy of thin country, as represented by the protest Douse of Lord., moon body that was bringing dareepect and contempt upon the greet mass of the people and therefore it• claws should be cut and its teeth drawn. its would not exterminate the Peers; he would simply have them as ornament., but of power they should have abaulutely none. (Applause.) Mr. P. E. Smith said ye were sunning to _</p>
