        <p>DUKE OF REDFORD hod just been selling I.nd on one of his estates, and the sum of £554,000 had been realized. Greeted that the land was honestly hi., was the vales of £554,000 In the land iteelf? If It ems, where did it come from ? Did the Duke put It there ? Did he invest or risk anythin; ? Did ha employ 'anybody to create the value? Ile did not. The £sai,ooo of value in the land hsd beer, crested by the people who were living on and around that land. The land- osiner, however, had nut hitherto been called ROO to bear Lis lair share of taxation. The workingman who named El per week pill morel. tlestion, proportion- ate to his eernings, than Andre,. Carnegie, the million lire. If a scheme of taxation could be brought into force by which every man should pay proportion- ately on the value he tuld, It would be °pliable and fair. 'that was precisely whet the House of Lords would not have. It would rounds their land. On the authority of Sir Henry Maine, there were comparati rely few landowners in this country who mold show any legs' authority for the ownership of the land which they said was their.. After all, it KR• because TILE GOVERNMENT WAS STRIKING AT I.AN'I MONOPOLY (oat the House of Lord. was kicking. Al.nufacturere mho w..nted LO invest their money in new industrial entered*ma and employ thousanda of men, were handi- capped by the difficulty In obtaining the neceessry laud. If land wan vacant and there was money to hand to eruct factorial ; If men were waiting for employment and the Isodoeoer was not using his land hut simply sitting ow it for the purpose of acquiring the value Chick the public, need created, ass the Government doing wrong in taxing the 'slue which UTIO community created up to lit, lain peony in order to benefit the people who could inn touch the lead ? (Applause.) The tariff reformere fame along with a cheery by which it an article cost id.. they were seise to make it coat to., mid It would be much cheaper. (Laughter).</p>
