        <p>—• matter which had been apeman, !Wooed boom to be, by recent visits to refomustory whools. Although most of the boys most on their admiseion been bean rough, uncouth and bad, they were now as well behaved as could be desired. Am a lover of music,be eta struck with the tact that those boys who played in the school band were foremost in Milled branches of work where. tbe unmusical bay• were more fitted for agriculture. Tho li.eipline inculcated in school aboold be carried on in after-life until it became a habit, an that work and pleasure would be govoreed by a certai• method. The Mayor concluded by offering his eon- gratulati JIM to the headniuter and his collpioes and alto to the Education Committen and the lYirect or upon the splendid results attaiood by the school. (Applause,. A PLEASING ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>The •udiencei then hid the pleasure of witnessing • most interesting entertainment which um very !appreciatively received. lb. first part of lb* pro- gramme took the form of the ?anal play " Ls Merita</p>
        <p>Agricole," In which the character. were taken 157 the following ; —Monsieur Rigatid. Is merchant) WiMena E Mason ; Madame Bigoted (hie wife) Nary Wipe Monsieur Pigand la merchant) Stanley Crank ; Madame Pigged, (hie wife) Charlotte Putnam Jacques (eon of hi Piped) Gilbert Wood ; Madeline (danghter of M Rigaud) Ethel Mire!! ; Motill)eer Leferrir and Mamie°, Margot (Physicians) William Cooper and Walter ord; Loots (friend of Al Itigand) Thomas Lightfoot ; Adolphe (friend of Al Pisani) William Medley ; Marie 'maid) Doris 'Thompson. The eeting thlongbont wee intelligent and refreshingly crisp sad •Igorous. William E. Maori; end Staolev Crank I gave eery realietic portraysls of the choleric rivals for Stele dialientions, their line, beins clearly uttered while the action wee natural bin strikingly descriptive. Mom, Witt% also demisea special mention for her lunaffected yet convincing acting as Msdante Rigand. it. performance had been well arranged by klieg E. Wilkinson. A contain" Riding Hood's reecue " 'arranged by Mr. I H. 11. Albery and Mini 11. Dawson), sea than submitted. The children had been carefully trained •ttd the singing of woloiste and chorus alike wag distinctly 'creditable. The voice, bad leen well selected. the tone I end blend being very plesming, and them wee nterked attention to expression. Tbe Gimlets woe lot given in ooncert form, but on the etal. with scenery and character cost tunee—• departure which greatly improved ihe effect. 'the soloist', all of whom ere to be highly complimented,..,,, —Red Iliding Hood. Doris 'Davis ; Alother, Mary Hackett ; Woodmen, Mr. R. 11. Albery ; Wolf, John Smittergood ; Itubln, Louie Lsigh ; Butt..- cop, Annie Hill ; Rose, Rhoda Hill. The pianoforte sad violin parte were tastefully played by Evelio* Butterworth and WM. BWlLillt respectively. The scenery bad been painted by etudente of the School of Art, under the direction of Mr. C. bleweon. After the prize distribution tee was served to parent. and tritude