      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WHY WORK IS SCARCE, AND HOW TO MEND MATTERS. By W. R Lester, M.A. VIM GOES ON IN THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>WHY WORK IS SCARCE, AND HOW TO MEND MATTERS. By W. R Lester, M.A. VIM GOES ON IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>_ An important Small HoldiogSociety in the Midlands rents 300 a•res at 31s. Cd. per acre, while equally good land eurrounding is let at about ne. per acre br large forms. The Small Holding, Society in clearly over-rented by alicut £lBO yearly. which inseam it of capital, and makes it unable to buy proper stock and implements. To get money the member, are thinking of seeking • loon through a credit bank. Lot then they will have to pay interest at 5 or 8 per cent for what they borrow, whereas if they were rented at the same figure as the surrounding farms they would have the mosey in pocket, al would not need to get into debt. Mesnwhile they struggle on without proper stock, so that not only is their produce molter than it should be, but meo in the towns who ought Li be making their ploughs and harrows are oleo idle.</p>
        <p>WHY HIGH RENTS IN THE COUNTRY MEAN</p>
        <p>IDLE YEN IN THE TOWNS.</p>
        <p>- - - -- Thus tie high rent these mall holders have to pay in responsible for their poverty, and not only prevents them from producing vegetables in abundance, but MALI, impo-sible for them to give orders to the n!ough and implement makers in the towns. Dot for the land monopoly, which corn., between them like a lank wall, the small holders would be employiog the ploughluakere, and the plough-makera would be employing the small holders, by each buying what the othei• produce. Perhaps, however, you will object that there is no use sending more stuff 'market,.. markets are already glutted and priers often cut to nothing. Here you get to the heart of the matter, and I am glad you raise the objection.</p>
        <p>WHY IS THE MARKET FOR PRODUCE</p>
        <p>GLUTTED?</p>
        <p>It is quite true that markets are glutted, but we want to know the reason why. Is it because everyone has all the vegetables be desires, or is it because millions of Englishmen and women are so por that they cannot buy enough to keep thernselve• and their families in decent health ? Markets are not glutted because produce is too pleritiftil. The true reason is that customers are too poor to boy what is produced. Surely the idea of over prochtc•iou is ridiculous to long as the barest needs of millions of honest workers remain unatistied !</p>
        <p>THE REMEDY FOR GLUTTED MARKETS. The temedy, therefore, is to increase the number cf customers who ran go to market with something ha their pockets. In other words, we mast helm wages. Now, whete are the beet markets of the .matt-holder and Janitor? Sorely in our great towns. Why are our town markets not much brisker than at present ? Because of the manes of under-paid, or unemployed men and women who can but little or nothing. What is the root cause of their paTchy ? The answer will show that tows and countly hang together, and tint the labour question and the lead question are the tame in both.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WHAT GOES ON IN THE TOWNS.</head>
        <p>WHAT GOES ON IN THE TOWNS.</p>
        <p>hound every growing town and village i• a ring of high-priced, unused land whose owners withhold It from use or ask p ices which builders, man.- lecturer. and traders cannot psy. In this way is throttled the natural, healthy growth of towns and villages, awl the expansion a their industry. This means slatk t,nde ; slack trade means unemployed men; unemployed men struggling for work means /ow wages all round ; low wages in the towns mean, poor useless cmtomers who cannot I,uy what farmers and small holders hare to sell. Glaued markets, in other words.</p>
        <p>HOW WORK IS MADE SCARCE IN THF: TOWNS. Look at a sample of what goes on, and see bow people are impovetiehed by the land monopoly. London's population is overflowing,and land is wanted for housing the people. Near by. no Wembley, lie some 9'.! aorta of imitable building land. This land was recently put up to public gale, and though it ma* bid up to E.. 32,000 by builders anxious to build the needed house., the owner withdrew it from the market sa/ing he would not let it go till .f. 45,101 was bid. (Note : Meantime, under our pmeut system of rating, this va'uable land is only • assessed on its agricultural vette, and so the owner gets off with a payment of ILI to the rates !) So, though the land to sorely wanted to house the teeming population, it atill remains locked up. Masons, bricklayers, joiners, and labourers are thou thrown on the labour market to cut down wage.. In thin way not only are town workers imporeii.hed,but also the small hol dere in the country, whose !ruble. they are prevented from buying. LAND MONOPOLY. AT THE ROOT OF THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>IIArrER.</head>
        <p>IIArrER.</p>
        <p>If we want to remo7relheT —disease we most remove the camre. Cut at the root, and destroy the land monopoly. The potre and certaia way to cut at the root it to tax Lat ij Values in town and country alike, sr,' no make the genie of withholding laud not w ,rtlx the enrd e In in iking a valuation of the land of all imin the Lletal Government has given us itaso iustruttient. Let on one it !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>110 W TO FREE THE LAND.</head>
        <p>110 W TO FREE THE LAND.</p>
        <p>After valuation. the next step must be to reverse the present syetem of taxation hy aLiolishing %MOOR farts buildings, dwelling-hotmes, factories aml shops, and substitute taxes on men in proportion to the VI 00 of the laud they own. Owners would then have to pay just as much in I ixes when they lock up valuable hind or imperfectly use it. an whoa they develop it fully, BO that no man could afford to own land without putting it t the best possible me. in other words, without employing the greatest possible amount of labour ox it according •• it was wanted f r farming, building, mining, sir any other purpose. Land now locked up would then come on the market, rents would fall, and our Small Holders would soon find themselves with their !:.lao in pocket every year, and able to keep men busy in the towns making implement, for them. Morei.ver, men like the owners of Wembley Estate could no longer •fford to cry "hands off !" because taxes would have to be paid oat the real value of the !rod all the time it lay idle. So building sites mould fall to • figure which builders could pay; thousands of tradesmeu is town and country &amp;lam would be net to work at decent wage., and they would soon buy up the increased produce of farmer and Small Holder in the country, who would be getting lower rents too. ALL A LAND QI'ESTMN AT THE BOTTOM. Trace the matter to its source, and you will find the Land Question pure and simple. You have both the right and duty to insist on the free and full development of the land now held one of use at exorbitant prices or only half used. Once recurs that, and there will be work at good wages for all. No more need to emigrate across the ocean tr seek • livelihood. fao yuu want to being real prosperity to town and country alike? Then help to unlock the land. You MO dO it by insisting that Land Values shall lie taxed. Will you give • haul to those who are fighting the battle ?</p>
      </div>
