      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT TO SCOTLAND</head>
        <p>ROYAL VISIT TO SCOTLAND</p>
        <p>The King and Queen arrived at Edinburgh from Wales on Monday, and crawled by a squadron of Scottish horse drove through the streets to the Palace of Holyrood. The journey to the Palace was marked by a long roar of hearty cheering from crowds who had waited for hours. All the nobility of Scotland gathered to welcome their Sovereign and a Queen called by the name of Scotland's fairest Queen. Their Majesties engagements during the week include • Levee, State Banquet, Court, Garden Party, visits to St. Giles Cathedral, Royal Scottish Academy, Royal Infirmary. a presentation of colours to the 2nd Battalion Royal Scots. Our picture shows the Royal procession moving along Princes' Street. The Scott Memorial is seen in the background.</p>
        <p>• • . . •t ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HOLYROOD PALACE.</head>
        <p>HOLYROOD PALACE.</p>
        <p>Preparations for the Royal Visit to Edinburgh have been going on for some time, and Holyrood Palace has undergone a thorough process of renovation and reconstruction. Little bad been done to the building since it was last in occupation half -a-century ago. £B,OOO has now been spent, and the historic Peace has been made a fitting residence for their Majesties. The private apartments arc on the second floor, and command a pleasant outlook upon Use grounds and hills of Holyrood Park. To accommodate their Majesties' guests two large annexes have been erected in woad, one a large supper room capable of seating 1,000 persons, and a miller one to accommodate 250.</p>
        <p>Ens Eileen Dartrey. "My Lady's Chamber F the stage for a very interesting ,cene, to ark; very casual and brief apFearances of ills, contribute not a little. She is worth Carney Bros indulge in a voc.il wherein one ie a tramp and the no is too much cheap sentiment make the turn really me,' would iinprove matters excellent work and ERRLESTOWN OPINION.</p>
        <p>Miss Eileen Dartrey. "My I.ady'a Chamber " finds the stage tor a very interesting rcene, to which the very casual and brief appearances of Miss Dartrey contribute not • little. She is worth • better part. Carney Bros indulge in • vocal scene " Brothers," wherein one is a tramp and the other • dads. There is too much cheep sentiment and indiffeient music to make the turn really melitorions. A little humour would improve matters. The orchestra, as usnal,dos• excellent work and the pictures are interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>A CURIOUS BIRD'S NEST.</p>
        <p>A very curious gift has been made to the museum of a French town. It is a bird's nest made entirely of steel. There is a watch factory in the town and also many watch-makers and many bits of fine steel,the remains of broken springs which are thrown aside. Last summer they found this curious nest which had been built in the works-yard by a pair of wagtails. It measured ten centimetres in circumference (get out your French measure-table to see how big this is) and was made of nothing at all except watch- springs. When the birds had hatched their young and the brood had learned to fly a watchmaker secured .their unique nest and sent it to the Museum. It is a very interest- ing proof of the intelligence of birds in adapting anything which comes within their reach.</p>
        <p>HOW GREAT MEN HAVE BEGUN LIFE. A patient and thoughtful man in America has been spending his time in making a list of the t'unisand greatest and most successful men who have arisen in his country during the past generat'on. He has also been analysing the list to see how they began life. The result of his enquiry is so interesting that I am going to give it to you in brief form.</p>
        <p>Of course by successful men he does not mean money-makers only, but those who have become inventors and who have influenced the moral as well as the physical live? of .the people.</p>
        <p>This is how the thousand started life. Three hundred started as farmers sons.</p>
        <p>Two hundred-etar---ted-as- -m- es—se—n-ge-r hO;-;</p>
        <p>Two hundred were newsboys. One hundred were printers' apprentices. One hundred were apprenticed in manu- factories.</p>
        <p>Fifty began at the bottom in railway work. Fifty—only fifty—bad wealthy parents to give them a start.</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY. A LUCKY ffiCAPE.</p>
        <p>The farmer and his wife watched their dog as he chased madly down the track after the fouro'clock train. Redid it every day, and always returned exhausted. "I wonder why he chases that train," remarked the wife, with her eyes on a little cloud of dust that showed where Rover was. "That's nothothering me," answered her husband. "I'm wondering what he'd do with it if he caught it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHILI) AND MOTHER.</head>
        <p>CHILI) AND MOTHER.</p>
        <p>0 Bother-My-Love, if you'll give me your heed, And go where I ask you to wander. I will lead you away to a beantiful land The Dreamland that's waiting out yonder. We'll welk in • eweet-poeie pardon out there. Where moonlight and starlight are streaming, And the flowers and the birds are filling the air With the fragrance and music of dreaming. There'll be no little tired-out IN to undress, No questione or cares to perplex you ; There'll be no little bruise. or mpg to arras, Nor patching of stockings tl vex you. For I'll nck you away on. eilver-dew stream, And ging you asleep when you're weary, And no one shall know of our beautiful dream But you and your own little dearie. And when I am tired I'll nestle my head In the bosom that !toothed roe no often, And the wide-awske eters *ball sing in my Mead A song which our dreaming shall soften. to, Mother-my-Love, let me take your dear band, And away through the starlight we'll wander ; Away through the mint to the beautiful land 'the Dreemlend that's waiting out yonder ! F,I.OF,NE FIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEACHER'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</head>
