      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CARNARVON INVESTIIURE DAY.</head>
        <p>CARNARVON INVESTIIURE DAY.</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIONS OF A WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
        <p>During the week embracing the date of the investiture of the young Prince of Wales at Carnarvon, I was spending a quiet holiday in North Wales, and becoming infected with some of the loyal enthusiasm by which I found myself surrounded, decided to visit the ancient town on the day of the all- important function, with a view to seeing at least something of the proceedings. Accordingly, after a pleasant sail along 111,rtai Strait, I landed at Carnarvon Pier at about eleven o'clock on the forenoon of, Thursday, July 13th. Whilst approaching the town there was to be seen from the steamers deck, the road leading into Carnarvon from Griffith's Crossing, the destination of the Royal train from Holyhead and the starting point of the processian tcv Carnarvon Castle. The highway was gay 1 with bunting and then already thronged with people. Doubtful of being able to get into the main streets after arrival at this com- paratively late hour, I made my way town- wards from the landing place, and succeeded in getting into the thoroughfare leading to the castle, at a point a short distance on the town side of the railway station. I found too—thanks no doubt to excellent Police arrangements—that whilst the narrow street was thronged on each side, and every window and point of vantage occupied, there was no uncomfortable crowding or crushing. There was now await of about two hours before His Royal Highness, the Prince, was due to arrive.</p>
        <p>The wait, however, was anything but a tedious one. Not only was the street inter- estingon account of being lined with military and police, and gay with venetian masts, festoons and bunting, but there was a continual stream of prominent people passing to the castle, by carriage and on foot. There were gentlemen in court dress and official uniforms, naval and military officers, municipal and local government dignitaries, ladies gaily dressed, &amp;c. A couple of striking figures were two Welsh bards or Druids, clad from head to foot in flowing white robes. A motor car passed containing a clean shaved elderly gentleman in a naval looking uniform. An onlock .r was certain it was the Prime Minister, but the naval dress could not bo accounted for, though subsequent enquiries ilicited the fact that it was none other than Mr. Asquith, in his Trinity House uniform. The Home Secretary, Mr. W. Churchill, was more easily recognised. It would be a grand thing for the waiting crowds at royal and other functions if all prominent and official person- ages could be prevailed upon to display a numbered card as they pass along, or carry attached to their persons an identification mark like motor cars. Then all that would be necessary would be the publication of a , programme or key, and everything would be plain.</p>
        <p>A most pleasing feature of the waiting interval was the singing of the school children occupying stands on the line of the procession. They sang the national anthem "God bless the Prince of Wales," " Men of Harlech," Ste. both in English and Welsh. The young people were in splendid voice, and their singing was thoroughly appreciated, and heartily applauded, as it well deserved, by the waiting crowds. Ono had a strong desire, like Oliver Twist, to ask for more. At about 1.30 p.m. loud cheering in the distance heralded the approach of the Prirce of Wales, and presently his carriage came along with its escort of the Denbighshire HtlBBBll4. His Royal Highness, who looked a most bright and pleasant Prince in his smart naval uniform, continuously saluted in recognition of the hearty cheers of the crowd who gave him a most enthusiastic welcome.</p>
        <p>After an interval of about half-an-hour, resounding cheers gave notice of the approach of their Majesties, the King and Queen, who were received with such bursts of loyal enthusiasm as I am sure could not be exceeded anywhere. Both graciously acknow- ledged the plaudits of their devoted subjects as the Royal carriage proceeded at a walking paoe towards the castle. Princess Mary, who accompanied her parents, looked in all respects a bright and pretty young English girl. Both the Queen and Princess were dressed in white. Their' Majesties were escorted by a troop of Life Guards, the stalwart and superbly- mounted troopers, with their waving plumes, and glittering helmets and cuirasses, forming quite a brilliant spectacle in the narrow old highway. The Royal carriage was also accompanied by a party of the Yeomen of the Guard, whose quaint old-world uniform attacted a good deal of notice when they were first seen in the street on their march out to meet the royal party. Having never before seen any of the Royal Family I was pleased to find that the reproductions of their photographs seen so often in the newspapers are most faithful likenesses of the august persons. I think the public of to-day , are greatly indebted to a highly efficient press for the excellent manner in which they put before us pictures both of notable people and important events. Shortly before the passing of the royal carriage I was suddenly bailed in the crowd by a Warrington acquaintance, who I found was engaged at Carnarvon on business connected with the investiture function.</p>
        <p>After the procession had passed to the K`,astle I was fortunate enough to obtain some much-needed refreshment at a cafe which speedily became crowded—one might , almost say besieged—and there I saw a Warrington lady. I have no doubt there were many other representatives of the "good old town' in the Welsh Metropolis, though I was only able to recognise toe two. Finding the way down to the Castle and vicinity closed by the police, I waited the procession's return which took place about 4 p.m. The Prince then rode in the carriage with the King, Queen, and Princess Mary, wearing his gold chaplet, robe, &amp;c., as Prince ,of Wales. Again the waiting masses of ' people greeted their Majesties and the Prince and Princess right royally.</p>
        <p>It was not until about five o'clock that I was permitted to attempt to make my way castlevrards, and then the crowds in the narrow streets were so dense that to reach the vicinity of the Castle and be in time for my steamer (sailing at 5.30 p.m.) was impos- sible, so I reluctantly gave up the attempt and walked to the pier. There again I found a large concourse of. people, but in due time all seemed to get aboard their respective steamers. Our boat, to Llandudno, Bangor, Sic., was crowded, but after another enjoy- able run along Menai Strait, I reached my destination safely, very well pleased with my day's outing, and my first sight of our King and Queen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PILGRIMAGES \e-wo3</head>
        <p>PILGRIMAGES \e-wo3</p>
        <p>EYAM. A Village Of Noble Memories</p>
        <p>The hardy cyclist may feel equal to the strain, but we ourselves have always quailed before the hills of Derbyshire and taken the journeys either on foot or behind the best horseflesh obtainable. For the present journey a cheap ticket to Grindleford and a march into the recesses of the hills is most to be commended.</p>
        <p>By universal consent the circle of hill country which we know of as the Peak District ranks among the finest pieces of scenery in England. Some of its beauties may be discerned from the Midland Railway, but to appreciate it to the full there is nothing like a day of tramping. When the mind has leisure the great panorama of hill and dale, field and stream, makes an impression which is never effaced.</p>
        <p>Two things draw us to Eyam and either I would be enough to justify the pilgrimage. The first is the splendour of its surround- ings and its own tranquil beauty ; the second attraction is the greater, and rests in the fact that Eyam, among English villages has won imperishable honour. The ' glory of the village is the glory of quiet courage in the fare of a terrible calamity, and one of the most recent chronicles of Eyam has gone to the length of saying: "The immortal victors of Thermopylo and Marathon who fought so bravely in liberty's holy cause, have no greater, no stronger claim to the admiration of succeeding genera- tions than the humble villagers of Eyam in the year 1666."</p>
        <p>THE GREAT PLAGUE.</p>
        <p>It was during the years of the Great Plague that the glory was won. This little place was visited by the awful pest in 1666 and the heroism lay in the fact that its people</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>11011PICSSPN.8 PULPIT.</head>
        <p>11011PICSSPN.8 PULPIT.</p>
        <p>THE PLAGUE COT</p>
        <p>be:ame martyrs, willingly sacrificing their own lives in order that the plague might be prevented from spreading. The story is touching and it is terrible; yet it is one of the great stories of our nation, and we can never read it without a solemn toy.</p>
        <p>There is one spot we visit which brings hack with terrible force the memory of those awful days. The people did not gather in church for fear of contagion, but twice a week as well as on Sundays the Rector held a service in .a little dell. A stream of water runs through it and taking his stand upon a rocky bank he gathered his flock upon the opposite side. With the water running between he poured out his heart in words of courage and consolation while the brave, quiet villagers listened with solemn moistened eyes. Never twice the same congregation, for every day brought the tragedy of sudden mysterious death.</p>
        <p>_v... For William Mompeeson the climax of</p>
        <p>• ', ".- •-• •••; 0„ '.. ' ' . . , . .. • A 7:.'",•;# : : • .... . . : • • . .I.N litlP , ••••:,1-1 '." 4 -- ° 11-. • ,‘:- _, ~.' ,44 1 • 7", . • ht • . /1. •*'" 12; • ... . ..... . ... ..,,. . ~ _. .. :11111 7- ~,5.,4'--'" •••••:',--''' ' - :-...-. I- ' ''.' 4-',‘..., - ---r ' 410 .. -•• ... . • ...................... ..............._,.. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TIIF. PLAGUE AT EYAK</head>
        <p>TIIF. PLAGUE AT EYAK</p>
        <p>Everyone knows the story of the Great , Plague of London which swept uncounted thousands to their death in 1665 and 1666. There was no postal system then to carry the news t the corners of the kingdom. Little Eyam—romote among its lovely hills —was almost out of the world and had heard little of the plight of London. There was little likelihood of any fugitive from the great city flying thence, and as for any thought of danger who could imagine the plague visiting such a garden of loveliness? So in the fall of the year 1665 while the carts were parading London to the cry "Bring out your dead" and bodies were being flung wholesale into huge pits, the rustics of Eyam were dancing at the village wakes and giving themselves up to their annual feast of merriment. It was to be the last festival for many long years, and most of them sang and danced that day for the last time. For a box was arriving in the village by the carriers cart addressed to the home of the village tailor. It was a parcel of samples of cloth from London and certain other made-up garments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FATAL- PARCEL.</head>
        <p>THE FATAL- PARCEL.</p>
        <p>The cottage still stands to which the (aril parcel was delivered, and is known by the ominous r.ame of Plague Cottage. The package had come from the infected area in the metropolis and bore the germs of the dreaded disease.</p>
        <p>The cloth was found to be damp and was hung to dry. Within a few hours the tailor was stricken down. The marks of the plague, a pistule upon the breast and groin, were manifest, and in a few hours he was dead. His name was George Vicars and the Church register shows that he was buried on September 7th, 1665. For three weeks there was nothing to create alarm, and then an Edward Cooper was suddenly stricken. Four others followed before the end of the month and it became evident that there was cause for alarm. In October the number had grown to 23, and during six months from four to nine people died each month,all with the same symptoms.</p>
        <p>Eyam was a village with a population of only 350 inhabitants and such death rate in so small a community was sufficiently alarm- ing. At the beginning of June 1666 the number of those who had perished counts up to 77 and the death roll is entered in full in the Church register. But from this date onward the entries cease. The churchyard, indeed, had to close its gates, for the inhabitants fell so thick and fast that funeral rites were abandoned and the fallen ones hastily placed underground without coffin or shroud.</p>
        <p>The plague had broken out in terrifying fury. In June 19 had fallen victims, in July the number had risen to 56 and during August there were 77 whose bodies were laid in gardens and fields wherever they fell. Panic seized the people. They were afraid of each other because of contagion, and some families fled the infected area into neighbouring places. This srt an example to others and all whom death had not claimed resolved to desert the village and take up homes else- where.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>VILLAGE OF HEROES,</head>
        <p>A VILLAGE OF HEROES,</p>
        <p>And now comes the story of 4ublime sacrifice. The village held two ministers. One of them was Thomas Stanley who had been ejected under the Act of Uniformity in 1662 and was therefore one of the first Non- conformists. The other was the new rector William Mompesson a young man of 27 with a wife and two children. Both played heroic parts, but the peculiar glory of that dark day belongs to Mompesson.</p>
        <p>With the force that only a brave heart can command he asked them to remain, pointing out that in travelling they would I carry the dreaded disease to others and endanger the whole country. At all costs the country must be saved, and better that a village should perish than a nation. He offsred as his part of the sacrifice to remain and die amongst them. The noble appeal found an echo and not a man or woman lek the village. . _</p>
        <p>Then came the solemn Nan of campaign. as A boundary line w drawn round the village, about half-a-mile in circuit, beyond which no one was to go. No one must enter and no one must leave. Food was brought from the outside world and deposited at a given spot whence the villagers fetched it, leaving money there in payment. The boundary fixed, those within it calmly took their part in the awful drama of death. , Twice a day a body was rapidly dragged from a home and thrust into the ground. The church was closed and no one registered the dead; the bells bung silent and in awful quietness they waited for the strokes of the angel of death. In the month of September only 45 were left, and of these 14 died in the , first week of October. It seemed as though no one would live to tell the tale, for five out of every six had been taken. Then on October 11th the plague ceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>TAU ES AT EVAM.</head>
        <p>TAU ES AT EVAM.</p>
        <p>trial came with the death of his young wife. We see her grave in the churchyard and think of the brave heart that could bury her there and still he the hero and minister of the stricken village.</p>
        <p>CONSECRATED GROUND.</p>
        <p>If there be such a thing as consecrated soil then Eyam is -.Mirely consecrated, for we tread everywhere upon the dust of heroes. The dead are everywhere! The mountain riot; the plain; the woods pretend; All the lone dells, the Fertile aod the fair. Ate one vast belie' ground.</p>
        <p>Here and there a pile of stones marks a place of burial, and upon one hillside are some simple tombstones bearing names.</p>
        <p>- At another spot which we climb a hill to see, a rough stone wall that surrounds a tiny cemetery.</p>
        <p>We see, too, Mom pessons Well, a hill-side spring at which food and money were exchanged with the outside world.</p>
        <p>William Mompesson lived through the Plague, and told the story of it in unaffected and quiet words. In November, when all was over, he wrote to a friend: " Now, blessed be God, all our fears are over, for none have died of the plague sine,, tb! eleven! h of October. . . . For mv part have scarcely apparel to shelter my holy, having , wanted more than I needed, merely for example. . . . I have largely tasted of the goodness of the Creator, and the grim looks of death did yet affsight me.</p>
        <p>Brave soul No wonder Eyani and the world classes him among its heroes. But in that day of terror, fear reigned supreme, and he had to wait long for the day of appreciation. Three years later he was appointed to the living of Eakring, Notts, but the parishioners refused to receive him I because of his experience in plague-stricken Eyam, and he had to reside in a hut until their dread had given way to calm. Eakring surely got a rector of whom it was not worthy.</p>
        <p>The church and churchyard with its fine sundial is a happy place to linger and Eyam is a place of unspoiled beauty, but somehow everything is overshadowed by the memory of the brave hearts of long ago. Life would be sweet amid these glorious scenes if it were sweet anywhere, and the summer of 1666 would be as rich as the summer of 1911, yet just here hundreds closed their eyes upon a glad world in the hope that their death would mean life to others.</p>
        <p>We have spent long hours upon the field of Waterloo, but Waterloo has not the glory of Eyam. I A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ISUFFERED AGONY WITH ECZEMA</head>
        <p>ISUFFERED AGONY WITH ECZEMA</p>
