        <p>the letters, by her directions, are inkeit strsight to her; she looks thews up." "She is s tigress," said Deepe. "But we'll be oven with her yet." _ . .</p>
        <p>Mary laughed. "I want to be," she said. "She will read that letter from Teheran," continued Deepe, "after the old man hat gone." "What elm do you expect?" said Mary. Of course she will read it. "Yen," answered Mary. "Look here, Mary. You say you know where the diamond is?"</p>
        <p>"Yes." "Can you secure it to-night?" "Imposetible." "But it is eater "It is safe." "Mary, do you think by any chance that old Denali*li will tell bin daughter about the diamond and this man, Gerard Lonadste, be- fore he dim?" "I can't my," answered Mary. Then she added, dropping on her knem and laying her hand on her lover'n arm: "To tell you the tenth, that in why I sent for you. I think it more than probable that the old man will tell Rebecca all about the diamond and ite hintory; in fact I am almost certain he will." "Oh, that we could hear!" mid Arnold Dcepe. "I have an idea," said Mary. "It is highly dangerous, but there is a posaibility of its nurceeding." "Yon are a clever little thing, my Mary," mid Deepe. "Now, out with your thought; what is it?" "I want to be prettent," mid Mary, "when Rebecca in informed by her father of the true history of the diamond." Deepe clapped Mary on the shoulder. You are plucky!' he mid. "You are the woman of all women for me. But how can you manage thin?" "The nurse ix nearly dead with fatigue. I could put on her cap and apron and slip into the room in the dark." "No, that won't do," mid Deepc; "it L. too dangerom. Besides, I ham seen the nurse, and ahe in taller than you; I know it</p>
        <p>' "There is a cupboard in the room in which I could hide, but the difficulty is how to get to it. Rebecca and Nurse Prescott never leave Mr. Ilenshall alone for an instant." Deepe covered his face with his hand. Ile was thinking hard. If I could hide in that cupboard," ed Marv, "I should know everything." knowledge would be invaluable,' said Deepe. "But how am I to get into the cupboardt" continued the girl. "That's the putele," said Deepe. "Now. do let me think. I've gone through no many tight places that I ,mnriot see why I should not get you into that cupboard without any body knowing. Once there, however, you roust stay the whole night long." "As if I minded that. But bow am I to get there?" "That's the queatica," said Arnold. "It would be very awkward, too, if you were dis- covered." . . .</p>
        <p>"I shan't be. Why should they go to the cupboard to-night. The old man keeps mt of his w•rdrob• there, but there is only one garment he will need in the future—his winding "Meet." "Come, come," said Deepe' "don't be too morbid. Now, give me a few minutes I. 'hick." . .</p>
        <p>..... s. Mary eat down quietly by the fire. She had • woman's iprick intuition, and was 1101 in the least serupulous. Nevertheless, hoe to get into that cupboard, from to act as eavesdropper on the momentous occasion which was so likely to occur, baffled even her wits. Deeps suddenly raised his bead. "Who sleeps ill the room at the other side of the old Man!" .</p>
        <p>"No owe," eald Mary at once. "There in just • landing and a window." "The back of Ow cupboard," said Deg*, "most be flush with the wall of the land- ing." "I suppose so; I have never thought about . _ .</p>
        <p>"Can we not get to the cupboard from the landing?" was toe nest remark. "Hew woold that be possible? Every mond would be heard. We should haw to remove the paper and probably open the wall."</p>
        <p>"Leave it to me," said Deepe. "The old nian won't tell what he has to tell until the morning. I knot: the ways of the dying. You go to your bed as usual. Let the who house disperse to rest, but let me stay. here. Keep your clock going. At three 111 the morning come out •ild meet me on the land- ing. I believe I shall have managed a way to get you into the cupboard. When within, however, can you push back the lock?" "Yes; easily. It is • very poor lock, and only requires judicious pressure to open when I apply it. The cupboard is alwaye kept just on the hasp. I can open it an</p>
        <p>"Well, I believe we shall succeed; but I won't reveal my plans. Come out of your room at three. If I fail, I fail; but I don't think it likely that I shall."</p>
