      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CHAPTER IV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>Some of the most active, some of the busiest of men make up their minds very quickly when death stares them in the face. Resolute, uncomplaining, nteadfaat, courage- ous all their days, they do not lose their courage in face of the grim foe. On the con- trary, they fold their arm* calmly and, with out a particle of fear, or even regret, meet the inevitable.</p>
        <p>Da the very first night after Robert Hen- shall's illness he had made up his mind that tie moat die. There came a few days of swift and terrible pain; there came clouded hours when he could only speak the random words of delirium, and then there followed a great peace and the assurance that the River of Death was full in tight, and that all worldly affairs, an far 414 he, Robert llenshall, was concerned, were at an end. He must leave his prosperous, commercial life; he must say good-bye to that fortune which he had piled up. honestly—moat honestly—but with such pains, such years of toil. Even the diamond which lay in its little cane over his heart and which was, he felt, very nearly hitt own pm, session (for he knew nothing of the letter waiting for him downstalri) must never realise for him the enormous sum which he felt he could get for it. • '• '" ••</p>
        <p>But none of these things really mattered. Death is s strange leveller, and somehow the man who is dying seem but little diffe- rence between riches and poverty, between the life of the very prosperous, and the life of those who fail; between rank and low des- cent. All thesis things are measured at their true worth. and the dying man is content that such should be the case. He is gradu- ally loosening his moorings-4o revert to an Id metaphor—and the process is scarcely painful. There wan only one thing that troubled Robert Ilenshall when he lay a-dying, and that was the thought of leaving Rebecca; for somehow Rebecca—thin brilliant, beautiful. gifted child of his—belonged to a part of him very far removed from the worldly part. Was she not his treasure, the light of his eyes? Was idle not the very soul of him? It was true that Ile would leave her well off, but somehow, he could not think much of money then. He would leave his darling without himself, lie must go away, and she inset stay behind. Nothing greatly mat- tered except this. He wished that he could take her Ath him.</p>
        <p>to Rebecca, her calmness during her father's illness astonished even Mary, sub knew how devoted the pair were to each other. She shared the nurse's duties, and in- sisted on sleeping on a small bed in her father's sick-room. That room was now befit in exquisite order, and all that could be done to lengthen Robert Ilenshall's life was accomplished. But death is a sure foe, and advances steadily when he has made up his mind to claim his victim.</p>
        <p>One of the first things Retirees had done was to take her father'. correepondenea and lock it up until such time as he either re- covered or died. She knew that all impor- tant business letters would go to hie city ad- dress, but his private letters were there for him to read when he was well, or for her to do what eke willed with.</p>
        <p>There came a day after the old man had been ill nearly a fortnight which denoted a serious change. Mr. Ilenshall himself spoke to the doctor. All the delirium had left him; the fever had gone. Ile was angering front weakness, however, of so extreme a type that his heart might atop at any moment.</p>
        <p>..... "How long do you give me?" said lieu- hull, speaking to the medical man. "Perhaps until morning," was the reply. "Thank you," maid Ilenshall. Rebecca was in the room. Her father turned his dying eyes and fixed their, with a wistful longing on her face. She replied with a steady glance, full of love and cour- age. . . . .</p>
        <p>-"Tile doctor went upstairs. Mary, who bad to all appearance got over her disappoint- ment, met him an usual in the hall. "What news of the dear patient, doctor?" "I grieve to tell you, Miss Gray, that my poor friend is dying. If he sees the morning it is about as much as he will do. I shall look in again before midnight. No, Miss Gray; nothing will save him. His heart is past recovery and there are serious symptom', of blood poisoning, caused by the sharp at- tack of pneumonia which ha has just hued through. ' "Then there is no hope?" said Mary. "None; but he is a good, brave man, not afrsid to meet his Maker; sod what a splen- did girl Miss flenshall is Mary was not in the mood to hear Rebecca praised. When the doctor had gone she moved, eat-like, about the house. Her feel- inga during the last fortnight had been in- describable. They were perhaps all the more bitter because she was forced to keep them to herself.</p>
        <p>After a few minutes, however, she put on her hat and jacket, and went softly out. She sent a telegram to Arnold Deepe, The words of the telegram were these: "Monahan not expected to live until morn- ing. 'Have you anything you want me to dot" Deepe received thin telegram on his return to his comfortless lodginge. By this time he had .Mary completely in his power. She had given up all idea of !securing the two thou- sand pounds and giving her lover up. By so doing, she woold work with Rebecca, not against her. She hated Rebecca now on fer- vent:), that her wile desire was to injure her. Deepe arrived at the home in Paddock-row between eight and nine o'clock. Mary herself opened the door to him. "1 was waiting in the hall," she said. .1 heard your step. Come in softly; there is a lire in the dining-room." Deepe obeyed. "Here is something hot for you to drink," said the girl, "and I have saved you • plate of meat and vegetables." . . . . .</p>
        <p>"That'. rigid," he said, beginning to eat and drink eagerly.</p>
        <p>Mary did not speak at all until he had finished. When Deeps had drained his glass to the last drop, he sank into a chair by the fire, spread out his long limbs to the warmth. and looked up at the girl. "So he's about to shuffle?"</p>
        <p>"YOU know where the diamond Mt" "Yen."</p>
        <p>"Why won't you tell me. Mary mine?" "There is no IMP is better for one to hold s secret than two.'</p>
        <p>Deepe looked round the room. "What about the old man'n lettere during him illne..? What about that letter from Teherant"</p>
        <p>"Rebecca ;gave directions about the pest the day alter her return," said Mary; ' and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>There was a great hush over the house in Paddock Row. In the midst of that hush might hit, been seen a man stealing softly in stockinged feet upstairs. The man carried in his hand a small dark lantern. Ile paused just outside the sick males room. If he were seen, if for any reason the nurse or Rebecca came out, all would be lost.</p>
        <p>But he had tle daring which belongs t, great criminals. lie vary soon discovered the °tact spot which must be the back of the cupboard. To his relief, he found that hi. task e.g less difficult than he had feared. klary had spoken of the trouble of remo•ing the paper, but there was no paper to re• move. The wall was wainscotted. "Mope silently, and with knowledge which he had gained, no matter how, managed to loosen a panel. Having done this, he found nothing between him and the cupboard but a thick sheet of canvas, lie slit that open noise- lessly, pushed the panel gently in its place, put out the lantern, and waited for Mary te appear. . . . .</p>
        <p>here was a church near by. The church cvvned a clock. The clock struck them I times, and, true to her promise, Mary Dray appeared, the nioutilight falling over her. Her teeth were chattering. She wan very cold and very frightened. Without giving her time to speak or make the most remote sound, Deepe removed the panel, showed her the slit in the paper, •na. in fact, pushed her into the cupboard. lie then pushed- the panel back again, went downstairs as quietly as he had come up, and a moment later was out of the house and on hie way to his own miser- able rooms.</p>
        <p>The man wee elated. He was trembling with excitement lie gave no thought, either, as to how Mary Aolliti escape from the cup- board. He had got her in, she was clever enough to find hcr own way out. She would obtain the knovilcdge. She would make all his plane in the future tensible. He imagined himself already on the pinnacle of wealth. Meanwhile, as 13,,epe had said --for he was • man of vaned experiences- Henshall moved restkesly. He turned his eyes uponl his daughter. "I feel stronger," he said. "I am better. Can you manage to put another pillow uncles my head? I know lam dying. but I shall, not die just yet. I have much to say In you." Rebecca did what her father required. She had • strong stimulant it, a medicinv bottle near by. She gave him • dove, whir he swallowed almost with eagerneea. "Ab," he said, "this will keep me going for . bit. Becky, I should like to be quits acme with you. Send none to her room !co little."</p>
        <p>Rebecca gave the necessary orders. an Nene Prescott west away. It was lust at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday July 22, 1911.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 22, 1911.</p>
        <p>the very moment when she was leaving the room that Mary, within the cupboard, rise," the opportunity to push hack the hasp inside the door and open it a quarter of an inch. Henshall was now, to all appear- ances, alone with Rebecca, but Mary could hear every word. Rebecca knelt down by her father's side. She kept her face within a few inches of his, imd her firm, cool, strong young hand lay upon.one.hl his...</p>
        <p>."Oh! it'll could only give you some of n 1 youth and strength!" she said. "1-1 don t want you to leave Inc. If you must go—oh, that I could go to!" "No," my darling," he answered. "I did wish for a little that much might be the ease, but I have changed my mind. You are young and must live your life. Becky, you will find all my affairs in perfect order. You will be • rich woman, my dearest—not that money '' • •</p>
        <p>woman like you it means a great deal. If means that you eau help others, that you can do a lot of good. Becky, there are a few things that I must say to you. Are you listening?" "With all my heart and with all my soul,'' was the answer.</p>
        <p>"After I am gone," said the old man, you will be good to Mary Gray. She has been with us for some yearn. Give her a little money, if necessary, and if she breaks off her engagement to that scoundrel Arnold Deepe. you might tel her have a couple of thousand pounds. But not otherwise—you promise?" "I promise," said Rebecca. "If she insists on marrying him, she will be fearfully unhappy," said the old man. "But women are generally infatuated when they imagine themselves in love, even with • worthless fellow like that. You might give her a trousseau, however. You will be kind to her?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, father. Certainly, father." "But I have one request to make." "What is that, dearest'!" "She in not to stay with you; she is to leave you. I don't trust her." "Nor do I trust her, father." (How Mary Gray shook with rage as alto listened to these words.) "But we needn't talk about her now," continued Rebecca, speaking as soothingly as she could. "Have a little more of your restorative. You are gelling weak and tired. I know you hare other things to tell me. Let Mary Gray alone " With some difficulty ilenshall managed to swallow amither spoonful of the strong resto- rative. Then he said, almost with eager.</p>
        <p>" You are right; I have something of much more importance to say. Rebecca, my child, there is a little black ribbon fastened round my neck." "The ribbon that holds your talisman? You never would tell me what it was," said Rebecca. "I used to fancy that the talis- inns was perhaps a lock of my mother's</p>
        <p>"Ah, no, child ; not that. It hoe nothing to do with your mother. But listen ; lam weaker than I thought." Ilia voice dropped almost to a whisper. Mary, in her rage and curiosity, pushed the cupboard door open a little further. "I will tell you an briefly as I can, Becky, my dear. The ribbon round my neck has fastened to it a little case. Within the case is a diamond." . . .</p>
        <p>"A:diamond, father?" "Yes; not an ordinary gem, by any mesas. The moat valuable diamond, per - Naps, in the whole of England -one of the most precious, in the world. I have had its value appraised by a man who known all about gems, and I could at any moment sell tlie diamond for eighty to ninety Gunman,' poinids. I will now tell you, dear, exactly how it Caine into my possession. You may remember, Rebecca, that time when I was away from you in Persia?" "I remember," said Rebecca, gently. "I was at school, and I missed you so dread. fully. You were away for a year." "Yes; I was amassing a considerable for- tune in Persian carpets and silk. I was a rich man even then. All my toils and my de. sire to make money were caused by my love for hear darling!" said the girl, cared for money!" "My child, it sometimes comes to one as a Lift. To one like you, money can be a great, an inestimable blessing. Now, my dear, I want you to unfasten the black rib bou. Move it gently round till you come to the knot. Don't shake me much, for lam very, very weak. Unfasten it now, my dear, muf•tie the ribbon round your own neck and let the little ease which contains the diamond rest •gainnt TOUT own heart. No one in all the world will know that you are wearing it. And, Becky, you must wear it day and night, day and night, for a short tunic longer. lon must promise me." "I promise." "Do it then, Becky; do it at once before I die. Don't let the nurse see the ribbon, and, above all things, don't let Mary Gray see the ribbon. Sometimes I feel afraid of Mary Gray—not that she can poesibly know." Rebecca, without an mstant'a hesitation, unfastened the piece of black ribbon, re• moved it from her father's neck, and tied it securely round her own. "Now it in all right," she said to the dying man; "and the wonderful, precious diamond rests against my heart ; and—be sure of one thing—l will wear it day and night as long az it is necessary." "I will tell you in a few words about bow I came by it, Becky. When I was in Persia I spent some months at Teheran. Towards the end of my stay I came across a very at- tractive young Englishnian, whose name was Gerard Lonsiale. He was in a mercantile house, and was doing a little business on hue own account. Ile was a particularly fresh, pleasant, honourable young fellow. But he got mixed sip with the Armenia.. Ar- menians abound in Teheran; and one day Lonsdale came to me in considerable difficul- ties. In short, lie wanted five hundred pounds to get himself out of a serious scrape. Nothing dishonourable, nothing at all; but one of the Armenian merchants had cheated him and would have sold the poor fellow up and lost him his position if be could not got immediat• financial aid. I never saw anyone in such despair. Ile told me then that he had a mother at home to whom he made a monthly allowance and who wax almost it. pendent on him for her livelihood. Ile asked me quite frankly if I would advance him the money. Now, my dear Becky, lam not a man given to impulae, and I asked the bov --for he was only a little over twenty—if had any security to offer. Ile put bin hand into his pocket and produeed the diamond, which is now resting against your heart. Ile said that he had not the least idea of its value, but that he had bought it the day be- fore from a Permian who had offered it to him for a small sum. Lonsdale said that if he were in England he believed he could sell the gem for a considerable amount, but 00111 a make no use of it whatever at Teheran. Ile asked me if I would accept the diamond as security for the fire hundred pounds. I have a keen eve for precious stones, and knew at a glance 'that it was worth a vast lot more than BO money the poor fellow needed. I had never seen any stone quite so beautiful in all my life before. The light that issued from it was dazzling, and there was a rose glow about it which I have never noticed be- fore en any diamond.. - .</p>
        <p>"as if I</p>
        <p>"I Immediately made terms with Lonadale. They were thew,. That I was to lead him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>Limn. and then, pve nn.nd7,n ponnu!,</p>
        <p>• h wan to take the diamond with me to England and keep it in safety. If Lansdale did not write or come to claim the gem within seven years from the exact date when I gave him the money the diamond was to become my property; but if he returned before the seven yearn were up and gave me my capital beck with five per cent interest. I was to restore him the diamond. His tact ivords to me were, 'I will write to you often during the seven yearn. Mr. Ilenshall; if you don't hear, you will conclude that I am dead, and will regard the gem ftc your private property.' "Since that day I only received one letter from him, in which he told me that his mother wan dead, that lie had no other near relations, and that he was doing fairly well. It in six years since I had that letter. On the twentieth of February the seven yearn will have expired and the diamond will be. long to me. You are then at liberty to sell it, Rebecca, for what you can get for it. Yon mast take it to Montague in Bond-street. He is a special friend of mine, and Inc will give you a large sum for the atone. If by any chance Lansdale turns up between now and the twentieth of February you will restore him the diamond, provided he gives you back five hundred pounds with interest. "That in nII, my child. I triad you. 'flint diamond has been, since I have micertained its value, a burden to me. I have been afraid of it. I have not dared even to pat it in the bank, but have uorn it about my rr-, min, trusting that no one would discover its wherealmuts. You will be faithful in this matter, my child. I can trust you abso- /Orly."</p>
        <p>lon can—alwilotely," wax the reply. Then Rebecca said, after a long panne : " wineecely hope that Mr. lainadale will come back. I do not want the money that the dia- mond will fetch." . . . .</p>
        <p>. ..... But, old Hennl.ll was too tired to make any reply to thin npeeell. Fainter and fainter Irew 1114 breathing. Although Rebecca trod to give him some more of the atimulant, he could not nwallow it. girl left the room in a hurry to call Nurse Prenrott. Now wan Mary's opportunity. Quick no thought she darted from her hiding Oiler and flew up to the side of the speechless old man. "I know your twernt!" she .aid, "and what yon think of me. it me tell YOU that your daughter ía in danger. Alt----! hate you an munch as I hate her!' "</p>
        <p>Footsie'. mild fron we roomre hear.d approaching. Mary aet tho • •</p>
        <p>When Rebecca and the nurre came bark old Ifenshall was only gasping sighs, a look of distress tintitteruble on his face. lie tried to speak. but in vain. A few minutes later he was dead.... .</p>
