      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King and the flighlands..--.1 he King most be longing for bin holiday in the Highlands aft°, all the Coronation ceremonials end rush of social gaieties daring the sesson,and hi. Mejesty is to peg a wiles of shooting •isits before going to Balmoral. One is expected to be to Ihe Alsekintosh of Mackintosh at May flail, near Inverness, where the King, on neve.' occlusions before to came to the Throne, has had excellent sport on the moors. The Mrickintosh cleims to he chief of the Clan C.hatten, which figures in the desperate contest dencribed in The Nair Maid of Perth," but the title hie's° claimed by Cluny,the chief of the Mao Phmsons. 11th those magnates use the mine crest, a wildest with the motto," Touch not the cat but with the glove." To the Southron, it seems a little at, ng to omit the " Mister " when addressing either chief, hut whilst Mackintosh is correct, his wife is Mrs. Mackintosh—the daughter of a wealthy South Wales conlowner.</p>
        <p>The Passing of the Milkinald.—Women of the present day have taken top many new occupation., hut some of the old ones are going, and among them is that of the milkmaid. in many parte of the country it in said there, is an absolute dearth of inilkinsids' and iho milking merlin., is ready to take her place. Illachines for this purpose have been on the market for ninny years past, but they have been greatly improved, end those an view at the various Agi it ulturel dhows, claim lobe as near perfection es porsiblo. An expert milkmaid one milk 30 or ,10 rows in two or throe limn., and a machine an inquired for every ten much row, costs about Ll:i. There seem. little doubt that it will eventually carte piesturestre figure from the m untry side, whom Milton, and the peel., have held up to our admiration. tree it In not many towns-folk have seen the maid whore face wen her fortune, but none the lase, everyone mast regret her pasting into the limbo of the forgotten pact. The Ineritation of Flarriage.—A French judre, in dealing e ith a breach of pronibm CARP, hes been cote. meeting upon changing women, and nemesia that the time is coming when the hal itution of marriage will le conducted with butt little of its old romance and senti- ment. According to long-established custom, families encourege young people to "get to know each other," and if this ends In a proposal, the girl hen to pretend that she deer not want to marry, but sweetly giveaway. Tke yen mania then told by his pro,pective father-in- law that he must be Inn position to marry, and.nooord. Mg to the French judge, it it the long welting, often incidental to thin advice, that is most productive of breech of promise Bal.!. All this, we are told, in futile sentiment and decit, and that such mitten should be the subject of plain bargaining, and a strati n con. tract, with royalties and daniegeo for breaking It, stated beforehand. Such ideas, must, of course, shoots old-fashioned people, but we line in changing time., and it in perfectly true that I he modern girl, denoted to gamer, out-door sports, and the strenuous, self-reliant life, Is losing much of her sentimentality and mese, sled love of romance.</p>
        <p>.„.- Holiday Packing.—Pecking for the holidays present. , fetter difficulties this year than of yere. Fabrics generelly are light in weight and holiday °Wows are akiropy and o little trimmed that they ~ilj go into small compass, and are ...Sy method. So that where.o in the old days we wanted two trunks one will now euffice. It in true we can hardly may the earns of hat hose., which in some cases almost reach to the dimensions of a crate" for each hat But for holiday weer the average traveller finds it better to leave the hogs feather-trimmed hat at home, nod with a decided inclination to don smaller hats, we ought to have less trouble with the carriage of such item., and the peck- ing of robe. and wrap• no that they may emerge fresh and uncrossed at the end of a j othey, ahould be a comparatively rely task. The Provincial Royal Tons--The King and Queen, in paying State visits to the capitals of !Wend and Scotland, are follossine the example net p King Edward and Queen Alexandre, in the early part of the lest reign. Queen Victoria had been many years on the throne before she paid her first visit to the Irish people In 1019, but the late King accompanied by Queen Alexandra, paid three official visits to Ireland, the first, of which was iu July, 1903. On the present °caution there le an important addition to the programme of provincial Court CPT flilolliPP, and arrangement., namely, the investiture of the lirince of Wales taut week at Carnation Castle. The Pri.e has had no email part to pley In the Coronation cerernoniels, but with these over, the King bin made it known that he does nut wish him to ondettake any publics engagerneota out il halo of age. When the proper time comes, Ilia Majesty intende to revive for bin younger sone, the lio3al dukedoms of Kent, Sussex and Gloucester. The law of England confers a special status only upon the eldest son end danghter of the Sovereign, Including the eldest eon's wife, and the King's younger none, although called " Prince.," are atrickly speaking, commcnere, until raised to the peerage. The Eternal Femioine. At the Univernity College of Egyptian antiquities, may be seen various relic. which show that the lollies of fashion are not all of yesterday. There has lat. ly heeau added a series of portraits of the Roman period, painted on cedar panels moat of them hy WNW/ of molten coloured wax, IMd on hy a broth. They nhow that there lived two thourand years ago, much the some varied types of women as Paint to-day. There was the refined educated woman, with her narrow face, nod the coarse featured woman with her super- fluity of adornment, so much •ffected by the 'Arriet of the present day. Then there are fresco paintingu of still mote ancient times which show that the Greek wowen of 2,000 years Ii C , were tight laced at their waists, that they had flounced or accordion pleated skirts, no elaborate ooiffore, and hats and high heeled shoe., that might have come hum the most modern and most fashionable Parisian establiahment.</p>
        <p>Bargain. In bale !anti—Clime on the hrels of the Coronation have come the Summer sales, and these to many women are ae exciting an that great event. Shopping stood &amp;lama ate stendtill during the de). of</p>
        <p>the Coronation, but it has been resumed with greater zest than ever now, and never were there 'teeter bargains, or • wider choice of rt idly I end iftd garments at ridiculously low pricer.. The allure who Loose rant to buy may now gain: r a ticli buricet, and lay in Meeks that will .last till tire July Salen cone round egain. Unfortunately oreiyi.no is not ii, oh size, it thorns who are, bare a wonderful choice of coma end skirts, lovely•ftennoon hoar, delicious blouses, end dainty lingerie, nut to tneotion the many acoeseor tea, such as scarves, ii ckv.tar, lege, fie and In there letter all can have • abate. 'the real bed:bone of eve.; I. hoe/ewer the ermy of piece goods, that fill the counter. to overflowing, and here it is aria the r•ver and tinder stock-aloe l whereona will flnd treasure trove. In fors, the big horese• are o Poling menial bargaine,nird ▪ purohase made now, will nut be regretted later on. Beads Populer.—lt ts not easy to account for the position that beads hold in the realm of dm.. They have Invaded every &amp;I:ono-mut, and will moreover, continue to hold tie place they have won. Therefore the lovely pieces of colouted embroidery that can be bought no cheaply at the sales, will prove • good invest. mad, for outlining the pattern In gold, or coloured beads is Interesting work end will add greatly to the value of the embroidery as a trimming later on. The Old-lace Ilue—Vast queniltle. of cream and ecru lam are being offered in etleland, but the wiser choice Is • deep ivory, as nearly as poeiblo re:trembling real old lace. Such a choice le of the greatest possible imports°ce to tie women past her 3 °nth, to whom white, or este crest', lace ahem the neck end yoke is altogether undesirable. happily the light-hued lee. or net yoke bas teat ppeared, it having been ti Wen ye etc] I bet few Inc.., however (teeth and young, look their last in an all dark puttee r e bodice. . . .</p>
        <p>Care of the tomplexion.—Wh .1. with coretant motor- ing. sod all the sports girls and women nowadays Indulge In, complexions ana not what they used to be. Much may be done however, to stave off the worst effects or sure and wind, by auc..h simple procedures ati • buttermilk fa..bath, alter hours out of door.; gentle masaagi, with olive Oil, or • mixture of yolk of egg and 10IPWItiff• This latter in made as follow, a—Take two yolks of egge, beat well, with an egg capful of rose- water, and brittle. The mixture will keep good for three or four &amp;yr. For freckle', which are no t rouble. 1.1110 pint now, potato jitice is invaluable, while another homely cure for the trouble, is ri paste made of calcined magma. and lemon juice. Thit should be spread on the face at night, the backs 01 the hands being likewi. I leftist!, if sunburnt.</p>
