      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, N.P.</p>
        <p>WILL THE INSURANCE BILL PASS.</p>
        <p>The Insurance Bill is going through Corn-' mitten much more smoothly than might have been expected, thanks to the indirect closure which has been imposed by the terms of the Financial Resolution, which has wiped out pages of amendments which aimed at trying to get more money out of the State. The Chancellor of the Exchequer is far and away the smartest man on Vie Treasury bench at piloting a Bill through Committee. He is personally popular among all parties, which is a great asset; he has tact and amiability ; and on occasions he can be humorous and, if necessary, ironical. If any man can get this Insurance Bill through Parliament Mr. Lloyd George will do it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>BRIGHTER CHANCES.</head>
        <p>BRIGHTER CHANCES.</p>
        <p>The chances in the House of Commons of the Bill going through are brighter than they were a fortnight ago. But it cannot be said that the measure is becoming more popular in the country. Mr. Lloyd George has been extremely anxious to get through the Bill as quickly as possible. If his object is to pass it without any thought of the after conse- quences, he is doubtless right to do this rushing. For there can be no doubt that I opposition to the Bill, as it stands, is grow- ing rapidly in the country. When it was first introduced it was welcomed with such shouts of approval from all quarters I that those who were not very sure about it thought it best to keep silent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>GROWING OPPOSITION</head>
        <p>GROWING OPPOSITION</p>
        <p>But in the two months since it was printed criticism has been brought to bear upon its provisions from innumerable quarters and points of views, and the result is that there is not one interest which is not raising opposition to some parts of it. The doctors, the chemists, the midwives, the nurses, the herbalists, the insurance COM - panics, the women, the friendly societies, the trade unions, the Labour organisations, the Socialists the employers, and many other bodies too numerous to mention are all up in arms against some one or other of the pro- visions of the Bill.</p>
        <p>The doctors have shown a unanimity of opposition which is remarkable even in a profession which has never been lacking in looking after its professioryil interests. The negotiations with the Chancellor by the representatives of the medical men are said to be going on satisfactorily, and may eventuate in an agreement. But lithe views of the individual doctors which one meets are to prevail, then the Bill is doomed, for unless the hearty co-operation of the medical men can be secured the Act could not possibly work satisfactorily. The chemists are up against the suggestion of contract payment, and they seem to have a good case which it may not be difficult to meet. THE MEDICAL BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>A very important point has arisen as to medical benefit. It is now very generally agreed that the Bill will have to be amended so as to give the patient the free choice of a doctor, but it is not clear that any other class of medical practitioner but the allopathic physician will be recognised. There are many folly qualified medical men who do not practice that treatment, and the question has cropped up as to whether the patient shall not only have the choice of a doctor, but the choice of method of treatment also. The anti-vaccinators and the anti-vivisectionists are very seriously disturbed about the Bill; because it lays down that a person receiving medical attendance must do exactly as the doctor tells him or lose his benefit, and as most doctors believe in vaccination and serum treatment it is feared that many conscientious objectors to vaccination and vivisection will be forced to submit to things they do not approve. These are samples of the innumer- able difficulties which have cropped up in the detailed consideration of the Bill. WOMEN OBJECTORS.</p>
        <p>No section' Of -objectors to the -Bill have so , serious grounds for complaint as the women. It is very gratifying to see them so vigorously protesting against the way in which they are treated under the Bill. A great protest meeting was held last Saturday in London, attended by thousands of industrial women and housewives to express their indignation, and to demand amendments which will give them better terms. Other meetings ot women with the same object have been held almost daily of late. The Anti-Sweating League is organising protest meetings to ask for the fairer treatment of industrial women who are earning very low wages.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY OPPOSITION. Very strong opposition is coming from many of the Friendly Societies to some of the provisions of the Bill, and they are all asking for amendments which will, if they are granted, completely alter the character of the measure. Many of the Friendly Societies are circularising Members of Parliament asking them to oppose the Bill unless it is very materially altered in many respects. It in difficult to see how the Friendly Society man is going to gain much under this Bill.; These societies certainly deserve every con- sideration, and it would be a thousand pities if the Act were to interfere with the good work they have been doing, without at least</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>TO MAKE THE CHILDREN HAPPY.</head>
        <p>TO MAKE THE CHILDREN HAPPY.</p>
        <p>FORTIFY THEM AGAINST DISEASE</p>
