      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Wevococt</head>
        <p>Wevococt</p>
        <p>-11, mak, all the ,Itift,renee. Every gine, Neils Vi-Cocoa in I. packets and 9d. and l&amp; lid, tins.</p>
        <p>doing more tor the workers than these societies have done. It seems certain that the progress of the voluntary p.trt of the work of these Friendly Societies will be arrested if the Bill becomes law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>TRADE UNIONS.</head>
        <p>THE TRADE UNIONS.</p>
        <p>The Trade Unions are now waking up on , this Bill. Every day Trades Councils and Trade Union branches are passing resolutions either demanding the withdrawal of the Bill or asking for very drastic amendments. It is perfectly clear that the recent Conference on the Bill which was held in London under the auspices of the Joint Board of the Trade Unions and the Labour Party represented only the official mind of the movement. The Scottish Trades Union Congress Parliamen- tary Committee has issued a Manifesto demanding that the Scheme shall be Non- contributory, and the funds provided wholly by Parliament. That means they are opposed to the principle on which this Bill is based. The Fabian Society, which is entitled to be heard upon such a question as this, has passed a resolution asking for the with- drawal of the Bill on the ground of its unsatisfactory character. Objection is taken to the contribdtory principle, to the poll tax on workmen, to the Post Office contributory section; and to several other points. The Society, in conjunction with the Independent Labour Party, is organising protest meetings at which the withdrawal of the Bill is demanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EMPLOYERS</head>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS</p>
        <p>The opposition of the employers is now beginning to find expression. Chambers of Commerce are passing resolutions demand- ing very drastic amendments. As a sample of the way in which the employers are look- ing ink.) the effect of this Bill upon their trades mention may be made of a very interesting letter in this week's NATION signed "A Lancashire Liberal." In this letter the writer relates his own business experience in the cotton trade and shows that the Bill will tax the profits of his firm up to Bs. in the on the average profits of the last ten years. This is a far worse case than the one I quoted in the House of Commons last week, where the burden, if it remained upon the employer would represent an income of 2s. in the .C. ft is the unfair- ness of imposing the burden on employers in a way which puts the heaviest burden on the employer in the most useful trades which is causing so much dissatisfaction. A PECULIAR POSITION.</p>
        <p>The peculiarity about the public attitude to this Bill is that everybody is in sympathy with the object. We should all like to see something done to help the working class in time of sickness and adversity. This feeling makes one hesitate to criticise the Bill unduly for fear that one's motives may be misunderstood. Nobody wants to kill the Bill if it can be amended into a Bill which will be a really beneficial one. The general feeling was very well expressed the other day by a man who said he could not make up his mind whether the Bill was 60 per cent, good and 40 per •vnt. bad, or 40 per cent, good and 60 per cent. bad. In view of the perplexity of the Bill, and the public feeling towards it, it would probably be the best thing if the Bill mild be withdrawn for the present until the country has had more time in which to get to thoroughly under- stand it.</p>
        <p>CB For Cakes, Pastry, Pkv:clings &amp; PIM; ORWICK BAKING POWDER.</p>
