        <p>(Applause., Having mentioned the facts that •lbletee, eniployers end labourers were now in favour of total abstinence the rev. gentleman said that they had also captured social customs, which was the greatest capture of all. When he commenced his ministry in eight out of ten toes. he want to, it sews always the prow thing to offer the minister or the visit., a gime of wine. Prior to going into the pulpit the ministers War! generally offered a glass of fort n ine—(leughter)—and they could always tell whether the sermon was con- sidered good or not according to whether another glass was offered after the service. (Renewed laughter) Also there wee almost always alcoholic drinks on the table for dinner. lint during the last !nor years in the bundred• of horn.. be had been to In had never term alcoholic drinks on the lel la except n fire occasicn (Loud applause.) And also grist thanks were doe t our late King who said that to drink his health in water was quite as acceptable to bins as if It were drool in wine. (Applause.) The Trade need to say they were going to a; end millions in their defence, but they bad not got it to spend now. When egked some time nco as to how the liquor traffic could best he defended he said they shcold try to</p>
