      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>INIVRE into IN A Moron A.. 11.00 r —Arthur C.Whitcey LI•erpool, wan summoned at Cheater on Saturday for di iving a motor car in a meaner ongerotto to the public on June 23r01,in hook-road. Defendant pleaded not guilty and was represented by Mr.t:rimro.het Liver- pool. Superintendent lloole explained that Mr.VVhitney Was driving in the direction of Frodsham, and whew near the junction of Here-lone end Ifoole-road he met a Mr. Lengley,of Stockton Heath, who wee driving to motor cycle with a side car, which contained his wife land chill. Defendant WAS driving in the centre of the rood end Mr. Langley was on his proper side. TOMB- diatt,ly opposite Ilare-lane, ecrendant, it was alleged, turned bis one evil to go into the late. Mr. Lengley tuned to bin off hide. Dotendant then went to the straight again, and Mr. l.ang'ey to avoid a collision turned to the right out of hie way, and his cycle end hide oar were. overturned, himself and h:s wife and child being thrown out. Defendant did all be could end took Mr. Langley to a doctor. —Evidence was given bearing cut this stamen-- Mr. Clrindrod raid that Mr. Whitney'. case war Om! it was Mr. Iwngley who appeared lobe turning to go down the lane, and to ••oid him defendant pulled up so moddeoly that his car skidded right *cross the rosd. If it had not been for defendant's pri senor of mind a more seer i ono accident would have happened.—Defendont gave evidence, and said tint both he and Langley blew their horns. If whom had not swerved to avoid colliding he could not Lave helped killing Mr. Langley Miss Wording, who was in the car at the time, gave similar e•idence.—Defendant was fitted £5 and cock,</p>
        <p>WE,LEY AN SI'SDAY Scum-ft.—The snood slimmer treat in connection with this school was held in Thursday when over 2CO scholars, teachers and friends spent a pleasant day at Overton Hills, Frudsham. The scholars assembled at the schoolroom at 8 a.m. and proceeded on lorries dc. kindly lent for the cccasion, being immediately followed by two large 'buses con. taining the parents and friends. The journey through I the beautiful country wse much enjoyed, the refreshing rains of the previous night hiving added to the comfort of the conditions. Overton was reached about 11 o'clock, when the children were provided with bane end tee, after which they quickly dispersed, seeking the many enjoyments to be found at thls popular pleasure resort. Some of the friend@ visited th• caves, others climbed decd.', Ladder, erhilet climbing the hills provided amusement for the l children. Gimes of bowls, rounders, end cricket were I greatly enjoyed ; the young ladies entering heartily in these games with the gentlemen. At o'clock, tee wee served in the room. provided by Men. Parker Hoes*, Mersey View. The return journey was commenced at 7 o'clock, Stockton Heath being safely reached about 930. Tbe weather, though threatening at times, was all that could be desired, and the party were delighted with the date outing. PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above was held in the 11,11 Cale on Tbursday evening. The chair was occupied by Mr F Pennon, and there were also present r—Slesers J AlcDermid, J Jfallowr, J Whitley, II J Westbrook, W Mcktyre, F Rose, J Saintou leuroe3 or) and W Warburton (assistant clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>FIRE HELL.</head>
        <p>FIRE HELL.</p>
        <p>A letter woe read from the Chief Constable of Cheshire in reply toe letter from the Cleik, Raking for twrmiion to erect the fire hell on the new police build- ings now in the coolinG erection. Ile stated that YO had referred the matter to the County Surveyor, end that after consideraCon he regretted to try that he Cr old not ree his way to comply with the rertmat. It was decided to refer the matter to the lire Appli- atoms Committee.</p>
        <p>W E,•,T V _,T</p>
        <p>The Clerk also rend a further letter from the Chief Engineer of the Marx heater Ship Canal Co., with regard to the Committee payirg half the cost of a catch pit which the Company proposed to erect in the syphon at the Treaty Map Lock. Toe Committee bad replied to Isis letter stating that they could not see their way to pay half the coat, hut that they would raise no objection to cne heing erected in accordauce with the previous agreement. The Chief Engineer replied that he regrett. d the attitude arrived at in their decision. It vas derided to forward a further letter referring to the original agreements.</p>
        <p>Penketh. LABOUR P., WM—Open-sir meetings were held on the recreation ground on Monday and 'Tuesday evenings commening at 7.45 each evening, under the auspices of the above pert, who had the une of the yellow mu of the Land Nationalimt ion Society, Lancashire Branch. The speaker on Monday evening was Sir. Smith, of Manchester, who is better known, so he said himself, ne Smith the land grabber. Ile Sp.ic for over an hour on the objects of the society amongst uhich were to restore the land of England to the people of Englnnd ; to glisran•re to the cultimtor of the soil the results of his labour; and to secure the increased valtte in lowan nod on an districts to those who create it. Land skooldthe as free as the air ne Itrosthe.—After the speech questions were asked, one in ing `• In which way did the apeaker propose to get the ant?"l Ile would hey the land end lay for the same by government hi nen, except where it could he proved that the pergon holding the land or his forefathers had confronted it. Then in thin case he would take it away from them and give it to the rigbtlul owners, namely, the people. (Cheers.) Mr. Marshall presided.—On Tuesday evening Mr. Woolstencroft took the chair, and Mr. Thompeon, of Maachenter, wan the epeaker and dealt with the queetion in a manterly way. He proved that if the land wan owned by the State it we tild relieve the iron trade at the rate of Spec ton of finished steel In the way of mining royalties.</p>
        <p>P A RI,II (:OUN4 NCO,/ g Tr tr.—Viscount Widmer (Newton, C) recently raked the Chancellor of the Exchequer whether be is aware that Mr. J. Wood- word, of Penketh, mar Warrington, Lancashire, who returned his income for the year ending April, 11110, aa .C 99 7s. 91. was served with a demand note for lrs. 6d. income tax ; whether he is aware that Mr. Woodward wrote to Somerset House on the 19th June, 19111, com- plaining of this trectorent, and that before any reply was received Mr. Woodmid was dint raised (*or 21s 6d. ; whether he is aware that for the following year ending Aprll, 1911, Mr. Woodward, whom income bad not altered, was only asked to pay 2s. 01. income tax, and will he my what action he proposesto takein the matter. Mr. Lloyd George by written reply).-1 art informed that for the year 19011.10 an amesainent to income tax under schedule D in respect of prclits svm made on! Mr. Woodward by the district commissioners of trace in dm course. As Mr. Woodward dil not appml against the arsmarnent athis the prescribed time, the einem- meat svaa confirmed, and the duty became payable as chlrged. Tho dilly was ultimately recovered by dis- traint. For 1910-11 Mr. Woodward appealed in due time against the assessment rinds upon him, and, having satisfied the district commissioners that he was entitled to exemption, the assessment was discharged. The charge of 3s. ed. made upon him that year relate., to another matter, and is inale in respect of ground rent front which he is entitled to deduct the tax he has borm.</p>
