        <head>SPEEI. II DAY AT THE FRIENDS SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SPEEI. II DAY AT THE FRIENDS SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On 'nitre-lay the Friends' School, i-oll'e-th,Veld-its " General Meeting," °the r,vine " Speech Day," when about a hundred parents en 4 friends cam, front far and near in response to the imitation of the Headmaster and hie wife, Mr. and Mr,. W. E. Brown. : . .</p>
        <p>During the morning, lessons were given In the class- rooms and parente had the opportunity of seeing and hearing just huvr their children were receiving know• ledge in different subject. In one room about thirty children were responding to questions in French, while in anothtr a similar number of tiny one. reoitssi. spelt, 4.c. Scripture and chemistry occupied other class-rooms, though peals'u the keenert interest wee shown in the practical departments. Thin reboot warmer a capital workshop and the ho's under Mr. Malcolm are bring trained in accurate and nest carpentry. In the II Kitchen," • large well•rquipped building, boys and girls are trained in cookery, and here the girls germ •n a Imirable demonstration under the direct ion of Miss Graham. An intereeting exhibition of polirhe.l articles, laundry, : sewing, knitting, ,to, all the work of scholarapccopied the side tables, while in other Mom, drawings, map, photographs, geological specimen. , were diaplarel. Not less interesting ea, the Nature Study fllass under the tuition of Mr IV Robinson !Lim Caddis- wermr, stickleback. ,to., looked glass at borne in an aquaritim well furnished with various pondered, and grasser, sod • moat excellent collection of wild It were (90 different specimens baring been found within a radius of three miles), showed how much interest was being directed to the study of nature, and the all round development of healthy active outdoor life.</p>
        <p>lathe evening the prise. and certificates were dis- tributed by Mr. lhontaa lostarence, J.P., of Southport. It ie e•ident that a high standard of work is maintained throughout the school, and the system of co-education neema to work with minket! si.ccess. At tho same time keen interest is taken iaspic t, swimming is much to the fore, end the tine concrete pia, ground gives ample opportunity for roller skatinr. Following the p ist.giving, some well rendered noisiest item., under the tuition of Miss Marsland, hrous,ht to a close a most interesting day. Lymm. PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>' The monthly L 1 11111 l Potty &amp;radius were held on Wednesday morning at the villege police station. The j magistrates pretest were Admiral Junes Parry in the j chair), Mrj in J. E. G. Grocer, Mr. S. W. Gould, Hun. J. E. Crcas and Colonel Leigh. During the hearing of the first case the two latter gentlemen did cot act. COLONEL SERVANT CONVIeTeII or 011UNKI‘NNESS.—Grec• Wills, wife of Mr. Wills, wetchman to Colonel Legh, of High Legh, wax enrunioned for being drunk on the Ist July. Mr. T. S. Steel who represented defendant, put in a plea of not guilty.—P.C. Gauld raid that at 11.45 on the night in queetion he was called out of bed by three men who said that defendant was drunk in the road. It. went j outside and found her lying in the roeilwey by the side of her cycle which vies baiting spinst the hedge. Seeing t hat she chuld not walk properly with her bicycle, he took her hi me. On tie war she fell down three times and emelt of whiskev.—"Albert Yerwood, Fred Jones and IVlliorn Edward Alaseey, oil of Iligh I.egh, spoke to teeing defendant on the roadway, and as they considered she ...drank they inf Amid the police—Mr. Steel said that the defendint and her husband bad been to Warrington on the day in operation and all that the woman had during the day Mar two small ports It was a physical imposiihility for her to be drunk. The busliend bad left Mrs. Wills at Latchford as she wanted to go Into the town again to look at various shops and when Mrs. Wills came to get on her cycle abe fectud that it was puncturod. Not buing any money with born) as to pay to have it repaired she decided to walk to Hien Legh. Oa the way, it being very late, the wu molested during which she hurt her ankle and war very etch defeodant and her husband gave evidence bearing out Mr. Steel's statement.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 55, nod costa (18a. in rill). . _</p>
        <p>ti I,CELLANEOL'S.—SamiteI Owen was lined sa. for riding a bicycle at Lyrnru on he let July at 50 minutes poet 10 with, ut a lighted lamp attached.—William Craven, of Manchester, wen fined, like amount for. simile, offence.—Charlotte Leigh of Statham wets fined In. 01. fe acing bad language to Ellen Taylor, a neighbour. INQUESTS AT LYNIM. Inquest. were held at Ly not Co irt lions. On Satur• day and Monday by Mn. T. It &amp;away, the coroner. The (rat related to the death of Taornaa 'Sit..., a coal. beaver, whose body was founl In the Bridgewater Canal. I,ymm, no Saturdly.—Evidence or.. given to the effect that after a heavy day's work uoloading a coal boat the man ondreamai far a swim, and after crossing the canal his mole. iVillioct Whitelegg, oti sad him. lie got oh rit-hook and managed to locate the body and brought it to the •ide. Sergeant Washington was sent for and every rffirt was made to restore anlinaticn. Dr. Therm who was summoned, expressed little hope of the man's recovery, but the work of trying to revive the man was con lotted without effect. A verdict of " Found drowned" was returoed. The Coroner complimented Sergeant Waskington on his efforts to restore life. _ .</p>
        <p>The second Inquiry was totiching the death of William Oldham, aged 74, of Rush Green, Lymm.—Mrs. Alice Lowndee, of Rush Green, Lymm, a neighbour, said on July 7th she put Oldhim in a chair out of doors whilst she attended to her husband's dinner. Shortly afterwards she found Oldham on the floor near the door wae able to move shout with the aid of his crutches Ile salil hie head as. gone funny?' He died on Saturday.—Dr. 1.. Thorpe mid he WWI called in to me the man on Tumidity. Ile was told that Oldem had met with en accident to hie arm. Hie opinion, after attend- ing the man for the not three year., wan that he had died from general weakens., hmtened be the shock of the •ccitierst.—A verdict in accordance with the medical •videLoe was re' ironed.</p>
        <p>PRINIFfIvr.Mi lIOItsT Coribit, linoii'l The anniversary servics sere celebrated an this church on Sunday, when the ties K. Siokes late of Alt rincham occupied the pulpit, end preached two excellent sermon.. The service, throughout the day were bright and cheerful. Collection. were in aid of chapel funds. Cm ran Nits-dos; Bk.:D.—The open-sir service was held in Djer's-lane, Rosh Green, ou Sonday evening last, when a good number assembled. The addreesm were givin by the Ken ft Wit Whitearay and Mr Sydnoy Linley, and were much epprecisted. Mr Broome pre- sided. Nest Sonila)'• meeting will be held in Newfield.</p>
