      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Frodsham.</head>
        <p>Frodsham.</p>
        <p>LIKE i CITY The house was in a beastly insanitary condition. I cannot imagine any alum in the city much worse," eeid Ur. Selby, in his report on the house of Thomas Johnson, of Itrow Top, Froileham Bridge, stile we. charged at the Frodshem sesaionor on 1% ednesday.—According to the evidence of the Inspector of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children and Selby, the three children of the defendant were in a very dirty condi- tion.—The defendant said he could not pay enyone to look after the children since the death of his wife, and he himeelf went to work at sin o'clock in the morning until 630 at night, with a short intervel for brealfaet end dinner, and he was working the greater part of every night tel ing to keep the houne and children clean. —A tine of El was imposed, with the altarnati-e of fourteen days' imprieonment.</p>
