      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHAFE'. ANNIVERSARY. The annual anniversary sermons were preached in this place on Sunday not by the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones. of Manchester. At the morning service Mr. Jones spoke from the wores "Teaching them to do whatsoever I command you.' Ile raid Jesus -</p>
        <p>la;;;;;—it was Ills to -command but ours to obey. Obedience is a weak spot of the Church and always has been. Intellectual faculties are developed before the moral, hence the difficulty of obedience. Ile also mention.' that the Church put three things first which ought to besecond. First,neces- eity of particular belief ; second, religious sentiment ; third,Conversion makes a Christian. mis he went on to show was not ions conversion is only an entrance into Christ's school. Ile also spoke of ninny things which Christ commanded beet were not performed, but owing to lack of time he was unable to deal fully with them. —ln tbe afternoon a sacred cantata wee given by the choir, entitled " Christ and His soldiers," under the leadership of Mr. I.evi Richardson. Solos were sting by Miss Gladding, Miss Olive Wadding, Min Ilipkiss, Mire Mason, Mr. L. Richardson and Mr. P. Bailey. Several of the choruses by the choir were swell rendered, but the perfornianc, throughout would have been much more enjoyeble had the choir and one or two of the soloist, had a little more pennies. l'lns Wadding (piano) end Mr. A. Dumbell (organ) acted as accom- "lathe evening the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones preached to • crowded congregation on the necessity of the present generation studying the history of their forefathers nod learning lessens thereby. He said if he was naked what study yielded the largest nnd most beneficial results he would say the study of history. Nothing is s illuminating as the history of the experiences of the Lumen race. A man who audioe, history can command the word of all generations to assist him in the 'nutrition of all his problems and to shale Lisa how to live. The lesson of the text was that we onght to learn from the sins of other people. We have more to learn from the failure of other people than we have from their successes. Sin and temptation were the snore in all ages. The first sins of people are not intellectual, Lot they are einn which come from the passions. Sin appeals to the likes and dislikes before it touches the mind. The wont sins of modern society are too ugly to be spoken if, Ile mid he had no moral respect for people whose nate for things that are pleasant is stronger Han a hatred of what is morally wrong. The things which are lightly , spoken of to-day amongst men are considend vice in' woman. Ought this lobe so. Sixty years of personal experience chewed him that people usually fail where they least expect to do no. God is faithful and will cot allow us to be tempted more than we can bear if our weakness is linked to Dinne power, the strongest men being the one who realizes his one weakness. Solos were song at the evening sere'. by klisses Ilrpkiss and Wadding. Collect ions were taken at all the services in aid of the trust funds.</p>
        <p>"TIIE DESTRUCTION OF THE LIQUOR TRAFFIC.-</p>
        <p>On Nlondav evening the (lurch was crowded, the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones having been announced to deliver lectors entitled The destruction of the liquor cradle." Mr. Joseph Bailey presided. and along with him on the dais were the Rev. Thorns. Wadding, Mr. William Howlett end Mr. James Duckworth.—The Chairman described the drink question as the moat appalling one that the citizens of this couutry had to</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. Lloyd-Jones fully 'sustained his reputa- tion an a first-clams lectursr, making his subject intensely interesting by his eloquence and characteristic humour. He said the question of the liquor traffic was the greatest question to the people of this country ; for he did not know of any eTi I comparable to the drink, and he did not know of anything that would In of ruch hem fit to the British nation as the destruction of the liquor trade. But groat es it wax he was certsin it would be accomplished, not that the trade would be curted, modified or changed, hut that it would be</p>
        <p>I'ITEREV I,E,THOYID wad at no distant date down the corridor of centuties, for he believed it would occur in the life-tints of some of the children present. Then there would le no public house in that parish or in Warrington. (Applause.) He was mire it would happen in Wales very soon that was, if Englishmen would stay at home daze:ter); for the Yuly force in Wales that kept up undesirable institutions warr the force ol the Englishman. (Renewed laughter.) They were getting near, home rule in Wale., for recently what had never happened before bad taken place, two Nonconformists haviog taken e lending pert in the Investiture. (Applause ) ile only wished that ei•iliastion in Enema,' had made torch strides. (Lstighter and applause). Cuntinoing be, said that when he wag suggesting the destruction of the liquor trellic he did not admit that he was theotia- ing or talking wide of tl.e mark or with his fret ofl the earth. Even now there were over 100 ntenthers of the Hoare of Lords why would not haves public house on their estate and there ware over 1000 parishes in Greet Britain which bed no publichouses—and the death rate there had not incrrased but decroased. Surely it Wan not too notch to say that such a condition of things would become puma]. (Applause.) If forty years •go there were ten hindrencer to the destruction , of the drink trade eight of them had now disappeared. All the argumentative defences—and hewer paying the trade a great compliment when he said it had been , argurnentatice—of the trade had dieappeared and the only two defences today were the egpetites of certain people who unfortunately liked alcohol and the pockets of those in the trade—and neither of thins was erg.. mentative. LaughteniAll the rational defences—if there had been any—had fine. In hi. boyhood the fighting egainst the trade was in the hands of earttest,enthusisa. tic uneducated people and as all great reform. bad begun at the bottom end gone to the top it fins no reflection upon the temperance most to say that it was alerted by comparatively ignorant but larne-hearted men. But • great change hod come +bout and he would &amp;Ern) that if ell Godly and earnest men said tint another word againat the liquor trade it would go—and those who would send it would be</p>
        <p>16N ri,TS AND P. 1, onft. (Applause). Ile did not say that all the doctors were on their side, bet when he was boy a teetotal doctor wan a white elephant. Nobody believed a man under- stood the family constitution unless he recommended alcoholic drinks. (Laughter). But to-day in England, on the continent and in America there were thousands of medical men who bad declared against the one of alcohol. Having given instances of how milk had mmerceded wino in hospitals, the lecturer said that the temperance advocate. had also captured politicians. Ho remembered the time when there was no difference between one party and the other party—he would not name them—but to-day can party was solid in favour of temperance. (Applaente). .They were the</p>
        <p>ADAARN ENEMIES or TICE I.NOOR TRADE sod further enlightenment no doubt would have the same effect upon another party. But it was a great capture to have got wholesale the Liberal party, though be should be hoping too much if they were all living up to their professions. ,Laughter.) They had also captured educationists and now in nearly all the schools in the country legions were given to the boy, and girls no the evils of alcohol and the advantages of sobriety,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TIIII 6ATIIEPING I:01 NI) THE CROS,</head>
        <p>TIIII 6ATIIEPING I:01 NI) THE CROS,</p>
        <p>A Al ry iretty scene was witnessed an Wednesday last, when the scholars of the FreeChorch sc'ho'co—la.o.eleltrated their annual Walking Day. The clerk of the weather seemed to realise the needs of the occasion, for the day watt evelything that could he desirt d The scholars assembled at their .rejective schools at too tielt ck, after which they met together at the Cross and jt Med in singing a hymn. Afterwards they formed in proctsaion and proceeded through the village,Eagle Brow, Brookfield, Elm Ttee-road, Cherry-laneand Booth's Hill, where a stop was made oppr ~ite the Inure of Miss Braeendele, a Pt Caine° Methodist worker, whole at preeent undergoing a tey severe Illness, and a hymn was sung. They then proceeded doom 11, olfe Hill, New-road, Eagle lirow. the village, to Hush Green, where another hymn was rung The children the nproceeded tack attain to the l'rt PC where the National Anthem was sung. They then separated and net to their vat itus schncis for Ira. After ten they indulged in evrts in Mr. Ilcughton's field, and there they enjoyed a moat lappy earning.</p>
        <p>CI!MIMI' LANK Bern, Client:L.—The 92nd anniver• miry was held on Sunday, when two norm ins were preached by Mr. Alevander of Birkenhead. Ti. wAs provided titter the afternoon service for those who had come a distance, and ti this • good number stayed. Go.ol oongregations were present and thoroughly appreciated the homely services. The collections were well up to those of previous years. • CONGREUATIONAL CIIIIIITI IN SFR elcf: TlAND.—There was an excellent gathering of young people on Monday evening at this meeting. The nddreas was given b• Mr Tweedie " Character " and was listened to with grsat interest. A solo wae rendered by kik. Annie Miller 0 Love's invitation," very creditably. Miss Fenny Sotton presided. The meeting concluded with several shirt players by the membeis. Boirlor C 1111.1.1, 111Glicii•LoNr.—The Sonday school anniversary services were held oo Semi.p in this church, when the Rev. Frederick Simusls, of Brighton Goose, Nfrinchester, preached two excellent sermons ti very good coogregatiorw, especially in the evening when the church was tilled. Solos were rendered by Miss M. 1.11 uod rat tof 1.5 aim, and Mr. (I. Gough, of WerrlnSton, for w Itch they Crete be congratulated. In the aftornosn en address was given to the children by Mr. &amp;annals, which was very interesting and greatly helptul. Several hymns were rendsred by the scholars in a most praiseworthy 111111313e1. The services during the iley were in every war a grest success. The collections were in aid of the Sunday school f</p>
        <p>M. Twist mid that hitherto the p irks on • SO oday had boon fre.piented merely by cwi trim sod a few adults, but last S indsy, in Sotton Pork, there were between 2.009 end 3,900 people enjoying the mimic. That elms was • good reason way it ehorild to continued.</p>
        <p>Mr. Glynn said that if the peopie who bad been kick. log op • bother about Sunday music, would Only go to the perks end.., for themselves. to thought they wdold Ire convinced it was a very good thing for the [public. TO Chairman amid that the objection was • matter of principle. The Mayor (Alderman Ileeeham) naked the chair-am Ire would as a favour visit the park, where the mob: was Ling given nest Sunday sad sue for himaell. The Chairman said he would. the committee decided to continue the bends to CI, cod of the season.</p>
        <p>Grapp3ahall. seessut sm. TANNIN° COMFANti.—The employees of the shove with their wirers and friends held their annual works trip on Saturday lost. Southport wee the place 'elected. ens with the pressiliag good weather an enjoyable drly was spent. They left Warrington at 9.32, and arrived in Soutbpsrt at II o'clock. it svas quite a rel letter day for •11, for no sooner had they arrival wisest shsy beheld Mr. ageism. Nolte in his aeroplane. They had • splendid view of Mr. White dying over the town, a sight which will not be readily forgotten. They dined at 1230, after which Mr. W, Walker (managing director, we heartily tbsekad for the kind consideration he took in the firm'. employees. —Mr. leach moved the vote of thanks, nod it was :seconded by Mr. Bate. lIDKPENDEar Mlrriranist Cf11.7,11.—0n Sunday last this Methodists of Grapnenhall had a very good dsy. It was their annuli choir sermons and services</p>
