      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Mr. Parker ard Ccmpulsory Service. The Case of the Burglar and the</head>
        <p>Mr. Parker ard Ccmpulsory Service. The Case of the Burglar and the</p>
        <p>Value of Life</p>
        <p>in Mr. T., nion's reply to nty article on " evtutulsory Military Service," I e pans me so many compliments, that were it possil le to Hush in print this column wculd he nosy. I would certainly have great dith nlty in livirg up to these, and in acknow- ledging I would &amp;tire to say that while diffeting (IN in Mr. Too nrce is an rssential point. I very much atlntire the zeal he manifest., and samifiets he make., in the cause if Compulsory National Service. • Mr. Townroe appears to ,apg at that I have borrorred from Callyle. and in no doing, re•ersed his teaching I was not arra•n on iii I read Mr. Townroe's letter that Carlyle Intd written on the nribject. Mr. Townroe reminds me that I t .oh the chair at ODE of the Nat tonal Service Leegue nreetinge. May I remind Mr. Townroe that inn ctur.enting to do this, I made it a conditi n that I ahould he free to explain nip positi. n. Mr. Townn e accept. d r•ds condition. and in speaking, while I expressed appt oval of the general affect of utilitay service on the phynique,and the need of meetings to discuss such ruleiliollB. I expiesely disassociated topsail from any approval of the com- pulsory pat t of the movement, Mr. 'I ownroe tries to fasten upon me inconsistency by predicting what I should do if a burglar rntered nip house to rifle my cafe. According toUr. Townroe I would sally forth with a poker. fly so doing I would probably provoke an attack front the butglar, who to nave himself, and avoid imprisonment, might not henitate, if aimed, to shoot. I void in my law, and repeat. that I do not think physical life is by any means the most valuable thing in the world, but no one has a tight to endanger it unless for some worthy object. For my part, I should certainly out provoke an attack from a man put in • desperate pus tics if my vale were full of sovereigns instead of pipers. Mr. Towtroe seem. to think I should show pluck in &amp;fending as he ruts it " Mrs. Parker and the home" with a poker. In my opinion I would he a fool if I provoked an attack. 11l followed such a foolhardy course Mr.. Parker's safety and futurewell- being would certainly be endangered. My life may be of minor value to myself, hut it is of very great value to my family, and the best defence nip home has is the lit eservation of the life of husband and father. I troat I should Lot he lacking in courage in risking thy life for a worthy object, say the preserve- tion of the life of another, but to risk it for a little or big amount of money, would lie a coward's part. It nevus as though we needed a revaluation if our values.</p>
        <p>Now I have no objection to Mr. Townrce putting himself in a position with a burglar in which he may have to take life or put bin men in imn3Ment danger. but I do object to Mr. Townroe, or, as in the caws in dispute, the State compelling me to put myself in a like positir n. There M a certain similarity an well as dissimilarity between the conditions suggested by the burglar illustration and those in the matter in nue. lion. Mr. Townroe veld. of "defending Mrs. Parker and tie home." M.. Parker aid the home are not in danger union, Mr. Parker arts a foolish part. In like manner " The Englishman's Home " is not in danger in race of inva4m. 'As case of the burning of the fa rtnnteadn in South Africa may be raised againat this contention. But it 'Mould be remembered that these were turne I into forts for the fighting force; that in, the poker was brought out and used. Mr Townroe suggests that I depend too much in senti- mental platitude.. It seems to me that the case if the National Service League in largely made up of senti- ment and not reality. Private !lonian, lives, aid properly, would not be the object of attack in an invasion by any civilised force or community. I tru t I may claim to be ready to respond to the obligations resting upon every citizen to render national nervioe. What I contend in that everyone should be free to choore that form of ,ervice which he can 'rest render, and which in his opinion the nation most needs. And, further, that thin service should be voluntary late of real value to the individual and the nation The compelling force Omuta not he the fear of fines and in:lntl/unwept as net forth in the Bill fif the N d.L. but the compelling forcis of patriotism, love of kindred, the obligations 3pringing out of the unity of the nation, the bonds of humanity, and the universal redemption of the race an oymbolised by a cross " outside a city wall." Mr. Toranroe in well aware that there are foes in our midst which call for rational service to suppress and col quer, foes far more real, far more devastating, than the action if sty foreign foe could rossibly be, consumption, intemperance, cancee.'rlotli, destitutiwt which are continually warring againat the well-being of the commonwealth. An invasion from the foes Mr. Townroe has in mind is not likely to materialize, although be is right in considering and, if need be. preparing for the possibility. This country hiss not been seriously invaded for nearly a thousand years, in my opinion it sill not be invaded during the next thormand years But the foes referred to are in our midst .d are claiming their •ictinas fro m all claesen ; they ore constantly at war. They invade the '' man's How " in a manner more serious than that of any solitary for., they do not renmet any regulations about non-combatantn. The need of national service to combat the.. is infinitely greater than in cone eetion with the very remote possibiliry of a military invasion, remote recall-e the objects of such an invasion are in my opinir n at least —no longer operative. We presume Mr. Tr.wnroa would not make service againm these cornpulnory, nor would I. I @Mare the thought , I of Wi ham Watson in the lines :</p>
        <p>Your savage deserts howling near. Ycor wastes of ignorance, nice, and shame. Is there no room for victories here, No field for deed, cf fame ? Arise and corquer while reran The foe that in your midst residrs, And build within the mind of Man The Eropre that abides." I Lave seen a proposal for tLe formation or a National Compulsory Work League in which every- ,,fle should be compelled to do his ocher part of the work of the nation, so that the burden of toil might be more fairly distributed. The idea might be some. what difficult to Carry out, but it is fur more sensible, more priori*, less retitle:lentil, than the obligatory proposals of the National Service Leigne. CHARLES E. PARK ER. Penketh, July 19t1i, 1011.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>England's Decline.</head>
        <p>England's Decline.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Many men have prophesied he end of the world, but have been disappointed no often that they have turned their attention to things probable, and nob r prgoosticate the decline and fall of England, to to be brought about by our " implacable foe " the Getmto Emperor.</p>
        <p>Reports oneTiathian battleships with unprscedentrd death dealing machines, flying ships with wings carrying missiles loaded with carnate devils from " Tartarca '' to drop down our chiruceys,hidden mites lof dynamite only requiring the touch of German waiters in London to dash ns to destiuction ; thou- sand• cf things mythical and real to blow us to Afars and destroy this Mewed isle ; all hare their place in the fairy tales writtrn hy the screaming scareco”ngers, who with the Tersei.us apt.' ite of the god "Chemin." ask for more battleships, with sailors and soldiers by I the million.</p>
        <p>It seems too, that cur Colcnies are in danger of being snatched out of our grasp by Gerniany,who.owing to her rapidly increasing population, must find some place for the overpowering surplue. Such are the fanciful imaginations of the part and present *liters. Can this constitute our danger? Has England re ached the highest poitt of its trajectory? On the above questions I shall calmly and dispassionately dwell legardless of Cifferent political parties with tla ir contradictory and salfish party aimm cf nee Trade and Tariff Reform. One ride states that our imprla are increasing whilst nor exports are not keeping pace with other nations. °lmre assert the ccutrary and b th punks in their over zealous mood tee no other method of rrform, are no path through the wood but that of their own [ blazing. We are told that wealth in abundance, .d in bullion an ever increasing arm not, is um and this is held to be a sure sign of our prosperity. Can England decline under such conditions? alas the politician. And yr t how strange ! Thousands of emigrants me leaving this deceptive " Utopia" : England's brave and tluring none, the best hlood of the "Motherland." driven by want and privation, Journeying to Canada, the land of plenty and of great promise. Our Colonies unceasingly call and not in vain, for honest labour to be honestly rewarded, each man to do his share and the parasite to perish. Before crying out about the declining Itirth-ratt., the politician should prove that England can keep what alp, already has, or for ever crane canning Ball the preaching of prccreation. Canada, Argentina, America, Russia, send us wheat ; we export machinery ; they are getting their land in good heart, and ours is allowed to remain neglected and undeveloped. Stated in the fewest and simplett woids, We give up ell our valuable minerals " truly a nation's finest Sleet," for whit we should produce ourrelves. The rrofit on menufactured art:olte is certainly greater than the pri fit on farming pi, duce, end the troninaster arid ti vile manufacturers through offering larger w,tges than the farmers have drawn the labour from the rural districte to the cities, thereby making farming lees profitable and ctillicult, and ineroasing ' the competition for the lees conga trial and unhealthy occ,lti.attons in our towns... .</p>
        <p>The workingman eaves little out of his earnings, inversely masters have become enormously rich and lavish in their living. pleasures, and surerfluoue accumulations they tint it impossible to exist without innumerable at tei dents and servants ut their call. Now it is no false assertion to state that if a man build. a waneion, keepe servants and attendants, someone must pr duce fen d, clo'hing and fuel to keep them. The mansion of course had to be built, and broad labour for English workmen, lot all labour is wasted when utility is lost sight of. If a painter is engaged to paint a house, which owing to its lavish design lie spends a month in painting what, if utility had been desired, multi have been done in a week, then I consider the labour misdirected, and that it would have been better spent in painting a cottage, wiping out a alum, or producing food and clothing. The rich spend thcir money in prsiliming things, secondary to the wants of the great majority. If they spent it in cultivating the now barren moors and shooting estates and made it possible to grow cur own wheat at a profit, then tsr imports must decline and our exports need trouble as less. tin pint terge: re readily will ie.r.coel rdeeacdliarethal theyttl poie.ric.d herle rich their surplus gold in keeping innumerable very:min and sat ndanta, to the purchase of jewellery, fine clot) cog. diamonds even pay 1:100,(alti' for a single picture that was painted in a week and think it cheap) and will af,erwarile cut down the wages of the workmen and restrict the improvement and extensions of their farms and troika, until, owing to misdirected espital and labour, cur imports must exceed nor exporta; and England's decline is assured without the intervention of Germany. All eronomiste are agreed that if our imports exceed our exports ultimate bank- ruptcy is inevitable. What England wants. ard most have, is lees individual wealth with its huge st.,,re of surplus money used in buying up the Labour Market to misdirect it into channels of non-production to the detriment of the nation and i!. workers. The greater the number of non-producers there are carrying on occurationa of idle rich, servants, tailors, soldiers, bishop., minister,, lawyers, agents, rlerlos, the more must the worker do., and at a diminishing price. The more most our imports go up, because these people con- sume import. and produce nothing. Thus Canada receives more and more of ;his nation's gold and other ...ets in exchange for the supplies necessary to our existence, and in truth what she gets she has honestly earned. Instead of England retaining the title of " The Mother Country," Canada will have wrested it froio her : the title about.' go to the provider of the bread uf life. emphatically state that more workers and less shirkers mean decline of imports, an increase of exports, more wages, happier condit'ons, a rise in the birth-rate, and national prosperity. The Golden Age sung by the Grecian I:oets is then attained. " Men lived like gods, witb minds devoid of care, Are, from toil and misery ; then was not Timid old sge, tint nee in fast and bands Equally strong the banquet they enjoys' From every ill remote. They died as if (Yerroute with Sinop, and all good things were theirs, Thu bounteous Eftrth did of herself bring forth Fruit mach and plenteous, and in .ruietrese Their works midst numerous Ideally,' they pursue.- Yours faithfully, 6EORGE SMITE!. The Limes, Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
