      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>TOWN HALL KEEPER S SUICIDE Tragic End of Mr. G. A. Powis.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL KEEPER S SUICIDE Tragic End of Mr. G. A. Powis.</p>
        <p>A painful seasation was caused in Warrington yesterday morning when it became known (bat the body of Mr. George Albert Powis, hall-keeper at the Warrington Town Hall, bad hera fom.d in the Bridgwater canal at Ilcugh'a Bridge, Walton. Mr. POIVin had been noising from his home at the Tr.wn Hall since Monday. Ile was last Fern ali;a3 by PC. Newey who met him near the bottom of Bold-street at four o clOck in the morning.As he did not return,great anxiety was felt by his relatives and friends and the tragic solution of the mystery has occasion. d general regret. Mr. Powin was roe of Warrington'a best- known inhabitants, having occupied the position of hall-keeper and Mayor's attendant since March, 'Is9.Y. Ilia work was highly appreciated by the various Mayors under whom he had served and he wan a familiar figure at civic functions to which his official dutiis frequently took Min. Ile had charge of the Pair Hall and the ca.ering for Mayoral banquets was invariahly placed in Lis hands. His untimely end , will give much sorrow ton large circle towns- ! peoyle and great aympathy is felt with the widow in her sudden bereavement.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST. Mr. T. Ridgway, coroner, held the implied et the Stag Inn, Walton, lest evening. Mr. A. T. Hellaway, deputy town clerk, represented the Corporation. Walter Sumner, Hawthorne Grove, .Stockton Heitb, clerk in the Town Clerk'. department, Warrington, nephew of the deceased, gave et idercs of identifleatiort. Deceased was fifty-etas tears of age end W. hell- keeper at the Town Hall. Witness lent hint alive between 11 end 11.3,1 en Saturday morning, when he was inn ve,y weak state of health. In had been &amp;biking heavily. Witness bad &amp; conversation It Rh him with regard to his oonduct of the lest few weeks sad deceased /rid be was afraid there was something brewing.. .</p>
        <p>--fhe'isZorLnee : Did he me.. Le wee going to got into trouble? _ .</p>
        <p>Witness I rather think he won •fmid of dismissal or something almost as serious, owing to his conduct. 1)11 he ever threaten to take sway his life ?—No, exc. pt that bet... maid now and again. owing, I mippoes, to the after-effect. of drink or compinoots having been made about his drinking, tbvt be wished he waa away from it all. Ile lal said he would like to go away quietly by himself, nab. was sick of one thing sod soother.</p>
        <p>How long has this been going on ? Is it a matter nf yearn? Ile hati never been quite a sober man, but he be. not drunk to excess op to lour or five seeks ago. ' It Lisa come on rather suddenly ?—I think so. In reply to further questions by the C.troner. witness raid he telieved deceased was in bed •Il day on Sunday. His wife was too ill to attend the inquest. Mr. flallaway : Any remarks you wade to hint I' n t his conduct, were made purely in your personal caw ity as • relative? II itness : That ia so. In his own intereets,l thought I ought to !peer to him. P.C. N.;.. of Warringt on, a ii.l he last saw deceased alive at 130 on 31onday morning. Ile was then in Bold-street, lacing eelsre from the direotiun of ?iar key- street towards Arpley Cannon,. Witness sant "Hello, yr u are out early this morning," but deceased only I paused and then went on without making any definite reply. Witness had often seen deceased out °say —in . fact he hod occasionally been oat shooting with him at (cur o'clock in the morning, lie had no emeriti to suspect that anything was wrong; or he could have followsd the deceased. There;.. nothing unusual in his appearance. The Coroner : Did he appear to he sober ? Wane. r I had n. t gut opportunity of noticing. lea. about forty Isola assay. PC. bailey. of Acton Orange, Nicko to removing the body from the Bridgewater Canal near Dough's Bridge. There were at sign: of foul slay. Dr. Smith ea'd he attended the deceased on Sundty morning. Ile was then suffering from the effects or drink, end witness or nJuded that at CAL time tie ass not in his right mind. , The Coroner : Ile had e•idently been think lug very I heavily. I Witness lie t. Id main himself. I Did he do anythlng which caused you to insagine he would take his life 9—No. I have attend...l hire for years in ••tacks of this sort, end he has been all right in • few das A 'llse last time I at'onded him sr. in kfay•</p>
