      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Beswlck Titchett.</p>
        <p>A wedding of conwiderable local interest was. solemnized at the Legh-street Primitive Afethcdist Chapel on Thursday when Mr. William Robert Bessick, son of Mr. William Bees ick, Manchester- road, wart married to Mies Eva Titehett, youngest daughter of the late Mr. J. Titchett, of Wilderspzol-• causeway. The officiating minister• were the Rev.. W. Cover and J. longe. The hymns " Ihe voice that breathed o'er Eden " and " 0 love divine and golden" were sung and Mr. Fred Atkinson, Wll9 presided at the organ, playa I the Welling Mach. The bride, who wire given away by h, r brother, Mr. 0. Track tt, was attired in a soft cream satin embroidered dress and cream crinoline hat trimmed with ottrich plume. She wore a gold brooch, set with tunproiee, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a. bouquet composed of white carnations. sweet ran and lilies of the villey. 31ie5 Avce Berwick (sister of the bridegroom) and Miss F. Jones r niece- of ti. bride) were the bridesmaids. The,,, looked very pretty in pale blue silk embroidered dresses with black ham and their ornaments were gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegnorn. Each carried a bouquet of yellow marguerite. and forget- me-noto. The duties of best man were carried out by Mr. W. T. Eccles. Later in the day the har,py couple left let Llanfairfecban, where the honeymoon is. being spent, the bride travelling in a navy blue costume trimmed with black braid, end hat to match. A large number of beautiful and useful presents have been receivoi.</p>
      </div>
