        <p>• "The Declaration • • of London," I • is now a main topic of controversy; and rightly so, since the interests cf the British Empire, in the event of war, are deeply involved. It is of the greatest 0 importance that the people of this country should grasp the whole meaning and A effect of this international agreement: for when the people arrive at a conclusion • upon a vital subiect their will is not to be gainsaid. In times cf peace, and 0 in matters that affect their well-being, the people have again and again de- A clared their unmistakable verdict. Nothing, for instance, is of more supreme con- r sequence than health, and in this particular the public have long ago decided that BEECHAM'S PILLS stand unrivalled among the incurs For securing and maintaining the bcdCv vigour of the community. This efficacious niedicine, which is now held universal esteem, promotes a sound digestion, regulates the liver, bowels, and A kidneys, and protects the very fountains and foundations of health from disord.., • and disease. This is not a "Declaration of London," alone. It is the opinion :I 0 the world at large- of Great Britain of the Dominions overseas of America , of Continental countries,and of the Far East. Wherever, Indeed, Beechanes Pills have penetrated they have established themselves in the confidence vi nations 0 as possessing a value unequalled for general usefulness and fully iustifying A their reputation of being Worth a Guinea a Box. ia •</p>
        <p>were held in the morning at II and In the evening at 6 30, and turn conducted by Mr. It. 11. Wood, who , preached two excellent germ its. In the afternoon an ' open-air old-fashioned service of praise was held. Sacred selections wens very able rendered by the Stockton Heath Choral S misty. Mr. 11. E. longs c. ducted the choir whilst rimy rendered" We woo't brio, does,"" A wet, " Here by Flahylon's wave," and the " Hallelujah Chorus." Save for the weather , being somewhat boisterous, t be service was altogether very erj,-iyable. —6-- , ST. HELENS. AN ELECTION LIBEL. MR. TIIO3IAS GLOVER'S ACTION AGAINST NEWSPAPER : APOLOGY AND WITHDRAWAL., Mr. Thomas Glove,, ex-Ml'. for St. Ilelesx, was the plaintili in an action rcr libel at Manchester Amlxes en Tuesday. 'I he defendants were the proprietors of the "St. Helens and Prescot Reporter. 'the article corn' lilained of was published on the eye of the poll at the net General Election. Mr. M'Call id that the defendants' peper,which bad strongly advocated the Unionist cause, came out with. special edition on a day astutely chosen—the dry before the poll,. December 6th. The whole clone aidearid counsel, was tali. up aRI • spiteful personal attack I on the plaintiff, intended to ruin his chances in the I election. Defendants succeeded only too well In urrging out that purpose. Mr. lansilon, on Fehalf of defendant., apologised for , the article, which, he said, was written at • time of intense political excitement. They had no idea the article would be taken ss a personal attack on Mr. Glover. The case ins then withdrawn on terms on counsel's briefs, defendant's laying all the costs. SUNDAY MUSIC IN ST HELENS PARK'S. SHOULD IT BE CONTINUED? At tha St. Helena Parks Committee onlWerlneeday the question of Sunday music in am parks was again discussed. Tbe Chairman (Mr. Peet) said the public and the committee bed had experience of the effect of the bonds playing in the prrks, and he thought they ought to decide whether or no: the music should be continued.</p>
